1003	sqlserver	verify	Diagnose what the primary health check reports versus actual state	{"sql": "-- Check current failure condition level\\nSELECT ag.name, ag.failure_condition_level, ag.health_check_timeout,\\n  ags.primary_replica, ags.synchronization_health_desc\\nFROM sys.availability_groups ag\\nJOIN sys.dm_hadr_availability_group_states ags ON ag.group_id = ags.group_id;\\n\\n-- Check SQL Server component health (what sp_server_diagnostics reports)\\nSELECT * FROM sys.dm_os_ring_buffers\\nWHERE ring_buffer_type = N'RING_BUFFER_SCHEDULER_MONITOR'\\nORDER BY timestamp DESC;\\n\\n-- Check for blocking or scheduler issues\\nSELECT blocking_session_id, session_id, wait_type, wait_time, status\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_requests\\nWHERE blocking_session_id > 0\\nORDER BY wait_time DESC;\\n\\n-- Check user connections and batch activity\\nSELECT\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters WHERE counter_name = 'User Connections') AS user_connections,\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters WHERE counter_name = 'Batch Requests/sec') AS batch_requests_sec;", "parameters": [], "description": "Assess what the failure looks like to sp_server_diagnostics versus what the application reports. Identify whether the issue is scheduler starvation, I/O hangs, or application-level errors not reaching SQL Server's health check threshold."}	null	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.204708
1004	sqlserver	execute	Lower failure condition level to detect application-level failures	{"sql": "-- Lower failure condition level to catch more application-level failures\\n-- Current level 1 or 2 only detects server-down or service-unresponsive\\n-- Level 3 adds critical error detection (scheduler, I/O, memory, latch)\\n-- Level 4 adds moderate error detection\\n-- Level 5 catches any qualifying failure condition\\n\\n-- Recommended change: Level 2 → Level 3\\nALTER AVAILABILITY GROUP [AgName]\\n  SET (FAILURE_CONDITION_LEVEL = 3);\\n\\n-- For environments with persistent application-level hangs detected:\\nALTER AVAILABILITY GROUP [AgName]\\n  SET (FAILURE_CONDITION_LEVEL = 4);\\n\\n-- Verify:\\nSELECT name, failure_condition_level, health_check_timeout\\nFROM sys.availability_groups\\nWHERE name = N'AgName';", "parameters": ["AgName: availability group name", "FAILURE_CONDITION_LEVEL: 3 for critical errors (recommended starting point), 4 for moderate errors"], "description": "Lower the failure condition level from 1 or 2 to 3 or 4 to enable automatic failover when SQL Server reports critical component failures (corrupted memory, I/O subsystem failures, scheduler hangs) even though the service process is alive."}	{"sql": "-- Revert if false-positive failovers occur after sensitivity increase\\nALTER AVAILABILITY GROUP [AgName]\\n  SET (FAILURE_CONDITION_LEVEL = 2);"}	medium	t	t	00:01:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.204708
1005	sqlserver	instruct	Implement application-level health monitoring as complementary control	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "Since sp_server_diagnostics only monitors SQL Server-level components, implement complementary monitoring for application-level failures that SQL Server cannot detect:\\n\\n1. Configure SQL Server Agent alerts for Severity 17-25 errors:\\n   EXEC msdb.dbo.sp_add_alert @name = 'Severity 17+ errors', @severity = 17, @notification_message = 'Critical SQL Server error'\\n\\n2. Set up Windows Performance Monitor alerts for:\\n   - SQL Server: Batch Requests/sec dropping to near 0 while connections > threshold\\n   - SQL Server: Blocked processes > threshold\\n\\n3. Consider deploying a lightweight application-level health endpoint that tests actual query execution and triggers manual failover if genuine application failure is detected:\\n   EXEC sp_server_diagnostics  -- returns component health state\\n   (Returns: system, resource, query_processing, io_subsystem, events components)"}	null	low	f	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.204709
