1088	sqlserver	instruct	Reconfigure health check to use TCP probe on SQL Server listener port	{"sql": null, "parameters": ["listener_port: AG listener TCP port (typically 1433)", "probe_interval: Recommended 10-30 seconds", "probe_timeout: Set higher than SQL Server connect timeout (minimum 15s)"], "description": "Reconfigure the load balancer or monitoring system to use a TCP-level health check targeting the SQL Server AG listener port:\\n\\n1. For Azure Internal Load Balancer: Change probe type from HTTP to TCP, set port to the AG listener port (typically 1433). Do NOT use HTTP probe unless a dedicated HTTP health endpoint agent is deployed.\\n\\n2. For HAProxy: Use 'option tcp-check' with 'connect port 1433' — optionally follow with a SQL Server handshake check.\\n\\n3. For F5 / hardware LB: Use TCP half-open or full-open monitor targeting listener port 1433.\\n\\n4. If application-level (Layer 7) health validation is required: Deploy a health check agent (e.g., a PowerShell-based HTTP listener that runs 'SELECT 1' against the AG) and configure the HTTP probe to target that agent's port instead of the SQL listener port directly.\\n\\nNote: TCP probe passing means the port is open and accepting connections, but does NOT verify SQL authentication or database availability. For deeper health checking, use a SQL login probe with a lightweight query."}	null	medium	t	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.204762
