2325	sqlserver	instruct	Apply the CU to all mismatched components on each affected node	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"ssms_download": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/ssms/download-sql-server-management-studio-ssms", "engine_installer_flags": "/quiet /action=patch /IAcceptSQLServerLicenseTerms"}, "description": "SQL Server CU installers patch all installed components automatically when run with /action=patch. However, separately installed components (SSMS, SSDT, standalone SSIS) may require separate updates:\\n\\n1. Run the main CU installer, which patches engine + Agent + Full-Text + Replication:\\n   SQLServer<version>-KB<number>-x64.exe /quiet /action=patch /instancename=MSSQLSERVER /IAcceptSQLServerLicenseTerms\\n\\n2. For SQL Server Management Studio (if installed locally):\\n   Download and install the matching SSMS version from:\\n   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/ssms/release-notes-ssms\\n\\n3. For standalone SSIS instances:\\n   Apply the SSIS-specific CU package if available for the version.\\n\\n4. After patching, refresh the Windows Installer database:\\n   Run 'msiexec /fvoums {product-code}' if any component shows inconsistent state in the registry.\\n\\n5. Reboot the node if required by the installer (required flag will appear in the setup log)."}	{"description": "If the installer fails, check the Setup Bootstrap log. Common failure causes: pending reboot from a prior update (resolve via 'PendingFileRenameOperations' registry check), or another installer process is running. If component patching causes engine instability, uninstall the CU and restore from backup."}	medium	t	t	01:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389681
2326	sqlserver	verify	Confirm all components are at the target build and AG is healthy	{"sql": "-- Confirm engine build matches the target CU\\nSELECT SERVERPROPERTY('ProductVersion')   AS engine_version,\\n       SERVERPROPERTY('ProductUpdateLevel') AS cu_level,\\n       SERVERPROPERTY('ProductLevel')       AS product_level;\\n\\n-- Confirm AG is healthy and all replicas connected\\nSELECT ar.replica_server_name,\\n       ars.role_desc,\\n       ars.connected_state_desc,\\n       ars.synchronization_health_desc\\nFROM   sys.dm_hadr_availability_replica_states ars\\nJOIN   sys.availability_replicas ar ON ars.replica_id = ar.replica_id;\\n\\n-- Verify no databases have compatibility levels that suggest stale metadata\\nSELECT name, compatibility_level\\nFROM   sys.databases\\nWHERE  database_id > 4\\n  AND  state = 0\\n  AND  compatibility_level < CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('ProductMajorVersion') AS INT) * 10 - 10;", "parameters": {}, "description": "Confirm engine version matches the target CU on all nodes. Check AG replica connectivity and health. Identify databases still at lower compatibility levels that may need an ALTER DATABASE ... SET COMPATIBILITY_LEVEL update during the next maintenance window (do not change compatibility levels without application testing)."}	null	low	f	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389682
4581	sqlserver	verify	Confirm updated queue settings	{"sql": "SELECT sa.name, sa.queue_delay, sa.on_failure_desc FROM sys.server_audits sa WHERE sa.is_state_enabled = 1;", "parameters": [], "description": "Verifies the updated queue and failure settings."}	null	low	f	t	00:01:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.751069
2327	sqlserver	verify	Validate current AG health and node version inventory before beginning patching	{"sql": "-- AG health baseline\\nSELECT ag.name AS ag_name,\\n       ags.synchronization_health_desc,\\n       ags.primary_replica,\\n       COUNT(ar.replica_id)                                    AS total_replicas,\\n       SUM(CASE WHEN ars.connected_state_desc = 'CONNECTED' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS connected_replicas\\nFROM   sys.availability_groups ag\\nJOIN   sys.dm_hadr_availability_group_states ags ON ag.group_id = ags.group_id\\nJOIN   sys.availability_replicas ar ON ag.group_id = ar.group_id\\nLEFT   JOIN sys.dm_hadr_availability_replica_states ars ON ar.replica_id = ars.replica_id\\nGROUP  BY ag.name, ags.synchronization_health_desc, ags.primary_replica;\\n\\n-- Check for any pre-existing suspended databases\\nSELECT ar.replica_server_name,\\n       DB_NAME(drs.database_id) AS database_name,\\n       drs.synchronization_state_desc,\\n       drs.is_suspended\\nFROM   sys.dm_hadr_database_replica_states drs\\nJOIN   sys.availability_replicas ar ON drs.replica_id = ar.replica_id\\nWHERE  drs.is_suspended = 1\\n    OR drs.synchronization_health_desc != 'HEALTHY';", "parameters": {}, "description": "Do NOT begin patching if the AG shows synchronization_health_desc != 'HEALTHY' or any databases are suspended. Resolve pre-existing AG health issues first, then return to this resolution path. Record the initial version matrix for all replicas."}	null	low	f	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389683
2329	sqlserver	verify	Confirm all replicas at target build, document the process, and set up version monitoring	{"sql": "-- Post-patch: confirm all replicas at same build\\nSELECT ar.replica_server_name,\\n       ars.role_desc,\\n       ars.connected_state_desc,\\n       ars.synchronization_health_desc\\nFROM   sys.dm_hadr_availability_replica_states ars\\nJOIN   sys.availability_replicas ar ON ars.replica_id = ar.replica_id;\\n\\n-- Set up an Agent alert for future AG health degradation\\n-- (if not already configured)\\nUSE msdb;\\nEXEC sp_add_alert\\n    @name = N'AG Synchronization Health Warning',\\n    @message_id = 35264,\\n    @severity = 0,\\n    @enabled = 1,\\n    @delay_between_responses = 60,\\n    @notification_message = N'AG synchronization health has degraded. Check replica versions and data movement.';\\n\\n-- Verify the alert was created\\nSELECT name, enabled, message_id\\nFROM   msdb.dbo.sysalerts\\nWHERE  name = N'AG Synchronization Health Warning';", "parameters": {}, "description": "Confirm all replicas are CONNECTED with matching versions and the AG group is HEALTHY. Create (or verify) a SQL Server Agent alert for AG health events so version mismatches from future uncoordinated patches are detected quickly. Document the patching procedure as a standard operating procedure (SOP) in the team wiki."}	null	low	f	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389684
2330	sqlserver	verify	Confirm version lag and AG synchronization state on the missed node	{"sql": "-- Determine how many CU levels behind this node is\\nSELECT SERVERPROPERTY('ServerName')        AS server_name,\\n       SERVERPROPERTY('ProductVersion')     AS current_version,\\n       SERVERPROPERTY('ProductUpdateLevel') AS current_cu,\\n       SERVERPROPERTY('ProductLevel')       AS product_level,\\n       sqlserver_start_time,\\n       DATEDIFF(day, sqlserver_start_time, GETDATE()) AS days_since_restart\\nFROM   sys.dm_os_sys_info;\\n\\n-- Confirm the AG replica is still connected and synchronized\\nSELECT ar.replica_server_name,\\n       ars.role_desc,\\n       ars.connected_state_desc,\\n       ars.synchronization_health_desc,\\n       drs.synchronization_state_desc,\\n       drs.redo_queue_size,\\n       drs.log_send_queue_size\\nFROM   sys.dm_hadr_availability_replica_states ars\\nJOIN   sys.availability_replicas ar ON ars.replica_id = ar.replica_id\\nLEFT   JOIN sys.dm_hadr_database_replica_states drs ON ars.replica_id = drs.replica_id\\nWHERE  ar.replica_server_name = SERVERPROPERTY('ServerName');", "parameters": {}, "description": "Confirm the version gap between this node and the target build. Verify the replica is still participating in the AG. If the node is currently the primary, plan a controlled failover to a secondary before patching. Note the redo_queue_size — a large value means re-synchronization will take longer after the patch restart."}	null	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389685
31437	mariadb	configure	mysql_set_binlog_retention	{"sql": "SET GLOBAL binlog_expire_logs_seconds = 259200; -- 3 days", "parameters": ["expire_seconds"], "description": "Configure automatic binary log expiration to prevent future disk exhaustion"}	\N	medium	t	t	seconds	2026-05-11 22:17:39.229791
2332	sqlserver	verify	Confirm catch-up patch success and establish missed-window alert	{"sql": "-- Confirm this node is now at the target build\\nSELECT SERVERPROPERTY('ProductVersion')   AS new_version,\\n       SERVERPROPERTY('ProductUpdateLevel') AS new_cu;\\n\\n-- Confirm AG is fully healthy\\nSELECT ag.name,\\n       ags.synchronization_health_desc,\\n       ags.primary_replica\\nFROM   sys.dm_hadr_availability_group_states ags\\nJOIN   sys.availability_groups ag ON ags.group_id = ag.group_id;\\n\\n-- Create an Agent job to alert if replicas diverge in version in future\\n-- (pseudo-code — implement as a monitoring job step)\\n-- The job queries each replica's ProductVersion via linked server or CMS\\n-- and sends an alert if any replica is more than 1 CU behind the primary\\n\\n-- Confirm no databases are suspended on this replica\\nSELECT DB_NAME(drs.database_id) AS database_name,\\n       drs.synchronization_state_desc,\\n       drs.is_suspended\\nFROM   sys.dm_hadr_database_replica_states drs\\nWHERE  drs.is_local = 1\\n  AND  (drs.is_suspended = 1 OR drs.synchronization_state_desc NOT IN ('SYNCHRONIZED', 'SYNCHRONIZING'));", "parameters": {}, "description": "Verify the new build matches the target CU and the AG group is HEALTHY. If any databases are suspended, resume them with ALTER DATABASE <db> SET HADR RESUME on the primary. Update the maintenance schedule to include an explicit catch-up window process: if a node misses the primary window, it must be patched within 5 business days. Create a SQL Server Agent job or monitoring script that checks all AG replica versions weekly and alerts if any replica is more than one CU behind."}	null	low	f	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389686
2335	sqlserver	verify	Confirm sufficient free space exists before retrying patch	{"sql": "SELECT DISTINCT\\n  vs.volume_mount_point,\\n  vs.logical_volume_name,\\n  vs.total_bytes / 1073741824 AS total_gb,\\n  vs.available_bytes / 1073741824 AS available_gb,\\n  CAST(100.0 * vs.available_bytes / NULLIF(vs.total_bytes, 0) AS DECIMAL(5,1)) AS pct_free,\\n  CASE\\n    WHEN vs.available_bytes / 1073741824 < 2 THEN 'CRITICAL - do not proceed'\\n    WHEN vs.available_bytes / 1073741824 < 6 THEN 'WARNING - borderline'\\n    ELSE 'OK - proceed with patch'\\n  END AS space_status\\nFROM sys.master_files mf\\nCROSS APPLY sys.dm_os_volume_stats(mf.database_id, mf.file_id) vs\\nORDER BY vs.available_bytes ASC", "parameters": {"minimum_acceptable_free_gb": 6}, "description": "All volumes must show at minimum 6 GB available (2 GB absolute minimum for system drive, more for data volumes hosting large databases). Confirm no volume shows CRITICAL status before rerunning the installer."}	null	low	f	t	00:02:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389688
2692	sqlserver	execute	Enable forced parameterisation on affected databases	{"sql": "-- Enable forced parameterisation for the target database\\n-- Replace [YourDatabase] with the actual database name\\nALTER DATABASE [YourDatabase] SET PARAMETERIZATION FORCED;\\n\\n-- Verify\\nSELECT name, is_parameterization_forced\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE name = 'YourDatabase'", "parameters": {"database": "target application database", "PARAMETERIZATION": "FORCED"}, "description": "Forces SQL Server to parameterise most ad-hoc queries automatically, allowing plan reuse without requiring application code changes. Review exceptions (e.g., queries that benefit from literal-specific plans) before enabling."}	{"sql": "ALTER DATABASE [YourDatabase] SET PARAMETERIZATION SIMPLE;", "description": "Revert to SIMPLE parameterisation if query plan regressions are observed after enabling FORCED."}	medium	t	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389689
2334	sqlserver	execute	Shrink VLF-bloated transaction logs if safe to do so	{"sql": "-- Identify databases with transaction logs consuming excessive space\\nSELECT\\n  d.name AS db_name,\\n  d.log_reuse_wait_desc,\\n  mf.physical_name,\\n  mf.size * 8 / 1024 AS log_size_mb,\\n  FILEPROPERTY(mf.name, 'SpaceUsed') * 8 / 1024 AS log_used_mb,\\n  (mf.size - FILEPROPERTY(mf.name, 'SpaceUsed')) * 8 / 1024 AS log_free_mb\\nFROM sys.databases d\\nJOIN sys.master_files mf ON d.database_id = mf.database_id\\nWHERE mf.type = 1  -- log files\\n  AND d.state = 0\\n  AND (mf.size - FILEPROPERTY(mf.name, 'SpaceUsed')) * 8 / 1024 > 500\\nORDER BY log_free_mb DESC;\\n\\n-- For each FULL-recovery database safe to shrink (log_reuse_wait_desc = 'NOTHING'),\\n-- back up the log first, then shrink:\\n-- USE [target_db];\\n-- BACKUP LOG [target_db] TO DISK = 'NUL';\\n-- DBCC SHRINKFILE (N'target_db_log', 512);  -- shrink to 512 MB", "parameters": {"minimum_log_free_mb_to_consider": 500, "target_log_size_after_shrink_mb": 512}, "description": "Identify transaction log files with large free space. Only shrink logs on databases where log_reuse_wait_desc = 'NOTHING' and a log backup has just been taken. Do not shrink logs on databases with active replication, AG log shipping, or log_reuse_wait_desc = 'LOG_BACKUP' without first taking a log backup."}	{"description": "-- Log files grow automatically; no rollback is possible for shrink.\\n-- Re-growth will occur naturally as transactions proceed.\\n-- If shrinking caused VLF fragmentation, rebuild with ALTER DATABASE [db] MODIFY FILE (NAME=logfile, SIZE=desired_size);"}	medium	t	f	00:20:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389688
2337	sqlserver	verify	Confirm available memory is sufficient and retry patch	{"sql": "-- Wait 2-3 minutes after reducing max server memory, then verify:\\nSELECT\\n  total_physical_memory_kb / 1024 AS total_physical_mb,\\n  available_physical_memory_kb / 1024 AS available_physical_mb,\\n  total_page_file_kb / 1024 AS total_page_file_mb,\\n  available_page_file_kb / 1024 AS available_page_file_mb,\\n  system_memory_state_desc,\\n  CASE\\n    WHEN available_physical_memory_kb / 1024 >= 4096 THEN 'OK - sufficient memory for installer'\\n    WHEN available_physical_memory_kb / 1024 >= 2048 THEN 'WARNING - borderline, monitor closely'\\n    ELSE 'CRITICAL - do not proceed, free more memory'\\n  END AS memory_status\\nFROM sys.dm_os_sys_memory", "parameters": {"minimum_available_mb": 4096}, "description": "Confirm at least 4 GB of physical memory is available before launching the patch installer. If the system shows available_physical_mb >= 4096 and system_memory_state_desc = 'Available physical memory is high', it is safe to proceed with the patch installation."}	null	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.38969
2338	sqlserver	execute	Restore original max server memory after successful patch	{"sql": "-- After patch installation completes successfully, restore original max server memory.\\n-- Replace ORIGINAL_VALUE_MB with the value recorded in step 1.\\nEXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1;\\nRECONFIGURE;\\nEXEC sp_configure 'max server memory (MB)', ORIGINAL_VALUE_MB;\\nRECONFIGURE WITH OVERRIDE;\\nEXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 0;\\nRECONFIGURE;\\n\\n-- Verify restoration\\nSELECT name, value_in_use AS restored_value_mb\\nFROM sys.configurations\\nWHERE name = 'max server memory (MB)'", "parameters": {"original_value_placeholder": "ORIGINAL_VALUE_MB"}, "description": "Restore max server memory to its original production value so SQL Server can reclaim the buffer pool for normal workload performance. This step is mandatory after patch installation completes."}	null	low	f	t	00:02:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389691
31438	mariadb	verify	mysql_verify_disk_space_recovered	{"sql": "SHOW BINARY LOGS; SELECT ROUND(SUM(FILE_SIZE) / 1024 / 1024 / 1024, 2) AS total_binlog_gb FROM performance_schema.binary_log_files;", "parameters": [], "description": "Verify disk space has been recovered after purging binary logs"}	\N	low	f	t	seconds	2026-05-11 22:17:39.229791
2336	sqlserver	execute	Record current max server memory and reduce it to free RAM	{"sql": "-- First, record current setting for rollback\\nSELECT name, value_in_use AS current_value_mb\\nFROM sys.configurations\\nWHERE name = 'max server memory (MB)';\\n\\n-- Check current system memory to determine safe reduction\\nSELECT\\n  total_physical_memory_kb / 1024 AS total_physical_mb,\\n  available_physical_memory_kb / 1024 AS available_physical_mb,\\n  physical_memory_in_use_kb / 1024 AS sql_in_use_mb\\nFROM sys.dm_os_sys_memory\\nCROSS JOIN sys.dm_os_process_memory;\\n\\n-- Reduce max server memory: set to (current_value - 4096) or a value that leaves\\n-- at least 4 GB free for OS + installer. Adjust the number below based on your system.\\nEXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1;\\nRECONFIGURE;\\nEXEC sp_configure 'max server memory (MB)', 4096;  -- example: set to 4 GB\\nRECONFIGURE WITH OVERRIDE;", "parameters": {"note": "Replace 4096 with (total_physical_mb - 6144) for a system with more RAM", "memory_to_free_mb": 4096}, "description": "Record the current max server memory value for restoration after patching. Then reduce it by at least 4096 MB (or to a value leaving 4+ GB free for the OS and installer). SQL Server will begin releasing memory pages within seconds to minutes."}	{"description": "-- Restore original max server memory value (replace ORIGINAL_VALUE_MB with the recorded value)\\nEXEC sp_configure 'max server memory (MB)', ORIGINAL_VALUE_MB;\\nRECONFIGURE WITH OVERRIDE;\\nEXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 0;\\nRECONFIGURE;"}	medium	t	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.38969
2340	sqlserver	execute	Assess instance state and clean up any partial patch artifacts	{"sql": "-- Check current version state to determine if instance is in a partial-upgrade condition\\nSELECT\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductVersion') AS product_version,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductLevel') AS product_level,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductUpdateLevel') AS cu_level,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('IsSingleUser') AS is_single_user;\\n\\n-- Check if any databases are in suspect or recovery pending state\\nSELECT name, state_desc,\\n  DATABASEPROPERTYEX(name, 'Status') AS status,\\n  is_read_only\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE state_desc NOT IN ('ONLINE');\\n\\n-- Check for active single-user mode that setup may have left behind\\nSELECT name, is_single_user\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE is_single_user = 1 AND database_id > 4;", "parameters": {}, "description": "Determine whether the previous failed installation left the instance in a partial state. If any databases show non-ONLINE state or single_user mode was set by setup, remediate those before retrying. Also clean up any extracted setup files from %TEMP% or the SQL Server Setup Bootstrap folder."}	{"description": "-- If a database was inadvertently left in single-user mode by failed setup:\\n-- ALTER DATABASE [affected_db] SET MULTI_USER WITH ROLLBACK IMMEDIATE;"}	low	f	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389692
2694	sqlserver	instruct	Refactor ORM queries to use eager loading or batched fetching	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"ef_core_include": ".Include(x => x.RelatedEntities)", "context_lifetime": "Scoped (per HTTP request)", "nhibernate_batch_size": 25}, "description": "For Entity Framework Core: replace lazy-loaded navigation properties with .Include() or .ThenInclude() for known relationships. Use .AsSplitQuery() for large collection includes. For NHibernate: configure batch-size on collections (batch-size=\\"25\\") in mappings or use FETCH JOIN in HQL. For Dapper: use multi-mapping or QueryMultiple to load parent and child rows in a single round-trip. Ensure DbContext/ISession lifetime is scoped to the HTTP request, not transient, so the same connection is reused across all ORM calls in a single request."}	{"description": "Revert to previous ORM query code if data correctness issues arise after refactoring."}	medium	t	t	08:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389709
9289	postgresql	verify	Verify queries still use PK index for lookups	{"sql": "EXPLAIN (COSTS OFF) SELECT * FROM :table_name WHERE :column_name = :sample_value;", "parameters": ["table_name", "column_name", "sample_value"], "description": "Confirms the PK index is used for lookups on the column after the unique index is dropped."}	\N	low	f	t	seconds	2026-02-25 08:59:43.77256
2348	sqlserver	execute	Determine exact current version and identify the correct patch chain	{"sql": "-- Capture complete version fingerprint\\nSELECT\\n  @@VERSION AS full_version_string,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductVersion') AS build_number,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductLevel') AS rtm_or_sp,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductUpdateLevel') AS cu_level,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductMajorVersion') AS major,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('Edition') AS edition;\\n\\n-- Check all database compatibility levels for potential upgrade path issues\\nSELECT name, compatibility_level,\\n  state_desc, recovery_model_desc\\nFROM sys.databases\\nORDER BY database_id;\\n\\n-- Review error log for any version conflict messages from failed patch\\nCREATE TABLE #errlog (LogDate DATETIME, ProcessInfo VARCHAR(64), Text NVARCHAR(MAX));\\nINSERT INTO #errlog EXEC xp_readerrorlog 0, 1, N'Setup';\\nSELECT LogDate, Text FROM #errlog\\nWHERE Text LIKE '%cannot be applied%'\\n   OR Text LIKE '%requires%'\\n   OR Text LIKE '%prerequisite%'\\nORDER BY LogDate DESC;\\nDROP TABLE #errlog;", "parameters": {}, "description": "Capture the exact build number (4-part version like 16.0.4165.4) and map it against the Microsoft SQL Server build versions page. This identifies where in the CU chain the instance currently sits and what the correct next patch should be. Never skip SP levels when applying CUs: SQL Server 2019 CUs only apply to RTM, not to older CU or SP branches."}	null	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389696
2693	sqlserver	verify	Confirm compilation rate and plan cache health after changes	{"sql": "-- Compilation rate check\\nSELECT\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE counter_name = 'SQL Compilations/sec'\\n     AND object_name LIKE '%SQL Statistics%') AS compilations_sec,\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE counter_name = 'Batch Requests/sec'\\n     AND object_name LIKE '%SQL Statistics%') AS batch_requests_sec;\\n\\n-- Plan cache composition check\\nSELECT objtype, COUNT(*) AS plan_count,\\n  SUM(usecounts) AS total_uses,\\n  AVG(usecounts) AS avg_uses\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_cached_plans\\nGROUP BY objtype\\nORDER BY plan_count DESC;", "parameters": {}, "description": "Verify that compilations_sec / batch_requests_sec ratio has dropped below 10%. Prepared and Proc plan types should dominate the cache over Adhoc single-use entries."}	null	low	f	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389697
2350	sqlserver	verify	Confirm version consistency across instance and all databases post-patch	{"sql": "-- Verify final version matches the intended target\\nSELECT\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductVersion') AS final_version,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductUpdateLevel') AS final_cu,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductLevel') AS final_sp;\\n\\n-- Check all databases are ONLINE and at expected internal version\\nSELECT name, state_desc,\\n  compatibility_level,\\n  DATABASEPROPERTYEX(name, 'Version') AS internal_version\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE state_desc != 'ONLINE'\\n   OR DATABASEPROPERTYEX(name, 'Status') != 'NORMAL';\\n\\n-- Verify no databases are stuck in upgrade pending state\\nSELECT name, state_desc\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE state_desc IN ('RESTORING', 'RECOVERING', 'RECOVERY_PENDING', 'SUSPECT', 'EMERGENCY');", "parameters": {}, "description": "Confirm the instance reports the expected target build number, all databases are ONLINE, and no databases show non-ONLINE state that might indicate an incomplete catalog upgrade. If any database is in RECOVERY_PENDING or SUSPECT after patching, that database's catalog may need repair."}	null	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389698
2354	sqlserver	execute	Run DBCC CHECKDB on system databases to assess corruption extent	{"sql": "-- Check master database integrity\\nDBCC CHECKDB (master) WITH NO_INFOMSGS, ALL_ERRORMSGS, DATA_PURITY;\\n\\n-- Check model database\\nDBCC CHECKDB (model) WITH NO_INFOMSGS, ALL_ERRORMSGS, DATA_PURITY;\\n\\n-- Check msdb database\\nDBCC CHECKDB (msdb) WITH NO_INFOMSGS, ALL_ERRORMSGS, DATA_PURITY;\\n\\n-- Check tempdb (optional, recreated on restart)\\nDBCC CHECKDB (tempdb) WITH NO_INFOMSGS, ALL_ERRORMSGS;\\n\\n-- Also check suspect_pages for recent I/O errors on system databases\\nSELECT db.name, sp.file_id, sp.page_id, sp.event_type, sp.error_count, sp.last_update_date\\nFROM msdb.dbo.suspect_pages sp\\nJOIN sys.databases db ON sp.database_id = db.database_id\\nWHERE db.database_id IN (1, 2, 3, 4)\\nORDER BY sp.last_update_date DESC;", "parameters": {}, "description": "Run integrity checks on all system databases to determine the scope of corruption. CHECKDB output will categorize errors as 'repair_allow_data_loss', 'repair_rebuild', or 'not repairable'. Record all error messages before proceeding to the appropriate repair strategy."}	null	low	f	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389701
2360	sqlserver	execute	Assess database integrity and catalog the damage from hardware failure	{"sql": "-- Check current state of all databases\\nSELECT name, database_id, state_desc,\\n  is_read_only, is_in_standby, is_cleanly_shutdown,\\n  DATABASEPROPERTYEX(name, 'Status') AS db_status\\nFROM sys.databases\\nORDER BY database_id;\\n\\n-- Check suspect pages logged during or after the failure\\nSELECT db.name AS database_name,\\n  sp.file_id, sp.page_id,\\n  CASE sp.event_type\\n    WHEN 1 THEN '823 error (OS I/O)'\\n    WHEN 2 THEN 'Bad checksum'\\n    WHEN 3 THEN 'Torn page'\\n  END AS event_type,\\n  sp.error_count, sp.last_update_date\\nFROM msdb.dbo.suspect_pages sp\\nJOIN sys.databases db ON sp.database_id = db.database_id\\nWHERE sp.event_type IN (1, 2, 3)\\nORDER BY sp.last_update_date DESC;\\n\\n-- Check current version to understand if the patch partially applied\\nSELECT\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductVersion') AS product_version,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductUpdateLevel') AS cu_level;", "parameters": {}, "description": "Determine the extent of damage: which databases are impacted, whether the hardware failure caused data corruption, and whether the SQL Server instance is in a consistent pre-patch or post-patch state. Record all database states and version information before any remediation."}	null	low	f	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389701
4600	sqlserver	instruct	Add DDL and AUDIT_CHANGE groups to specification and ship logs to SIEM	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "Complete the protection: (1) Add SCHEMA_OBJECT_CHANGE_GROUP, DATABASE_OBJECT_CHANGE_GROUP, and AUDIT_CHANGE_GROUP to the server audit specification. (2) Configure real-time log shipping to a remote SIEM that sysadmins cannot access. (3) Set up SIEM alerts for AUDIT_CHANGE events. (4) Implement separation of duties: the DBA team should not have admin access to the SIEM or the audit file share."}	null	low	f	t	02:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.751077
2351	sqlserver	execute	Notify and terminate active connections gracefully	{"sql": "-- Set all user databases to restricted access to block new connections\\n-- (optional, use only if you cannot coordinate application shutdown)\\n-- ALTER DATABASE [application_db] SET RESTRICTED_USER WITH ROLLBACK AFTER 30 SECONDS;\\n\\n-- Kill long-running active sessions (> 5 minutes) that are blocking shutdown\\n-- Review first, then run the kill statements:\\nSELECT\\n  s.session_id,\\n  s.login_name,\\n  s.host_name,\\n  s.program_name,\\n  DATEDIFF(minute, r.start_time, GETDATE()) AS running_minutes,\\n  r.status,\\n  r.wait_type\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions s\\nINNER JOIN sys.dm_exec_requests r ON s.session_id = r.session_id\\nWHERE s.is_user_process = 1\\n  AND s.session_id != @@SPID\\n  AND DATEDIFF(minute, r.start_time, GETDATE()) > 5\\nORDER BY running_minutes DESC;\\n\\n-- After review, kill sessions (replace N with actual session_id values):\\n-- KILL N;", "parameters": {"max_allowed_running_minutes": 5}, "description": "First, coordinate with application teams to stop application connections. Then identify and kill any long-running queries or transactions that will not complete before the maintenance window. Use KILL SPID for each blocking session. For AG replicas, ensure log hardening is complete before killing sessions."}	{"description": "-- Restore multi-user access if RESTRICTED_USER was set:\\n-- ALTER DATABASE [application_db] SET MULTI_USER WITH ROLLBACK IMMEDIATE;"}	medium	t	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389699
2356	sqlserver	verify	Re-run post-patch upgrade scripts and confirm catalog is consistent	{"sql": "-- After repair, verify system catalog consistency\\nDBCC CHECKDB (master) WITH NO_INFOMSGS;\\nDBCC CHECKDB (model) WITH NO_INFOMSGS;\\nDBCC CHECKDB (msdb) WITH NO_INFOMSGS;\\n\\n-- Check all databases are in ONLINE state\\nSELECT name, state_desc, is_read_only\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE state_desc != 'ONLINE';\\n\\n-- Verify catalog version consistency\\nSELECT name,\\n  compatibility_level,\\n  DATABASEPROPERTYEX(name, 'Version') AS db_version\\nFROM sys.databases\\nORDER BY database_id;\\n\\n-- Verify post-patch catalog objects exist (example: check a known CU-added view)\\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS patch_objects_present\\nFROM sys.all_objects\\nWHERE modify_date > DATEADD(day, -1, GETDATE())\\n  AND type = 'V';", "parameters": {}, "description": "Confirm the catalog is clean after repair and that the post-patch upgrade scripts have completed. If the instance was restored to the pre-patch state, re-run the patch installation from a verified binary. If catalog objects added by the CU are visible, the upgrade scripts completed successfully."}	null	low	f	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389702
2359	sqlserver	verify	Confirm prerequisites are satisfied and retry SQL Server patch	{"sql": "-- After prerequisite installation and reboot, verify SQL Server is running at expected pre-patch version\\nSELECT\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductVersion') AS product_version,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductUpdateLevel') AS cu_level;\\n\\n-- Verify OS-level requirements from SQL Server's perspective\\nSELECT host_platform, host_distribution, host_release\\nFROM sys.dm_os_host_info;\\n\\n-- Check all databases are ONLINE before retrying patch\\nSELECT name, state_desc FROM sys.databases\\nWHERE state_desc != 'ONLINE';", "parameters": {}, "description": "Confirm SQL Server restarted successfully after the reboot, all databases are ONLINE, and the instance is at the expected pre-patch version. Then retry the SQL Server CU/SP installer — the prerequisite check should now pass. Monitor the setup log for any remaining failures."}	null	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389704
3706	sqlserver	execute	Update statistics on all tables with high modification counts	{"sql": "DECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(MAX) = '';\\nSELECT @sql = @sql + 'UPDATE STATISTICS ' +\\n  QUOTENAME(OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME(s.object_id)) + '.' +\\n  QUOTENAME(OBJECT_NAME(s.object_id)) + ' ' +\\n  QUOTENAME(s.name) + ' WITH FULLSCAN; '\\nFROM sys.stats s\\nCROSS APPLY sys.dm_db_stats_properties(s.object_id, s.stats_id) sp\\nWHERE sp.modification_counter > sp.rows * 0.10\\n  AND sp.rows > 1000;\\nEXEC sp_executesql @sql;", "parameters": [], "description": "Updates all statistics that have more than 10% of rows modified since last update, using full scan for maximum accuracy."}	null	low	f	f	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389705
31439	mariadb	diagnose	mysql_collect_crash_diagnostics	{"sql": "-- Check error log for assertion failure details:\\n-- grep -i 'assertion\\\\|signal\\\\|crash\\\\|core' /var/log/mysql/error.log | tail -50\\n-- Check if core dump exists:\\n-- ls -la /var/lib/mysql/core*\\nSELECT @@version, @@version_comment;", "parameters": [], "description": "Collect MySQL version, error log entries, and core dump location for crash analysis"}	\N	low	f	t	seconds	2026-05-11 22:17:39.229791
2362	sqlserver	verify	Validate instance integrity post-restoration and schedule patch retry on certified hardware	{"sql": "-- Verify all databases are online and intact after restoration\\nSELECT name, state_desc, recovery_model_desc,\\n  is_read_only, log_reuse_wait_desc\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE state_desc != 'ONLINE';\\n\\n-- Run CHECKDB on critical databases\\nDBCC CHECKDB (master) WITH NO_INFOMSGS, ALL_ERRORMSGS;\\n\\n-- Verify no suspect pages remain\\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS remaining_suspect_pages\\nFROM msdb.dbo.suspect_pages\\nWHERE event_type IN (1, 2, 3);\\n\\n-- Confirm version is at the pre-patch baseline (not partial-patch state)\\nSELECT\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductVersion') AS product_version,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductUpdateLevel') AS cu_level;\\n\\n-- Test basic query functionality\\nSELECT TOP 1 name FROM sys.databases WHERE state = 0;\\nSELECT TOP 1 name FROM sys.objects WHERE type = 'U';", "parameters": {"databases_to_checkdb": ["master", "model", "msdb"], "acceptable_suspect_page_count": 0}, "description": "Confirm all databases are ONLINE and CHECKDB reports no errors on key databases. Verify the version matches the pre-patch baseline (not a partial upgrade state). If all checks pass and hardware is certified, the instance is ready for a fresh patch attempt. Schedule the patch retry in a new maintenance window with hardware monitoring active."}	null	low	f	t	01:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389706
2363	sqlserver	instruct	Run CU/SP installer as local Administrator or dedicated setup account	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"installer_launch": "Right-click > Run as Administrator, or elevated cmd prompt", "domain_account_note": "If domain account, verify it is a member of local Administrators via: net localgroup Administrators", "required_privileges": "Local Administrators group membership on target server node"}, "description": "Right-click the CU or SP setup executable and choose 'Run as administrator'. Alternatively, open an elevated Command Prompt (Run as Administrator) and launch the installer from there. Do not launch from a UAC-limited account or a domain service account that lacks local admin rights. If using a dedicated SQL admin account, add it to the local Administrators group on the target node before running setup."}	null	low	t	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389707
2365	sqlserver	verify	Confirm service account permissions and retry patch installation	{"sql": "-- Verify service account after change\\nSELECT servicename, service_account, status_desc\\nFROM sys.dm_server_services;\\n\\n-- Confirm no permission errors in error log since account change\\nCREATE TABLE #errlog (LogDate DATETIME, ProcessInfo VARCHAR(64), Text NVARCHAR(MAX));\\nINSERT INTO #errlog EXEC xp_readerrorlog 0, 1, N'permission';\\nINSERT INTO #errlog EXEC xp_readerrorlog 0, 1, N'denied';\\nSELECT * FROM #errlog WHERE LogDate > DATEADD(hour, -1, GETDATE());\\nDROP TABLE #errlog;\\n\\n-- Verify SQL Server is running and accessible\\nSELECT\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductVersion') AS version,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductLevel') AS patch_level,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('Edition') AS edition,\\n  GETDATE() AS verification_time", "parameters": {"expected_outcome": "No permission denied errors; installer completes without error; ProductLevel shows new CU/SP level"}, "description": "After correcting the service account and permissions, verify SQL Server is running cleanly with no new permission errors in the error log. Then re-run the CU/SP installer. Upon successful completion, confirm the new patch level is reflected in SERVERPROPERTY."}	null	low	f	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389708
2368	sqlserver	verify	Verify SQL Server is running on supported OS and apply the CU/SP	{"sql": "-- Confirm SQL Server is running post-OS-upgrade or post-migration\\nSELECT\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ServerName') AS server_name,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductVersion') AS version,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('Edition') AS edition;\\n\\n-- Confirm OS is now at a supported version\\nSELECT host_platform, host_distribution, host_release\\nFROM sys.dm_os_host_info;\\n\\n-- Re-run platform compatibility check — expect 'Platform appears supported'\\nSELECT\\n  CASE\\n    WHEN CAST(SERVERPROPERTY('ProductMajorVersion') AS INT) IN (15,16)\\n      AND (SELECT host_release FROM sys.dm_os_host_info) >= '10.0'\\n      THEN 'Platform supported — proceed with CU/SP installation'\\n    ELSE 'Platform still not supported — review OS version'\\n  END AS platform_status;\\n\\n-- After CU/SP applied, verify new patch level\\nSELECT\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductVersion') AS new_version,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductLevel') AS new_patch_level,\\n  GETDATE() AS verified_at", "parameters": {"expected_patch_level_example": "CU14 (KB5007182) or whichever CU/SP was the target", "expected_host_release_sql2019_2022": ">= 10.0 (Windows Server 2016+)"}, "description": "Confirm OS version now meets the minimum requirement for the installed SQL Server version. Then re-run the CU/SP installer. After completion, verify the new patch level is reflected and all databases are online and accessible."}	null	low	f	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.395559
2366	sqlserver	execute	Document current SQL Server configuration and confirm target OS/version	{"sql": "-- Capture full configuration inventory before any changes\\nSELECT\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ServerName') AS server_name,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductVersion') AS product_version,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductLevel') AS product_level,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductMajorVersion') AS major_version,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('Edition') AS edition,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('EngineEdition') AS engine_edition,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('IsClustered') AS is_clustered,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('IsHadrEnabled') AS is_hadr_enabled;\\n\\nSELECT host_platform, host_distribution, host_release,\\n  host_service_pack_level, host_sku\\nFROM sys.dm_os_host_info;\\n\\nSELECT cpu_count, physical_memory_kb / 1024 AS physical_memory_mb,\\n  virtual_machine_type_desc\\nFROM sys.dm_os_sys_info;\\n\\n-- List all user databases for migration checklist\\nSELECT name, compatibility_level, recovery_model_desc,\\n  state_desc, is_read_only, collation_name,\\n  CAST(SUM(size * 8.0 / 1024) AS INT) AS size_mb\\nFROM sys.databases d\\nJOIN sys.master_files f ON d.database_id = f.database_id\\nWHERE d.database_id > 4 AND d.state = 0\\nGROUP BY d.name, compatibility_level, recovery_model_desc,\\n  d.state_desc, is_read_only, collation_name\\nORDER BY d.name", "parameters": {"reference": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/sql-server/install/hardware-and-software-requirements-for-installing-sql-server", "minimum_ram": "2 GB minimum, 4 GB recommended", "minimum_os_sql2019": "Windows Server 2016 (10.0) or later", "minimum_os_sql2022": "Windows Server 2016 (10.0) or later"}, "description": "Before making any changes, capture a complete inventory of the SQL Server instance: version, edition, clustering/AG status, OS details, and all user database metadata. This output will be used to verify the migration target matches the source configuration. Reference Microsoft's SQL Server hardware and software requirements page to confirm the supported OS versions for the installed SQL Server edition."}	{"description": "No changes made in this step; documentation only"}	low	f	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389709
31440	mariadb	execute	mysql_recovery_with_innodb_force	{"sql": "-- If MySQL won't start, add to my.cnf:\\n-- [mysqld]\\n-- innodb_force_recovery = 1\\n-- Start with increasing levels (1-6) until MySQL starts\\n-- Then dump data with mysqldump\\nSELECT 'Set innodb_force_recovery in my.cnf if MySQL cannot start' AS instruction;", "parameters": ["recovery_level"], "description": "Use InnoDB force recovery mode to start MySQL after crash for data dump"}	\N	medium	t	t	minutes	2026-05-11 22:17:39.229791
