2604	sqlserver	instruct	Provide developer guidance to eliminate static connection variable patterns	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"correct_pattern": "using (var conn = new SqlConnection(connStr)) { conn.Open(); ... }", "code_patterns_to_eliminate": ["static SqlConnection _conn = new SqlConnection(...)", "private SqlConnection _conn stored as class field", "Application.Cache[\\"conn\\"] = connection", "HttpContext.Items[\\"conn\\"] = connection (ASP.NET)"]}, "description": "Instruct the development team to audit all code paths for class-level or static SqlConnection fields. Required remediation pattern: (1) Move SqlConnection instantiation inside the method scope or use dependency injection with a scoped lifetime. (2) Wrap all SqlConnection usage in using() blocks or try/finally with explicit conn.Close(). (3) For .NET, never store SqlConnection in static fields, application caches, or singletons. (4) For Java (JDBC), never assign connections to static or instance fields on long-lived objects. (5) Ensure connection pool settings (Min Pool Size, Max Pool Size, Connection Lifetime) are explicitly configured in the connection string. (6) Deploy connection leak detection (e.g., Application Insights connection pool monitoring, JDBC leak detection threshold) to catch regressions. After code fix is deployed, restart the application to flush the existing static references and verify session count drops."}	{"description": "No rollback needed for code guidance. If new application build causes regressions, roll back the deployment and retain the SQL Server Agent cleanup job as a safeguard."}	low	f	t	3 days	2026-02-24 13:10:27.444976
2605	sqlserver	verify	Confirm session count has normalized after application remediation	{"sql": "-- Run 24-48 hours after application deployment\\nSELECT\\n  s.program_name,\\n  COUNT(*) AS total_sleeping,\\n  SUM(CASE WHEN DATEDIFF(HOUR, s.login_time, GETDATE()) > 24 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS sessions_over_24h,\\n  MAX(DATEDIFF(HOUR, s.login_time, GETDATE())) AS max_session_age_hours,\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE object_name LIKE '%General Statistics%'\\n     AND counter_name = 'User Connections') AS total_user_connections\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions s\\nWHERE s.status = 'sleeping'\\n  AND s.is_user_process = 1\\nGROUP BY s.program_name\\nORDER BY total_sleeping DESC;", "parameters": {"success_criteria": "sessions_over_24h = 0 for affected application; total_user_connections within pool max size limits"}, "description": "Confirms that sessions older than 24 hours have been eliminated and total connection count is within the expected range for the active workload."}	{"description": "If sessions_over_24h remains > 0, escalate: the application may have multiple deployment units or static references in a shared library not yet updated."}	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.444977
3821	sqlserver	instruct	Refactor queries to select only required columns	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "For each identified wide-result query: (1) Replace SELECT * with explicit column list of only needed columns. (2) Remove wide VARCHAR/NVARCHAR columns from SELECT if not needed downstream. (3) For ORDER BY queries, select narrow columns and add wide columns in a separate lookup if needed. (4) For GROUP BY queries, ensure only aggregated columns and grouping keys are selected. (5) If wide columns are needed, consider returning them in a second query using the primary key from the first query. (6) As an immediate workaround, add OPTION (MAX_GRANT_PERCENT = N) to cap the memory grant."}	null	low	f	t	5 days	2026-02-24 13:10:27.579287
2606	sqlserver	execute	Kill accumulated sleeping sessions for the affected application	{"sql": "-- Identify the top offending program_name from detection results, then kill all its idle sessions\\n-- Replace @target_program with the program_name identified in detection step 1\\nDECLARE @target_program NVARCHAR(256) = N'<program_name_from_detection>';\\n\\n-- Review before executing\\nSELECT\\n  'KILL ' + CAST(session_id AS VARCHAR(10)) AS kill_statement,\\n  session_id, login_time, last_request_end_time,\\n  DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) AS idle_minutes,\\n  open_transaction_count\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions\\nWHERE status = 'sleeping'\\n  AND is_user_process = 1\\n  AND program_name = @target_program\\n  AND open_transaction_count = 0\\n  AND DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) > 30\\nORDER BY login_time ASC;\\n\\n-- Execute KILL statements after review:\\n-- KILL <session_id>;", "parameters": {"target": "program_name identified in detection", "minimum_idle_minutes": 30, "open_transaction_filter": "= 0 only"}, "description": "Targets the specific program_name accumulating the most idle sessions. Sessions with open transactions are excluded and must be reviewed separately."}	{"description": "KILL cannot be undone. The application will re-establish connections on its next event trigger. Confirm with application team that all targeted sessions are genuinely abandoned before proceeding."}	medium	t	f	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.444977
2607	sqlserver	configure	Deploy SQL Server Agent watchdog job to automatically reclaim idle connections	{"sql": "USE msdb;\\nGO\\n\\n-- Create a watchdog job that kills sessions idle > 30 minutes with no open transactions\\n-- Adjust @idle_threshold_min to match application SLA\\nEXEC sp_add_job\\n  @job_name = N'Watchdog: Kill Idle Sessions (No Open Txn)';\\n\\nEXEC sp_add_jobstep\\n  @job_name = N'Watchdog: Kill Idle Sessions (No Open Txn)',\\n  @step_name = N'Kill idle sleeping sessions',\\n  @command = N'\\nDECLARE @idle_threshold_min INT = 30;\\nDECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(200);\\nDECLARE kill_cursor CURSOR FOR\\n  SELECT CAST(session_id AS VARCHAR(10))\\n  FROM sys.dm_exec_sessions\\n  WHERE status = ''sleeping''\\n    AND is_user_process = 1\\n    AND open_transaction_count = 0\\n    AND DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) > @idle_threshold_min;\\nOPEN kill_cursor;\\nFETCH NEXT FROM kill_cursor INTO @sql;\\nWHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0\\nBEGIN\\n  EXEC (''KILL '' + @sql);\\n  FETCH NEXT FROM kill_cursor INTO @sql;\\nEND\\nCLOSE kill_cursor;\\nDEALLOCATE kill_cursor;',\\n  @on_success_action = 1;\\n\\n-- Schedule every 10 minutes\\nEXEC sp_add_schedule\\n  @schedule_name = N'Every 10 minutes',\\n  @freq_type = 4,\\n  @freq_interval = 1,\\n  @freq_subday_type = 4,\\n  @freq_subday_interval = 10;\\n\\nEXEC sp_attach_schedule\\n  @job_name = N'Watchdog: Kill Idle Sessions (No Open Txn)',\\n  @schedule_name = N'Every 10 minutes';\\n\\nEXEC sp_add_jobserver\\n  @job_name = N'Watchdog: Kill Idle Sessions (No Open Txn)';", "parameters": {"scope": "all sleeping user sessions with no open transactions", "idle_threshold_minutes": 30, "schedule_interval_minutes": 10}, "description": "Deploys a SQL Server Agent job that runs every 10 minutes to terminate sessions idle more than 30 minutes with no open transactions. Acts as a DBA-side safeguard while application code is remediated."}	{"sql": "USE msdb;\\nEXEC sp_delete_job @job_name = N'Watchdog: Kill Idle Sessions (No Open Txn)';", "description": "Drop the watchdog job once the application code fix is verified and connection counts are stable."}	low	f	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.444978
2608	sqlserver	instruct	Instruct developers to deregister event listeners and scope connections within handlers	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"key_patterns_to_fix": ["SqlConnection stored in lambda closure captured by event delegate", "Connection opened in event subscription, closed only on unsubscribe", "Callback storing connection reference without IDisposable cleanup"]}, "description": "Provide the development team with the following remediation requirements: (1) Never store a SqlConnection/IDbConnection reference in an event listener field, delegate, or callback closure. (2) Each event handler invocation must open, use, and close its own connection within the handler scope using a using() block or equivalent. (3) For event-sourcing or message-consumer patterns: obtain a connection from the pool at the start of each message handler, dispose it before acknowledging the message. (4) Unregister event handlers when the owning object is disposed: implement IDisposable on any class that subscribes to events, and call event -= handler in Dispose(). (5) For long-lived subscribers (e.g., SignalR hubs, background services): use a connection factory pattern injected via DI, not a stored connection instance. (6) Add integration tests that fire 50+ events in sequence and assert that sys.dm_exec_sessions sleeping count does not grow."}	{"description": "No rollback for code guidance. The watchdog job from step 2 continues to protect the database while the fix is deployed."}	low	f	t	3 days	2026-02-24 13:10:27.444979
2610	sqlserver	execute	Kill burst sleeping sessions from the recursive leak pattern	{"sql": "-- Kill the burst sessions: all sleeping, no open transactions, from the same program_name\\n-- Replace @burst_app with the program_name identified in detection\\nDECLARE @burst_app NVARCHAR(256) = N'<program_name_from_detection>';\\n\\nSELECT\\n  'KILL ' + CAST(session_id AS VARCHAR(10)) AS kill_statement,\\n  session_id,\\n  login_time,\\n  DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) AS idle_minutes,\\n  open_transaction_count\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions\\nWHERE status = 'sleeping'\\n  AND is_user_process = 1\\n  AND program_name = @burst_app\\n  AND open_transaction_count = 0\\n  AND DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) > 10\\nORDER BY login_time;\\n\\n-- Execute KILL statements after review:\\n-- KILL <session_id>;", "parameters": {"target_application": "program_name from detection step 1", "idle_threshold_minutes": 10, "open_transaction_filter": "= 0"}, "description": "Terminates the sleeping sessions accumulated during the recursive burst. Scoped to the identified application and sessions with no open transactions."}	{"description": "KILL is irreversible. The application will create new connections on its next invocation. If the recursive function is still deployed and active, new bursts will recur immediately — do not kill without coordinating an application restart or fix deployment."}	medium	t	f	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.444979
2611	sqlserver	configure	Add Connection Lifetime to connection string to force pool recycle and cap accumulation	{"sql": "-- No T-SQL change required. Apply to application connection string.\\n-- Connection Lifetime=60 forces connections older than 60 seconds to be destroyed\\n-- when returned to the pool, preventing long-term accumulation.\\n-- Example for .NET SqlClient:\\n-- \\"Server=...;Database=...;Connection Timeout=30;Min Pool Size=2;Max Pool Size=50;Connection Lifetime=120;\\"\\n\\n-- Verify current connection count after change (run before and after restart):\\nSELECT\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE object_name LIKE '%General Statistics%'\\n     AND counter_name = 'User Connections') AS current_connections,\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE object_name LIKE '%General Statistics%'\\n     AND counter_name = 'Logins/sec') AS logins_per_sec,\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE object_name LIKE '%General Statistics%'\\n     AND counter_name = 'Logouts/sec') AS logouts_per_sec;", "parameters": {"max_pool_size": 50, "min_pool_size": 2, "connection_lifetime_seconds": 120}, "description": "Instructs the application team to add Connection Lifetime to the connection string so that pooled connections are recycled after a defined period, preventing burst connections from persisting indefinitely even if a code fix is delayed."}	{"description": "Remove the Connection Lifetime parameter from the connection string and restart the application. Note: removing Connection Lifetime reverts to no automatic pool recycle."}	low	f	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.44498
5834	postgresql	execute	Identify orphaned prepared transactions	{"sql": "SELECT gid, prepared, owner, database,\\n       now() - prepared AS age,\\n       transaction AS xid\\nFROM pg_prepared_xacts\\nORDER BY prepared;", "parameters": [], "description": "Lists all prepared transactions with their age and associated XID to identify orphaned ones blocking freeze."}	\N	low	f	t	seconds	2026-02-25 08:59:43.77256
2612	sqlserver	instruct	Refactor recursive functions to pass a single connection rather than opening per call	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"correct_pattern": "void RecursiveMethod(SqlConnection conn, ...) { /* use conn, do not open/close */ }", "pattern_to_eliminate": "SqlConnection conn = new SqlConnection(...); conn.Open(); inside recursive method body"}, "description": "Instruct the development team to apply the following fix pattern to all recursive database functions: (1) The top-level calling function opens the connection and passes it as a parameter to the recursive function. (2) The recursive function signature accepts IDbConnection (or SqlConnection) as a parameter — it never opens or owns its own connection. (3) The connection is closed/disposed only in the top-level caller after all recursion completes. (4) All early exit paths in the recursive function (exceptions, boundary conditions) must NOT attempt to close the passed connection — only the owner closes it. (5) If parallelism is needed within the recursion (e.g., Parallel.ForEach), use a connection-per-thread pattern scoped to each thread's work unit, not a shared connection. (6) Add a recursion depth guard and a maximum connection pool size alert so that future regressions are caught before pool exhaustion."}	{"description": "If refactored code causes regressions, revert to the previous version. The Connection Lifetime parameter from step 2 provides a pool-level safeguard during the fix cycle."}	low	f	t	2 days	2026-02-24 13:10:27.444981
2613	sqlserver	verify	Confirm no new burst sessions appear after recursive function is fixed	{"sql": "-- Run after deploying the fixed application build\\n-- Trigger a representative recursive operation and immediately check for session bursts\\nSELECT s.program_name, s.host_name,\\n  COUNT(*) AS session_count,\\n  MIN(s.login_time) AS burst_start,\\n  MAX(s.login_time) AS burst_end,\\n  DATEDIFF(SECOND, MIN(s.login_time), MAX(s.login_time)) AS burst_duration_sec\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions s\\nWHERE s.status = 'sleeping'\\n  AND s.is_user_process = 1\\n  AND DATEDIFF(MINUTE, s.last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) > 5\\nGROUP BY s.program_name, s.host_name\\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 5;\\n\\n-- Also verify pool connection count is stable\\nSELECT cntr_value AS user_connections\\nFROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\nWHERE object_name LIKE '%General Statistics%'\\n  AND counter_name = 'User Connections';", "parameters": {"success_criteria": "No group of 5+ sleeping sessions with burst_duration_sec < 300 from the same application after recursive operation"}, "description": "Triggers a representative recursive workload and confirms no session burst pattern appears in sys.dm_exec_sessions. User connection count should remain stable."}	{"description": "If burst pattern recurs, the fix is incomplete — likely a second recursive code path not covered by the initial fix. Repeat code audit with a broader scope."}	low	f	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.444981
2635	sqlserver	instruct	Instruct middleware developers to return connections to the pool after each proxied request	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"health_check_note": "Health check connections must be pooled separately with their own idle timeout", "critical_requirement": "Connection must be released to pool on EVERY proxied request completion, including error paths"}, "description": "Provide the middleware/platform team with the following remediation requirements: (1) Connection Scope: Each proxied request must acquire a connection at the start of the request and return (close/dispose) it at the end of the request — connections must not persist across requests in middleware internal state. (2) Buffer Flush: If the middleware buffers requests and dispatches them to SQL Server in batches, the connection must be returned to the pool between batches, not held open during buffer accumulation. (3) Connection Reuse within a Request: Within a single proxied request, reuse one connection for all SQL operations rather than opening one per SQL call. (4) Scale-out Awareness: Middleware must account for the number of running instances when sizing the per-instance pool. Use environment variables or service discovery to dynamically calculate per-instance limits when instances scale horizontally. (5) Health Check Connections: If the middleware performs SQL Server health checks, use a separate dedicated health-check connection or connection pool distinct from the request-serving pool, and ensure health check connections have their own idle eviction timeout. (6) Implement a connection pool metrics endpoint on the middleware that exposes current active, idle, and pending connection counts — this enables proactive monitoring before pool exhaustion."}	{"description": "No rollback for code/config guidance. Per-instance pool limits from step 2 continue to protect the server during the middleware fix cycle."}	low	f	t	7 days	2026-02-24 13:10:27.444982
2614	sqlserver	execute	Kill orphaned sessions attributed to library-driven connection leak	{"sql": "-- Target sessions from the affected application with library-pattern last queries\\n-- Replace @target_program with the program_name identified in detection\\nDECLARE @target_program NVARCHAR(256) = N'<program_name_from_detection>';\\n\\nSELECT\\n  'KILL ' + CAST(s.session_id AS VARCHAR(10)) AS kill_statement,\\n  s.session_id,\\n  s.login_time,\\n  DATEDIFF(MINUTE, s.last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) AS idle_minutes,\\n  s.open_transaction_count,\\n  t.text AS last_query\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions s\\nJOIN sys.dm_exec_connections c ON s.session_id = c.session_id\\nCROSS APPLY sys.dm_exec_sql_text(c.most_recent_sql_handle) t\\nWHERE s.status = 'sleeping'\\n  AND s.is_user_process = 1\\n  AND s.program_name = @target_program\\n  AND s.open_transaction_count = 0\\n  AND DATEDIFF(MINUTE, s.last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) > 30\\nORDER BY s.login_time;\\n\\n-- Execute KILL statements after review:\\n-- KILL <session_id>;", "parameters": {"target_application": "program_name from detection", "idle_threshold_minutes": 30, "open_transaction_filter": "= 0"}, "description": "Kills sleeping sessions from the application that uses the leaking library. Reviews last_query to confirm library SQL patterns before executing."}	{"description": "KILL is irreversible. Application will reconnect via the library on next invocation. If the library is still deployed in its leaking version, sessions will re-accumulate."}	medium	t	f	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.444983
