2624	sqlserver	instruct	Instruct developers to add explicit connection disposal in ORM exception handling paths	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"critical_patterns": ["SaveChanges() in try block without catch that disposes context", "BeginTransaction() without using block or explicit commit/rollback in all paths", "Session.close() only in happy path, not in exception handler"]}, "description": "Provide the development team with the following ORM connection management requirements: (1) Entity Framework Core: Always use DbContext in a using block or rely on DI scoped lifetime — never store DbContext in a long-lived field. (2) Wrap all SaveChanges() calls in try/catch; in the catch block, call context.Database.RollbackTransaction() if a transaction was explicitly started, then dispose the context. (3) For explicit transaction management: use using (var transaction = await context.Database.BeginTransactionAsync()) { ... await transaction.CommitAsync(); } — the using block ensures RollbackTransaction is called on exception. (4) Hibernate: Set session.flushMode = FlushMode.COMMIT and close the session in a finally block. Use try { ... session.close(); } finally { session.close(); } pattern. (5) For repository pattern: implement IDisposable on the repository and dispose the ORM session/context in Dispose(). (6) Add exception simulation tests: inject a database error mid-transaction and verify that sys.dm_exec_sessions shows no lingering open transactions from the test application afterward."}	{"description": "No rollback for code guidance. The monitoring query from step 1 can be re-run to detect regressions."}	low	f	t	5 days	2026-02-24 13:10:27.448137
2625	sqlserver	verify	Confirm no sessions with open DML transactions remain sleeping after ORM fix	{"sql": "-- Run after deploying ORM fix; simulate an error condition in the application\\n-- then verify no abandoned open-transaction sessions appear\\nSELECT s.program_name,\\n  COUNT(*) AS sessions_with_open_txn,\\n  MAX(DATEDIFF(MINUTE, s.last_request_end_time, GETDATE())) AS max_idle_min,\\n  SUM(dt.database_transaction_log_bytes_used) / 1048576.0 AS total_log_used_mb\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions s\\nJOIN sys.dm_tran_session_transactions st ON s.session_id = st.session_id\\nJOIN sys.dm_tran_database_transactions dt ON st.transaction_id = dt.transaction_id\\nWHERE s.status = 'sleeping'\\n  AND s.is_user_process = 1\\n  AND s.open_transaction_count > 0\\n  AND DATEDIFF(MINUTE, s.last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) > 5\\nGROUP BY s.program_name;", "parameters": {"success_criteria": "Zero rows returned for affected program_name even after simulated ORM error conditions"}, "description": "After injecting a simulated error condition in the application, confirms that zero sessions with open transactions remain sleeping for the fixed application."}	{"description": "If open-transaction sessions persist after error injection, the exception code path fix is incomplete. Identify the specific exception type that bypasses disposal and add targeted handling."}	low	f	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.448138
2626	sqlserver	execute	Kill sessions idle beyond the target pool timeout threshold	{"sql": "-- Kill all sleeping sessions idle longer than 2 hours with no open transactions\\n-- This clears the backlog before applying the configuration fix\\nSELECT\\n  'KILL ' + CAST(session_id AS VARCHAR(10)) AS kill_statement,\\n  session_id, program_name, host_name,\\n  DATEDIFF(HOUR, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) AS idle_hours,\\n  open_transaction_count\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions\\nWHERE status = 'sleeping'\\n  AND is_user_process = 1\\n  AND open_transaction_count = 0\\n  AND DATEDIFF(HOUR, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) >= 2\\nORDER BY idle_hours DESC;\\n\\n-- Review the list, then execute KILL statements:\\n-- KILL <session_id>;", "parameters": {"idle_threshold_hours": 2, "open_transaction_filter": "= 0"}, "description": "Generates KILL statements for all sessions idle 2 or more hours with no open transactions. Reviews the list to confirm no legitimate long-running batch jobs are included before executing."}	{"description": "KILL is irreversible. Applications will reconnect automatically via their pool on the next database request. If any session was a legitimate long-running batch, the batch will fail — confirm with the application team before killing."}	low	t	f	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.448139
2647	sqlserver	instruct	Instruct batch job owner to add connection disposal to error paths	{"parameters": {"urgency": "High — partial failures will recur and exhaust the pool", "target_team": "Batch job / ETL development team", "code_pattern": "using (SqlConnection conn = new SqlConnection(cs)) { using (SqlTransaction txn = conn.BeginTransaction()) { try { /* work */ txn.Commit(); } catch { txn.Rollback(); throw; } } }"}, "description": "The batch job must wrap all connection acquisition in try/finally or using blocks. Key fixes: (1) Wrap every SqlConnection in a 'using' block: 'using (var conn = new SqlConnection(cs)) { conn.Open(); ProcessBatch(conn); }'. (2) In parallel batch patterns (Parallel.ForEach, Task.WhenAll), each task must own and dispose its connection — never share a single connection across parallel tasks. (3) On partial failure, catch the exception, log it, and let the 'using' or 'finally' block dispose the connection before retrying or exiting. (4) If a transaction-per-batch-item pattern is used, wrap both connection AND transaction in nested using blocks."}	{"description": "Code change; rollback via deployment revert."}	low	f	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.449149
4602	sqlserver	verify	Compare DML vs DDL audit coverage	{"sql": "SELECT\\n  SUM(CASE WHEN sasd.audit_action_name LIKE '%ACCESS%' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS dml_access_groups,\\n  SUM(CASE WHEN sasd.audit_action_name LIKE '%CHANGE%' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS ddl_change_groups\\nFROM sys.server_audit_specifications sas\\nJOIN sys.server_audit_specification_details sasd ON sas.server_specification_id = sasd.server_specification_id\\nWHERE sas.is_state_enabled = 1;", "parameters": [], "description": "Shows the imbalance between DML access groups and DDL change groups."}	null	low	f	t	00:01:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.751078
2627	sqlserver	configure	Add idle connection eviction settings to all application connection pool configurations	{"sql": "-- No T-SQL required. Apply to application connection string or pool configuration.\\n\\n-- .NET SqlClient (connection string):\\n-- Connection Lifetime=120;    -- Destroy connections older than 120s when returned to pool\\n-- Connect Timeout=30;         -- Fail fast on pool exhaustion\\n-- Min Pool Size=2;            -- Keep 2 warm connections in pool\\n-- Max Pool Size=50;           -- Cap total pool size\\n\\n-- HikariCP (Java, application.properties or hikari.properties):\\n-- spring.datasource.hikari.idle-timeout=120000          -- Evict idle connections after 2 min\\n-- spring.datasource.hikari.minimum-idle=2\\n-- spring.datasource.hikari.maximum-pool-size=50\\n-- spring.datasource.hikari.keepalive-time=60000         -- Keep-alive ping to prevent firewall drops\\n-- spring.datasource.hikari.connection-timeout=30000\\n\\n-- Apache DBCP2 / Tomcat JDBC Pool:\\n-- minEvictableIdleTimeMillis=120000\\n-- timeBetweenEvictionRunsMillis=60000\\n-- testWhileIdle=true\\n-- validationQuery=SELECT 1\\n\\n-- Verify connection count drops after application restart with new config:\\nSELECT program_name,\\n  COUNT(*) AS total_sessions,\\n  SUM(CASE WHEN DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) > 120 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS idle_over_2hr\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions\\nWHERE status = 'sleeping' AND is_user_process = 1\\nGROUP BY program_name\\nORDER BY idle_over_2hr DESC;", "parameters": {"max_pool_size": 50, "min_pool_size": 2, "keepalive_seconds": 60, "recommended_idle_eviction_seconds": 120}, "description": "Provides idle connection eviction configuration for the most common connection pool frameworks. Each application must be restarted with the new configuration for the change to take effect."}	{"description": "Remove the idle timeout settings from the connection string and restart the application to revert to default (no eviction). The default behavior is to keep connections in the pool indefinitely, which is the source of the original issue."}	low	f	t	02:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.449679
2628	sqlserver	verify	Confirm idle session distribution normalizes after pool configuration change	{"sql": "-- Run 2-4 hours after all applications are restarted with new pool config\\n-- Expect 8hr+ and 2-8hr buckets to be near-zero\\nSELECT\\n  CASE\\n    WHEN DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) < 5 THEN '0-5min'\\n    WHEN DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) < 30 THEN '5-30min'\\n    WHEN DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) < 120 THEN '30min-2hr'\\n    WHEN DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) < 480 THEN '2-8hr'\\n    ELSE '8hr+'\\n  END AS idle_bucket,\\n  COUNT(*) AS session_count\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions\\nWHERE status = 'sleeping' AND is_user_process = 1\\nGROUP BY\\n  CASE\\n    WHEN DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) < 5 THEN '0-5min'\\n    WHEN DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) < 30 THEN '5-30min'\\n    WHEN DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) < 120 THEN '30min-2hr'\\n    WHEN DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) < 480 THEN '2-8hr'\\n    ELSE '8hr+'\\n  END\\nORDER BY MIN(DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()));", "parameters": {"success_criteria": "2-8hr bucket = 0, 8hr+ bucket = 0 after 4 hours of normal operation"}, "description": "Re-runs the detection step 1 query to confirm the idle distribution has shifted. After successful configuration, the 2-8hr and 8hr+ buckets should have zero or near-zero sessions."}	{"description": "If 2hr+ buckets are still populated after the configuration change and application restart, one or more applications did not receive the updated configuration. Identify which program_names still have long-idle sessions and re-apply the config to those applications."}	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.449682
2629	sqlserver	execute	Kill all orphaned sleeping sessions with no open transactions idle over 60 minutes	{"sql": "-- Kill orphaned sessions: sleeping, no open transactions, idle > 60 min\\nSELECT\\n  'KILL ' + CAST(session_id AS VARCHAR(10)) AS kill_statement,\\n  session_id, program_name, host_name,\\n  DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) AS idle_minutes,\\n  login_time\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions\\nWHERE status = 'sleeping'\\n  AND is_user_process = 1\\n  AND open_transaction_count = 0\\n  AND DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) > 60\\nORDER BY idle_minutes DESC;\\n\\n-- After review, execute KILL statements:\\n-- KILL <session_id>;", "parameters": {"idle_threshold_minutes": 60, "open_transaction_filter": "= 0"}, "description": "Generates KILL statements for all orphaned sessions with no open transactions idle more than 60 minutes. These sessions have been abandoned by the application setting the connection variable to null."}	{"description": "KILL is irreversible. The .NET garbage collector may or may not have already finalized the SqlConnection object — killing the session ensures the server-side slot is reclaimed regardless of client-side GC state."}	low	t	f	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.449683
3210	sqlserver	verify	Measure NULL percentage in the indexed column	{"sql": "-- Replace <schema>, <table>, <column> with actual values\\nSELECT\\n  COUNT(*) AS total_rows,\\n  SUM(CASE WHEN [<column>] IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS null_rows,\\n  SUM(CASE WHEN [<column>] IS NOT NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS non_null_rows,\\n  CAST(SUM(CASE WHEN [<column>] IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS FLOAT)\\n    / COUNT(*) * 100 AS null_pct\\nFROM [<schema>].[<table>];", "parameters": {"table": "Target table", "column": "The nullable leading key column", "schema": "Target schema"}, "description": "Measures the exact proportion of NULL values to determine how much smaller a filtered index would be"}	null	low	f	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.525864
2630	sqlserver	configure	Set Connection Lifetime to ensure pool automatically recycles connections that were nulled without close	{"sql": "-- Apply to application connection string\\n-- Connection Lifetime forces connections to be destroyed (not recycled) when returned to pool\\n-- after exceeding the lifetime threshold — mitigates the delay between null-assignment and GC finalization\\n\\n-- .NET SqlClient:\\n-- \\"Server=...;Database=...;Connection Lifetime=120;Min Pool Size=2;Max Pool Size=50;\\"\\n\\n-- Verify the change reduces orphaned session count after application restart:\\nSELECT\\n  COUNT(*) AS orphaned_sessions,\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE object_name LIKE '%General Statistics%'\\n     AND counter_name = 'Logins/sec') AS logins_per_sec,\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE object_name LIKE '%General Statistics%'\\n     AND counter_name = 'Logouts/sec') AS logouts_per_sec\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions\\nWHERE status = 'sleeping'\\n  AND is_user_process = 1\\n  AND open_transaction_count = 0\\n  AND DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) > 60;", "parameters": {"connection_lifetime_seconds": 120}, "description": "Adds Connection Lifetime to the connection string so that pooled connections are destroyed after a maximum age, preventing nulled-without-close connections from sitting in the pool indefinitely."}	{"description": "Remove Connection Lifetime from the connection string and restart the application to revert to default pool behavior."}	low	f	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.449684
2700	sqlserver	execute	Calculate safe per-instance pool ceiling	{"sql": "-- Gather capacity inputs for pool sizing calculation\\nSELECT\\n  (SELECT CAST(value_in_use AS INT) FROM sys.configurations WHERE name = 'max worker threads') AS max_worker_threads,\\n  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sys.dm_os_schedulers WHERE status = 'VISIBLE ONLINE') AS cpu_schedulers,\\n  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sys.dm_exec_sessions WHERE is_user_process = 0) AS system_sessions,\\n  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sys.dm_exec_sessions WHERE is_user_process = 1) AS current_user_sessions,\\n  -- Safe user connection capacity = max_workers - system_reserve(64) - admin_reserve(32)\\n  (SELECT CAST(value_in_use AS INT) FROM sys.configurations WHERE name = 'max worker threads') - 96 AS safe_user_capacity", "parameters": {"admin_thread_reserve": 32, "system_thread_reserve": 64}, "description": "Use safe_user_capacity to determine MaxPoolSize per instance: MaxPoolSize = FLOOR(safe_user_capacity / number_of_app_instances). Reserve 96 threads for system and admin use."}	null	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.457065
