2631	sqlserver	instruct	Instruct developers to replace all conn = null patterns with explicit Close or using blocks	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"antipattern": "conn = null (without prior Close/Dispose)", "correct_java": "try (Connection conn = ds.getConnection()) { ... }", "correct_csharp": "using (var conn = new SqlConnection(connStr)) { conn.Open(); ... }"}, "description": "Provide the development team with the following remediation: (1) Audit the codebase for all patterns of the form: conn = null; or connection = null; and replace with: conn.Close(); conn.Dispose(); conn = null; or preferably eliminate the null assignment entirely by using a using block. (2) The correct pattern in .NET is: using (var conn = new SqlConnection(connStr)) { conn.Open(); ... } — the using block calls Dispose (which calls Close) on exit from the block regardless of exceptions. (3) In Java, use try-with-resources: try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection()) { ... } — this guarantees conn.close() is called on all exit paths. (4) For fields that hold a connection reference: implement IDisposable on the containing class and call conn.Close(); conn.Dispose(); in the Dispose() method. Never null the field without first closing. (5) Run a static code analysis tool (e.g., Roslyn analyzer CA2000, SonarQube rule java:S2095) to identify all SqlConnection/Connection objects that are not disposed before going out of scope. (6) Add a regression test: create a connection, set it to null (simulating the bug), and assert via sys.dm_exec_sessions that the session is reclaimed within the Connection Lifetime window."}	{"description": "No rollback for code guidance. Connection Lifetime from step 2 bounds the damage from any missed code paths."}	low	f	t	3 days	2026-02-24 13:10:27.4508
2632	sqlserver	verify	Confirm logins/sec and logouts/sec are balanced after code fix deployment	{"sql": "-- Logins/sec should approximately equal logouts/sec after the fix\\n-- orphaned_sessions should trend to 0\\nSELECT\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE object_name LIKE '%General Statistics%'\\n     AND counter_name = 'User Connections') AS current_connections,\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE object_name LIKE '%General Statistics%'\\n     AND counter_name = 'Logins/sec') AS logins_per_sec,\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE object_name LIKE '%General Statistics%'\\n     AND counter_name = 'Logouts/sec') AS logouts_per_sec,\\n  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sys.dm_exec_sessions\\n   WHERE status = 'sleeping' AND is_user_process = 1\\n     AND open_transaction_count = 0\\n     AND DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) > 60) AS orphaned_sessions;", "parameters": {"success_criteria": "logins_per_sec ~= logouts_per_sec (within 20%); orphaned_sessions = 0 after 2 hours"}, "description": "Confirms that logins/sec and logouts/sec are now balanced (within ±20%) and the orphaned_sessions count trends to zero. A persistent logins > logouts delta indicates the fix is incomplete."}	{"description": "If logins/sec consistently exceeds logouts/sec after the fix, there are additional code paths that null connections without closing. Re-run static analysis with broader scope."}	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.450803
2633	sqlserver	execute	Kill buffered sleeping sessions across all middleware instances	{"sql": "-- Kill sleeping sessions from all identified middleware instances\\n-- Targets sessions idle > 30 min with no open transactions, from multi-host applications\\nSELECT\\n  'KILL ' + CAST(s.session_id AS VARCHAR(10)) AS kill_statement,\\n  s.session_id, s.program_name, s.host_name,\\n  DATEDIFF(MINUTE, s.last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) AS idle_minutes\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions s\\nWHERE s.status = 'sleeping'\\n  AND s.is_user_process = 1\\n  AND s.open_transaction_count = 0\\n  AND DATEDIFF(MINUTE, s.last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) > 30\\n  AND s.program_name IN (\\n    SELECT program_name\\n    FROM sys.dm_exec_sessions\\n    WHERE status = 'sleeping' AND is_user_process = 1\\n      AND DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) > 30\\n    GROUP BY program_name\\n    HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT host_name) > 1 AND COUNT(*) > 15\\n  )\\nORDER BY s.program_name, s.host_name, s.login_time;\\n\\n-- Execute KILL statements after review:\\n-- KILL <session_id>;", "parameters": {"multi_host_filter": "program_name present on more than 1 host_name", "idle_threshold_minutes": 30, "open_transaction_filter": "= 0"}, "description": "Targets sleeping sessions from program_names that appear across multiple host names — the signature of middleware scale-out — with no open transactions and idle more than 30 minutes."}	{"description": "KILL is irreversible. Middleware instances will reconnect on their next database request. New connections will re-accumulate if the buffering configuration is not changed before the next scale-out event."}	medium	t	f	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.450804
2634	sqlserver	configure	Set per-middleware-instance pool limits and idle eviction to cap total connection consumption	{"sql": "-- No T-SQL required. Apply connection pool configuration to each middleware deployment.\\n\\n-- Calculate safe per-instance pool size:\\n-- Total safe connections = server max_connections * 0.8  (leave headroom)\\n-- Per-instance max = Total safe connections / number of middleware instances\\n-- Example: 200 max connections, 10 middleware instances = 20 per instance\\n\\n-- For .NET-based middleware (connection string per instance):\\n-- Max Pool Size=20;Min Pool Size=2;Connection Lifetime=120;Connect Timeout=30;\\n\\n-- For Java-based middleware (HikariCP per instance):\\n-- maximumPoolSize=20\\n-- minimumIdle=2\\n-- idleTimeout=120000\\n-- keepaliveTime=60000\\n-- connectionTimeout=30000\\n\\n-- For middleware that uses its own connection pool (e.g., ProxySQL, Envoy HTTP):\\n-- Set max_connections and connection_max_age at the proxy level\\n-- Ensure the proxy's max_connections * instance_count < SQL Server's max_connections * 0.8\\n\\n-- Verify after restart:\\nSELECT program_name, COUNT(DISTINCT host_name) AS instance_count, COUNT(*) AS total_connections,\\n  COUNT(*) / COUNT(DISTINCT host_name) AS connections_per_instance\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions\\nWHERE is_user_process = 1\\nGROUP BY program_name\\nHAVING COUNT(DISTINCT host_name) > 1\\nORDER BY total_connections DESC;", "parameters": {"keepalive_seconds": 60, "idle_eviction_seconds": 120, "max_pool_size_per_instance": "server_max_connections * 0.8 / middleware_instance_count"}, "description": "Calculates and applies per-instance connection pool limits so that horizontal scaling of the middleware layer does not proportionally increase connection consumption. Adds idle eviction to prevent buffered connections from persisting across request cycles."}	{"description": "Remove the added pool limit configuration from middleware instances and restart. The default (no max pool size) allows unlimited connection accumulation, which is the source of the issue."}	low	f	t	02:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.450805
2649	sqlserver	configure	Set connection pool max size limit for batch job application string	{"parameters": {"config_location": "Application connection string or configuration file", "recommended_max_pool_size": "20-50 for batch jobs (lower than OLTP pools of 100-200)"}, "description": "At the application level, configure the connection string for the batch job to include 'Max Pool Size=N' where N is a reasonable upper bound (e.g., 20-50 for a batch job). This limits how many connections a single runaway batch can consume and protects the total connection budget. Example connection string parameter: 'Max Pool Size=30;Min Pool Size=5;'. This change is made in the batch job's configuration file or connection string builder."}	{"description": "Remove or increase the Max Pool Size parameter in the connection string to restore previous behavior."}	low	f	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.451723
4700	sqlserver	verify	Confirm emergency rootcause grants are removed	{"sql": "SELECT COUNT(*) AS emergency_rootcause_grants\\nFROM sys.database_permissions dp\\nJOIN sys.database_principals pr ON dp.grantee_principal_id = pr.principal_id\\nWHERE pr.name = 'rootcause' AND dp.state_desc = 'GRANT'\\n  AND dp.permission_name IN ('ALTER', 'CONTROL', 'ALTER ANY SCHEMA', 'ALTER ANY USER');", "parameters": [], "description": "Verifies no emergency-level rootcause grants remain."}	null	low	f	t	00:01:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.770767
2636	sqlserver	verify	Confirm connections_per_instance is bounded after middleware fix and configuration change	{"sql": "-- Run after all middleware instances are restarted with new configuration\\n-- connections_per_instance should be <= configured Max Pool Size\\nSELECT program_name,\\n  COUNT(DISTINCT host_name) AS instance_count,\\n  COUNT(*) AS total_connections,\\n  COUNT(*) / COUNT(DISTINCT host_name) AS connections_per_instance,\\n  SUM(CASE WHEN status = 'sleeping' AND DATEDIFF(MINUTE, last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) > 30 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS idle_over_30min\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions\\nWHERE is_user_process = 1\\nGROUP BY program_name\\nHAVING COUNT(DISTINCT host_name) > 1\\nORDER BY total_connections DESC;\\n\\n-- Also verify total connection pressure is within safe limits\\nSELECT\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE object_name LIKE '%General Statistics%' AND counter_name = 'User Connections') AS total_connections,\\n  (SELECT CAST(value_in_use AS INT) FROM sys.configurations WHERE name = 'user connections') AS max_connections;", "parameters": {"success_criteria": "connections_per_instance <= Max Pool Size; idle_over_30min = 0 after 1 hour of normal load"}, "description": "Confirms that per-instance connection count is within the configured pool limit and idle_over_30min has been eliminated across all middleware instances."}	{"description": "If connections_per_instance still exceeds the pool limit, the middleware is not applying the new configuration. Verify the config was deployed to all instances, including any auto-scaling replicas that may have been created with old configuration."}	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.452058
2638	sqlserver	configure	Deploy SQL Server Agent job to auto-terminate abandoned transactions exceeding SLA threshold	{"sql": "USE msdb;\\nGO\\n\\n-- Job to detect and kill sleeping sessions with open transactions idle > 15 minutes\\n-- Adjust @idle_txn_threshold_min to match the maximum legitimate transaction idle time\\nEXEC sp_add_job @job_name = N'Auto-Kill: Abandoned Open Transactions';\\n\\nEXEC sp_add_jobstep\\n  @job_name = N'Auto-Kill: Abandoned Open Transactions',\\n  @step_name = N'Kill sleeping sessions with long-open transactions',\\n  @command = N'\\nDECLARE @idle_txn_threshold_min INT = 15;\\nDECLARE @session_id VARCHAR(10);\\nDECLARE @log_entry NVARCHAR(500);\\n\\nDECLARE kill_cur CURSOR FOR\\n  SELECT CAST(s.session_id AS VARCHAR(10))\\n  FROM sys.dm_tran_active_transactions at\\n  JOIN sys.dm_tran_session_transactions st ON at.transaction_id = st.transaction_id\\n  JOIN sys.dm_exec_sessions s ON st.session_id = s.session_id\\n  WHERE s.status = ''sleeping''\\n    AND s.is_user_process = 1\\n    AND DATEDIFF(MINUTE, s.last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) > @idle_txn_threshold_min;\\n\\nOPEN kill_cur;\\nFETCH NEXT FROM kill_cur INTO @session_id;\\nWHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0\\nBEGIN\\n  SET @log_entry = ''Killing session '' + @session_id + '' with abandoned open transaction at '' + CONVERT(NVARCHAR, GETDATE(), 120);\\n  EXEC xp_logevent 50001, @log_entry, ''WARNING'';\\n  EXEC (''KILL '' + @session_id);\\n  FETCH NEXT FROM kill_cur INTO @session_id;\\nEND\\nCLOSE kill_cur;\\nDEALLOCATE kill_cur;',\\n  @on_success_action = 1;\\n\\nEXEC sp_add_schedule\\n  @schedule_name = N'Every 5 minutes (abandoned txn check)',\\n  @freq_type = 4, @freq_interval = 1,\\n  @freq_subday_type = 4, @freq_subday_interval = 5;\\n\\nEXEC sp_attach_schedule\\n  @job_name = N'Auto-Kill: Abandoned Open Transactions',\\n  @schedule_name = N'Every 5 minutes (abandoned txn check)';\\n\\nEXEC sp_add_jobserver @job_name = N'Auto-Kill: Abandoned Open Transactions';", "parameters": {"audit": "xp_logevent severity WARNING for each killed session", "check_interval_minutes": 5, "idle_transaction_threshold_minutes": 15}, "description": "Creates a SQL Server Agent job that runs every 5 minutes to kill sleeping sessions with open transactions idle beyond the SLA threshold. Logs each kill event to the SQL Server error log via xp_logevent for audit purposes."}	{"sql": "USE msdb;\\nEXEC sp_delete_job @job_name = N'Auto-Kill: Abandoned Open Transactions';", "description": "Drop the Agent job if it is killing legitimate long-running transactions. Adjust @idle_txn_threshold_min before re-enabling if the threshold was set too low for the workload."}	medium	f	t	00:20:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.452062
2639	sqlserver	instruct	Provide developer guidance to ensure all transactions are explicitly committed or rolled back	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"t_sql_pattern": "BEGIN TRY BEGIN TRAN ... COMMIT TRAN END TRY BEGIN CATCH IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0 ROLLBACK TRAN; THROW; END CATCH", "dotnet_pattern": "using (var tx = conn.BeginTransaction()) { ... tx.Commit(); }", "connection_string_addition": "Transaction Binding=Explicit Unbind"}, "description": "Provide the development team with the following requirements to eliminate abandoned transactions: (1) Transaction Scope: Every BEGIN TRANSACTION must have a corresponding COMMIT or ROLLBACK in every code path, including exception handlers, early returns, and timeout paths. (2) Try/Catch/Finally Pattern (T-SQL): Use: BEGIN TRY BEGIN TRANSACTION ... COMMIT TRANSACTION END TRY BEGIN CATCH IF @@TRANCOUNT > 0 ROLLBACK TRANSACTION; THROW; END CATCH. (3) Application-layer transactions (.NET SqlTransaction): Wrap in using block; if SqlTransaction.Commit() throws, the Dispose() on the SqlTransaction object automatically issues ROLLBACK. Never catch exceptions that prevent reaching Commit() without explicitly rolling back. (4) Entity Framework transactions: Always use using (var tx = context.Database.BeginTransaction()) { ... tx.Commit(); } — the using block rolls back uncommitted transactions on dispose. (5) Savepoint management: For transactions using SAVE TRANSACTION, ensure all savepoint paths eventually either fully commit or fully rollback the outer transaction — partial commits can leave transactions in state 6 (committed with open savepoints). (6) Application-level transaction timeout: Implement a transaction timeout at the application layer (e.g., SqlCommand.CommandTimeout, TransactionScope timeout) so that runaway operations are cancelled and rolled back automatically rather than being abandoned. (7) Connection string SET options: Add Transaction Binding=Explicit Unbind to the connection string to prevent connection pool from returning a connection with an open transaction back to the pool."}	{"description": "No rollback for code guidance. The Agent job from step 2 continues to enforce the SLA threshold during the fix cycle."}	low	f	t	5 days	2026-02-24 13:10:27.452062
2650	sqlserver	verify	Confirm connection count returns to baseline after fix deployment	{"sql": "-- Check total sleeping sessions from the batch program\\nSELECT s.program_name,\\n  COUNT(*) AS total_sessions,\\n  SUM(CASE WHEN s.status = 'sleeping' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS sleeping,\\n  SUM(s.open_transaction_count) AS open_txns,\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE object_name LIKE '%General Statistics%'\\n     AND counter_name = 'User Connections') AS total_server_connections\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions s\\nWHERE s.is_user_process = 1\\nGROUP BY s.program_name\\nORDER BY total_sessions DESC;", "description": "Verifies that after code fix and KILL operations, the batch job's connection count returns to expected levels and no new open transactions accumulate during subsequent batch runs."}	{"description": "Read-only verification; no rollback needed."}	low	f	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.45275
2640	sqlserver	verify	Confirm no sleeping sessions retain long-lived open transactions after application fix	{"sql": "-- Run after deploying application fix and simulating error conditions\\n-- No rows should appear for the affected application\\nSELECT s.session_id, s.program_name,\\n  DATEDIFF(MINUTE, at.transaction_begin_time, GETDATE()) AS txn_age_min,\\n  DATEDIFF(MINUTE, s.last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) AS idle_min,\\n  dt.database_transaction_log_bytes_used / 1048576.0 AS log_used_mb,\\n  d.log_reuse_wait_desc\\nFROM sys.dm_tran_active_transactions at\\nJOIN sys.dm_tran_session_transactions st ON at.transaction_id = st.transaction_id\\nJOIN sys.dm_exec_sessions s ON st.session_id = s.session_id\\nLEFT JOIN sys.dm_tran_database_transactions dt ON at.transaction_id = dt.transaction_id\\nLEFT JOIN sys.databases d ON dt.database_id = d.database_id\\nWHERE s.status = 'sleeping'\\n  AND s.is_user_process = 1\\n  AND DATEDIFF(MINUTE, s.last_request_end_time, GETDATE()) > 5;\\n\\n-- Also verify log_reuse_wait_desc is no longer ACTIVE_TRANSACTION\\nSELECT name, log_reuse_wait_desc\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE log_reuse_wait_desc = 'ACTIVE_TRANSACTION';", "parameters": {"success_criteria": "Zero rows in first query for affected program_name; no databases with log_reuse_wait_desc = ACTIVE_TRANSACTION attributable to sleeping sessions"}, "description": "Confirms that no sleeping sessions retain open transactions after the application fix is deployed and error conditions are simulated. Also verifies that log_reuse_wait_desc has cleared for affected databases."}	{"description": "If sleeping sessions with open transactions persist, the application has additional unhandled exception paths. The Agent job from step 2 will continue to enforce the 15-minute kill threshold as a safeguard."}	low	f	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.452989
