2664	sqlserver	instruct	Standardize connection string construction in application code	{"parameters": {"correct_pattern": "IConfiguration.GetConnectionString(\\"DefaultConnection\\") → single canonical string", "rls_alternative": "Use SQL Server Row-Level Security predicates if per-user data isolation is needed", "pattern_to_eliminate": "new SqlConnectionStringBuilder { DataSource=server, UserID=perUserLogin, Password=perUserPass }"}, "description": "Audit and fix all locations in the application where connection strings are built or retrieved. Rules for pool-compatible connection strings: (1) Use a single, fixed login (service account) — never embed user credentials in per-request connection strings. For user-level data isolation, use views, row-level security, or application-level filtering instead. (2) Use a fixed 'Initial Catalog' or 'Database' parameter — never switch databases using 'USE [db]' T-SQL or by changing the database in the connection string per request. (3) Ensure 'Encrypt', 'TrustServerCertificate', and 'ApplicationName' are identical across all connection string instances in the application. (4) Store the single canonical connection string in one place (appsettings.json, environment variable, secrets manager) and inject it via dependency injection — never build it dynamically at runtime."}	{"description": "Revert application code changes via deployment rollback."}	low	f	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.453392
