2675	sqlserver	verify	Confirm connection drop pattern and identify firewall timeout threshold	{"sql": "-- Check for ASYNC_NETWORK_IO waits (indicates stalled network communication)\\nSELECT wait_type, waiting_tasks_count, wait_time_ms,\\n  wait_time_ms / NULLIF(waiting_tasks_count, 0) AS avg_wait_ms\\nFROM sys.dm_os_wait_stats\\nWHERE wait_type IN ('ASYNC_NETWORK_IO', 'PREEMPTIVE_OS_AUTHENTICATIONOPS',\\n  'PREEMPTIVE_OS_ACCEPTSECURITYCONTEXT')\\nORDER BY wait_time_ms DESC;\\n\\n-- Check login churn rate as indicator of dropped connections being re-established\\nSELECT cntr_value AS total_logins\\nFROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\nWHERE object_name LIKE '%General Statistics%' AND counter_name = 'Logins/sec';\\n\\n-- Check connection age distribution to see if connections cluster below firewall timeout\\nSELECT\\n  CASE\\n    WHEN DATEDIFF(SECOND, c.connect_time, GETDATE()) < 300 THEN '0-5min'\\n    WHEN DATEDIFF(SECOND, c.connect_time, GETDATE()) < 600 THEN '5-10min'\\n    WHEN DATEDIFF(SECOND, c.connect_time, GETDATE()) < 1800 THEN '10-30min'\\n    ELSE '30min+'\\n  END AS age_bucket,\\n  COUNT(*) AS connection_count\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_connections c\\nGROUP BY\\n  CASE\\n    WHEN DATEDIFF(SECOND, c.connect_time, GETDATE()) < 300 THEN '0-5min'\\n    WHEN DATEDIFF(SECOND, c.connect_time, GETDATE()) < 600 THEN '5-10min'\\n    WHEN DATEDIFF(SECOND, c.connect_time, GETDATE()) < 1800 THEN '10-30min'\\n    ELSE '30min+'\\n  END;", "description": "Identifies network-related wait accumulation and checks whether connection ages cluster below a threshold consistent with a firewall idle timeout. If no connections survive beyond 5-10 minutes despite a pool maxLifetime of 30 minutes, the firewall is dropping them first."}	{"description": "Read-only verification; no rollback needed."}	low	f	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.455884
2676	sqlserver	instruct	Set pool maxLifetime below firewall idle timeout and enable TCP keep-alive	{"parameters": {"jdbc_parameter": "socketKeepalive=true;socketTimeout=300", "aws_nat_recommendation": "maxLifetime = 300000 (5 min), keepalive interval = 60s", "on_prem_recommendation": "maxLifetime = 1800000 (30 min), keepalive interval = 120s", "azure_lb_recommendation": "maxLifetime = 210000 (3.5 min), keepalive interval = 60s"}, "description": "Two complementary approaches — implement both for defense in depth: (1) POOL EVICTION BEFORE FIREWALL TIMEOUT: Set pool maxLifetime to (firewall_idle_timeout_seconds - 30) * 1000 milliseconds. Example: AWS NAT 350s → maxLifetime=320000 (5.3 min). Azure LB 240s → maxLifetime=210000 (3.5 min). On-prem 3600s → maxLifetime=1800000 (30 min). For HikariCP: hikari.maxLifetime=<calculated_ms>. (2) TCP KEEP-ALIVE: Add 'socketKeepalive=true' to JDBC connection string for mssql-jdbc. For ADO.NET, TCP keepalive is OS-level: on the application server, run: netsh int tcp set global keepalivetime=60000 keepaliveinterval=5000 (60s idle before first keepalive, 5s between probes). For Linux app servers: sysctl -w net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time=60 net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_intvl=5 net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_probes=3"}	{"description": "Restore previous maxLifetime and remove socketKeepalive parameter. For OS-level keepalive: netsh int tcp set global keepalivetime=7200000 (Windows default)."}	low	f	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.455886
2677	sqlserver	configure	Configure SQL Server TCP settings to send keepalive probes	{"sql": "-- SQL Server does not natively send TCP keepalives in old versions.\\n-- Verify SQL Server version (2019+ supports TCP keepalive configuration):\\nSELECT @@VERSION;\\n\\n-- Check current SQL Server network configuration\\nSELECT registry_key, value_name, value_data\\nFROM sys.dm_server_registry\\nWHERE registry_key LIKE '%SuperSocketNetLib%'\\n  AND value_name IN ('Enabled', 'TcpKeepAlive', 'TcpPort');\\n\\n-- SQL Server TCP keep-alive behavior is controlled by the OS TCP stack.\\n-- On the SQL SERVER host, set OS keep-alive (run in elevated cmd/PowerShell):\\n-- netsh int tcp set global keepalivetime=120000   (2 minutes)\\n-- netsh int tcp set global keepaliveinterval=5000  (5 seconds)\\n-- Verify: netsh int tcp show global", "description": "Configures the SQL Server host OS TCP keepalive to send keepalive probes to clients, which prevents firewalls from dropping the connection during idle periods. This complements client-side keepalive configuration."}	{"description": "Restore default OS TCP keepalive: netsh int tcp set global keepalivetime=7200000 (Windows default of 2 hours)."}	low	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.455887
