3072	sqlserver	configure	Optimize network path and TCP settings for replication traffic	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"target_rtt_ms": "< 1 ms for same-datacenter synchronous replicas; > 5 ms RTT makes synchronous commit impractical", "recommended_network_speed": "10 GbE minimum; 25 GbE preferred for high log throughput"}, "description": "Before changing availability mode, attempt network optimization to reduce the RTT overhead of synchronous commit:\\n\\n1. Verify network speed: Replication endpoints should be on 10 GbE or faster dedicated interfaces. Confirm with network team.\\n2. Dedicate a VLAN for AG/mirroring traffic to avoid contention with application or backup traffic.\\n3. Optimize TCP settings on both primary and secondary Windows nodes:\\n   - Set TCP_NODELAY (disable Nagle algorithm) for SQL Server endpoint connections. SQL Server does this automatically for its own endpoints but verify with Wireshark if in doubt.\\n   - Review RSS (Receive Side Scaling) settings: `netsh int tcp show global`\\n   - Consider increasing TCP send/receive buffer sizes for high-throughput replication.\\n4. Check for jumbo frames (MTU 9000) on the replication VLAN — reduces framing overhead for large log writes.\\n5. On the SQL Server endpoint, verify LISTENER_PORT and ENDPOINT state:\\n   SELECT name, state_desc, type_desc, role_desc FROM sys.endpoints WHERE type_desc = 'DATABASE_MIRRORING';"}	{"sql": null, "description": "TCP and network configuration changes can be reverted by restoring previous MTU, RSS, and VLAN settings. SQL Server endpoint changes can be reverted via ALTER ENDPOINT."}	medium	t	t	04:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.505699
3893	sqlserver	verify	Verify thread creation succeeds and THREADPOOL waits stop	{"sql": "SELECT\\n  (SELECT max_workers_count FROM sys.dm_os_sys_info) AS max_workers,\\n  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sys.dm_os_workers) AS current_workers,\\n  (SELECT SUM(work_queue_count) FROM sys.dm_os_schedulers WHERE scheduler_id < 255) AS queued_requests,\\n  (SELECT wait_time_ms FROM sys.dm_os_wait_stats WHERE wait_type = 'THREADPOOL') AS threadpool_wait_ms;", "parameters": [], "description": "Confirms threads are being created successfully and no more THREADPOOL waits are occurring."}	null	low	f	t	00:01:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.579335
3074	sqlserver	verify	Measure current group commit efficiency via log write size and flush frequency	{"sql": "-- Log write characteristics — small avg_write_bytes = poor group commit coalescing\\nSELECT d.name,\\n  vfs.num_of_writes AS log_writes,\\n  vfs.num_of_bytes_written / 1048576.0 AS log_mb_written,\\n  vfs.num_of_bytes_written / NULLIF(vfs.num_of_writes, 0) AS avg_write_bytes,\\n  vfs.io_stall_write_ms / NULLIF(vfs.num_of_writes, 0) AS avg_write_ms\\nFROM sys.dm_io_virtual_file_stats(NULL, NULL) vfs\\nJOIN sys.master_files mf ON vfs.database_id = mf.database_id AND vfs.file_id = mf.file_id\\nJOIN sys.databases d ON vfs.database_id = d.database_id\\nWHERE mf.type = 1 AND d.database_id > 4 AND vfs.num_of_writes > 0\\nORDER BY avg_write_bytes ASC;\\n-- WRITELOG wait stats\\nSELECT wait_type, waiting_tasks_count,\\n  wait_time_ms / NULLIF(waiting_tasks_count, 0) AS avg_wait_ms\\nFROM sys.dm_os_wait_stats WHERE wait_type = 'WRITELOG';\\n-- Concurrent session count and transaction rate\\nSELECT\\n  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sys.dm_exec_sessions WHERE is_user_process = 1) AS active_sessions,\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE counter_name = 'Transactions/sec'\\n     AND object_name LIKE '%Databases%'\\n     AND instance_name = '_Total') AS transactions_per_sec,\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE counter_name = 'Batch Requests/sec'\\n     AND object_name LIKE '%SQL Statistics%') AS batch_requests_per_sec;", "parameters": {}, "description": "avg_write_bytes < 4,096 bytes combined with high WRITELOG waiting_tasks_count indicates each commit is being flushed individually (no coalescing). avg_write_bytes > 16,384 bytes indicates good coalescing is already occurring."}	null	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.5057
4031	sqlserver	verify	Verify statistics are current after bulk load	{"sql": "SELECT\\n  OBJECT_NAME(s.object_id) AS table_name,\\n  s.name AS stat_name,\\n  sp.last_updated,\\n  sp.modification_counter,\\n  CAST(sp.modification_counter AS FLOAT) / NULLIF(sp.rows, 0) * 100 AS mod_pct\\nFROM sys.stats s\\nCROSS APPLY sys.dm_db_stats_properties(s.object_id, s.stats_id) sp\\nWHERE sp.rows > 1000\\n  AND OBJECTPROPERTY(s.object_id, 'IsUserTable') = 1\\nORDER BY sp.modification_counter DESC;", "parameters": [], "description": "Confirms modification counters are near zero after statistics update, indicating all statistics reflect current data."}	null	low	f	t	00:01:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.614955
3075	sqlserver	instruct	Consolidate connections and increase concurrent transaction density for better natural coalescing	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"recommended_pool_min_size": "10-20 connections for OLTP applications", "target_concurrent_committers": "10+ concurrent sessions in active commit for natural group commit"}, "description": "If avg_write_bytes is small because there are too few concurrent committing sessions, consolidate application connections to increase the probability of concurrent commits:\\n\\n1. Implement or tune a connection pool: ensure the application uses a single shared pool with at least 10-20 concurrent connections for OLTP workloads. Isolated per-thread connection pools defeat group commit.\\n\\n2. Convert sequential commit loops to concurrent operations:\\n   - If a batch job processes rows sequentially, use parallel batches across multiple connections.\\n   - Producer-consumer patterns with a connection pool allow multiple writers to commit simultaneously.\\n\\n3. Use table-valued parameters or bulk INSERT to combine multiple logical writes into one larger transaction:\\n   - Instead of 100 single-row INSERTs each with their own COMMIT, use a single INSERT...VALUES (...),(...),...(...) for all rows.\\n\\n4. If using Service Broker or queuing, increase the dequeue batch size to process more messages per commit cycle.\\n\\n5. For read-heavy workloads with occasional writes, ensure read connections use READ COMMITTED SNAPSHOT (RCSI) to avoid reader-writer blocking, which artificially serializes commit timing."}	{"sql": null, "description": "Connection pool and application concurrency changes can be reverted by restoring the previous connection string and pool configuration."}	low	t	t	08:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.505701
