4925	sqlserver	execute	Disable suspicious Agent jobs referencing xp_cmdshell	{"sql": "-- Disable all Agent jobs that reference xp_cmdshell:\\nDECLARE @job_id UNIQUEIDENTIFIER;\\nDECLARE job_cursor CURSOR FOR\\n  SELECT DISTINCT j.job_id\\n  FROM msdb.dbo.sysjobs j\\n  JOIN msdb.dbo.sysjobsteps js ON j.job_id = js.job_id\\n  WHERE js.command LIKE '%xp_cmdshell%';\\nOPEN job_cursor;\\nFETCH NEXT FROM job_cursor INTO @job_id;\\nWHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0\\nBEGIN\\n  EXEC msdb.dbo.sp_update_job @job_id = @job_id, @enabled = 0;\\n  FETCH NEXT FROM job_cursor INTO @job_id;\\nEND;\\nCLOSE job_cursor;\\nDEALLOCATE job_cursor;", "parameters": [], "description": "Disables all Agent jobs that contain xp_cmdshell references. These should be reviewed by security team before being re-enabled or deleted."}	{"sql": "-- Re-enable specific legitimate jobs after review:\\n-- EXEC msdb.dbo.sp_update_job @job_name = 'JobName', @enabled = 1;", "description": "Re-enable individual jobs after security review confirms they are legitimate."}	medium	t	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.801021
4926	sqlserver	instruct	Investigate potential compromise and check for OS-level persistence	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "Engage the security team to investigate: (1) Review Windows Task Scheduler for tasks created by the SQL Server service account. (2) Check Windows services for any recently installed services by the SQL Server account. (3) Review the Windows registry for modifications by the SQL Server service account. (4) Check for any unauthorized user accounts created on the host. (5) Review Windows Event Logs for suspicious process creation events. (6) If compromise is confirmed, follow incident response procedures including forensic imaging before remediation."}	null	low	f	t	5 days	2026-02-24 13:10:27.801022
