5419	sqlserver	verify	Identify indexed PII columns and their query patterns	{"sql": "SELECT\\n  OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME(ic.object_id) AS schema_name,\\n  OBJECT_NAME(ic.object_id) AS table_name,\\n  COL_NAME(ic.object_id, ic.column_id) AS column_name,\\n  i.name AS index_name, i.type_desc AS index_type\\nFROM sys.index_columns ic\\nJOIN sys.indexes i ON ic.object_id = i.object_id AND ic.index_id = i.index_id\\nWHERE i.type > 0\\n  AND (COL_NAME(ic.object_id, ic.column_id) LIKE '%ssn%'\\n    OR COL_NAME(ic.object_id, ic.column_id) LIKE '%email%'\\n    OR COL_NAME(ic.object_id, ic.column_id) LIKE '%phone%'\\n    OR COL_NAME(ic.object_id, ic.column_id) LIKE '%national_id%');\\n\\nSELECT c.name AS column_name,\\n  OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME(c.object_id) AS schema_name,\\n  OBJECT_NAME(c.object_id) AS table_name,\\n  c.encryption_type_desc\\nFROM sys.columns c\\nWHERE c.encryption_type IS NOT NULL;", "parameters": [], "description": "Identifies indexed PII columns and existing encryption status."}	null	low	f	t	00:01:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.924616
5420	sqlserver	instruct	Apply deterministic encryption to searchable PII columns	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "1) Classify PII columns by query pattern: a) Equality search (WHERE email = @val): Use DETERMINISTIC encryption. Supports equality joins, GROUP BY, and indexing. b) Display only (SELECT ssn FROM ...): Use RANDOMIZED encryption. Stronger security but no server-side operations. c) Range search (WHERE dob BETWEEN ...): Cannot use Always Encrypted directly. Consider tokenization or application-level decryption. 2) Create CMK and CEK (see RC01 resolution for key setup). 3) For deterministic columns: a) The same plaintext always produces the same ciphertext (allows equality comparison). b) Indexes on the column continue to work for equality lookups. c) ALTER TABLE [dbo].[users] ALTER COLUMN [email] ADD ENCRYPTED WITH (ENCRYPTION_TYPE = DETERMINISTIC, ALGORITHM = 'AEAD_AES_256_CBC_HMAC_SHA_256', COLUMN_ENCRYPTION_KEY = [CEK_PII]). 4) Update application queries: a) Must use parameterized queries (sp_executesql or SqlCommand with parameters). b) The client driver encrypts parameter values before sending to the server. c) Equality searches work: WHERE email = @email_param. d) LIKE, ORDER BY, and range comparisons will NOT work on encrypted columns. 5) For columns that need LIKE searches: Consider storing a search hash alongside the encrypted value."}	{"sql": null, "description": "Remove encryption from affected columns if application compatibility issues arise."}	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.924977
