5454	sqlserver	instruct	Configure PII-safe error handling	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "1) Review custom error messages for PII patterns: a) Remove any message templates that include user data placeholders. b) Use generic messages: 'Input validation failed for field [field_name]' instead of 'Invalid SSN: [value]'. c) sp_altermessage to modify existing messages. 2) Application-level error handling: a) Configure applications to catch SQL exceptions and log only error numbers and generic descriptions. b) Never log the full exception message from SQL Server to application logs if it might contain query text. c) Use structured logging with PII-safe fields only. 3) SQL Server error log management: a) Increase error log rotation frequency: EXEC sp_cycle_errorlog (add to a nightly job). b) Restrict error log file access to DBAs only. c) Set the number of error log files to a reasonable number (6-12). 4) For RAISERROR/THROW in stored procedures: a) Never include parameter values in error messages. b) Use error codes and let the application look up user-friendly messages. c) Log details to a restricted audit table instead of RAISERROR."}	null	low	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.933949
5457	sqlserver	instruct	Replace broad DML audit with targeted security-only audit	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "1) Remove broad audit groups that capture SQL text: a) Remove BATCH_COMPLETED_GROUP (captures all SQL text). b) Remove DATABASE_OBJECT_ACCESS_GROUP if too broad. c) Replace with targeted groups: SUCCESSFUL_LOGIN_GROUP, FAILED_LOGIN_GROUP, LOGIN_CHANGE_PASSWORD_GROUP, SERVER_ROLE_MEMBER_CHANGE_GROUP, DATABASE_ROLE_MEMBER_CHANGE_GROUP, SCHEMA_OBJECT_PERMISSION_CHANGE_GROUP, BACKUP_RESTORE_GROUP. 2) For compliance that requires DML auditing on specific tables: a) Use database-level audit specs targeting only non-PII tables. b) Or audit PII tables with SCHEMA_OBJECT_ACCESS_GROUP but enable FORCED PARAMETERIZATION so literals are replaced with parameters. 3) Restrict audit file access: a) Move audit files to a restricted directory. b) Encrypt audit files using BitLocker or EFS. c) Grant access only to the security team. 4) Set audit file retention: a) MAX_FILES with MAX_ROLLOVER_FILES to limit accumulation. b) Archive and encrypt old audit files."}	{"sql": null, "description": "Re-add the broader audit groups if compliance requires them."}	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.933952
