830	sqlserver	execute	Disable NTFS compression and encryption on database file volumes	{"sql": "-- Verify current file locations\\nSELECT name, physical_name, type_desc, state_desc\\nFROM sys.master_files\\nWHERE database_id > 4\\nORDER BY database_id, type;\\n\\n-- After confirming file paths, run from Command Prompt (as Administrator):\\n-- compact /u /s /a /q \\"<data_volume_path>\\\\*.mdf\\" -- decompress MDF files\\n-- compact /u /s /a /q \\"<data_volume_path>\\\\*.ldf\\" -- decompress LDF files\\n-- cipher /d \\"<data_volume_path>\\" -- decrypt volume if EFS encrypted\\n\\n-- Verify SQL Server sector size alignment (must match physical sector size):\\nSELECT vs.volume_mount_point, vs.logical_sector_size, vs.physical_sector_size\\nFROM sys.master_files mf\\nCROSS APPLY sys.dm_os_volume_stats(mf.database_id, mf.file_id) vs\\nWHERE mf.database_id > 4;", "parameters": ["data_volume_path: path to the volume hosting SQL Server data files"], "description": "SQL Server does not support NTFS compressed or EFS-encrypted volumes for database files. Compression causes sector size mismatches and I/O errors. Decompress files and volumes hosting database files."}	null	medium	t	f	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.192357
837	sqlserver	execute	Restore multi-user mode and take clean backup	{"sql": "-- Return database to multi-user access\\nALTER DATABASE [<database_name>] SET ONLINE;\\nALTER DATABASE [<database_name>] SET MULTI_USER;\\n\\n-- Confirm database is accessible\\nSELECT name, state_desc, user_access_desc FROM sys.databases WHERE name = '<database_name>';\\n\\n-- Take immediate full backup of repaired database\\nBACKUP DATABASE [<database_name>]\\nTO DISK = '<backup_path>\\\\<database_name>_post_repair_' + CONVERT(VARCHAR, GETDATE(), 112) + '.bak'\\nWITH COMPRESSION, STATS = 10;\\n\\n-- Remove from suspect_pages if repair succeeded\\nDELETE FROM msdb.dbo.suspect_pages WHERE database_id = DB_ID('<database_name>');", "parameters": ["database_name: repaired database name", "backup_path: destination directory for the post-repair backup"], "description": "Return the database to normal multi-user access, take an immediate full backup of the repaired state, and clean up the suspect_pages table."}	null	medium	f	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.192361
849	sqlserver	instruct	Grant Write/Modify permission to backup target network share	{"sql": null, "parameters": ["share_path: UNC path of the backup target (e.g. \\\\\\\\fileserver\\\\sqlbackups)", "service_account: Windows identity from step 1"], "description": "On the file server hosting the backup share: (1) Open Computer Management > Shared Folders > Shares. (2) Right-click the share > Properties > Share Permissions: grant the SQL Agent service account (or proxy account) Change permission. (3) In NTFS Security tab, grant Modify on the underlying folder. If using DFS, apply permissions at the DFS namespace target folder. Validate with: net use \\\\\\\\<server>\\\\<share> from the SQL Server host using the service account credentials."}	null	medium	t	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.19237
853	sqlserver	instruct	Verify network share availability and re-mount if disconnected	{"sql": null, "parameters": ["unc_path: the path identified in step 1"], "description": "From the SQL Server host, run the following checks as the SQL Agent service account (or use PowerShell with -Credential): (1) Test-Path '\\\\\\\\<server>\\\\<share>' — should return True. (2) If False: confirm the file server is online; verify the share exists with Get-SmbShare on the file server; check firewall rules (port 445 TCP); re-establish DFS replication if applicable. (3) If the share was permanently relocated, obtain the new UNC path and proceed to the next step to update job configurations."}	null	medium	t	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.192372
862	sqlserver	configure	Limit MAXDOP and add BUFFERCOUNT/MAXTRANSFERSIZE to backup commands to reduce I/O pressure	{"sql": "-- Add resource-limiting options to BACKUP commands in job steps\\n-- Example: limit backup to 2 CPU threads and conservative buffer settings\\nUSE msdb;\\nEXEC dbo.sp_update_jobstep\\n  @job_name = N'<BackupJobName>',\\n  @step_id  = 1,\\n  @command  = N'BACKUP DATABASE [<DatabaseName>]\\n  TO DISK = N''<BackupPath>\\\\<DatabaseName>.bak''\\n  WITH COMPRESSION, STATS = 10,\\n    MAXTRANSFERSIZE = 1048576,  -- 1 MB transfers\\n    BUFFERCOUNT = 15,\\n    BLOCKSIZE = 65536;';", "parameters": ["BackupJobName: name of the backup job", "DatabaseName: target database", "BackupPath: verified backup directory"], "description": "Adding MAXTRANSFERSIZE and BUFFERCOUNT controls I/O burst size. For index rebuilds, set MAXDOP via sp_configure or USE HINT if needed."}	{"sql": "-- Revert job step to original command using sp_update_jobstep"}	low	t	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.192379
