2597	sqlserver	instruct	Audit and fix all Connection open/close call sites to use guaranteed-close patterns	{"sql": "-- No SQL Server change. Provide development team with these code patterns:\\n\\n-- C# / .NET: using statement guarantees Dispose() (which calls Close())\\n-- BEFORE (leak): SqlConnection conn = new SqlConnection(cs); conn.Open(); /* no guarantee of close */\\n-- AFTER (safe):\\n-- using (SqlConnection conn = new SqlConnection(cs))\\n-- using (SqlCommand cmd = new SqlCommand(sql, conn))\\n-- { conn.Open(); var result = cmd.ExecuteScalar(); }\\n\\n-- Java: try-with-resources guarantees close()\\n-- AFTER (safe):\\n-- try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection();\\n--      PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {\\n--   return ps.executeQuery();\\n-- } // conn.close() called automatically\\n\\n-- Python: context manager guarantees close()\\n-- with engine.connect() as conn:  # SQLAlchemy\\n--     result = conn.execute(text(sql))\\n-- # conn.__exit__ returns connection to pool\\n\\n-- Search for leaked patterns in the codebase:\\n-- grep -rn \\"new SqlConnection\\" --include='*.cs' . | grep -v 'using'\\n-- grep -rn \\"\\\\.getConnection()\\" --include='*.java' . | grep -v 'try ('\\n-- grep -rn \\"engine.connect()\\" --include='*.py' . | grep -v 'with '", "parameters": {}, "description": "Instructs the development team to audit all database connection acquisition points and convert any that do not use language-native guaranteed-close constructs. Provides grep patterns to locate vulnerable call sites in the codebase."}	null	low	f	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.44328
2600	sqlserver	instruct	Add try-finally blocks to all database call sites to guarantee rollback and connection close on exception	{"sql": "-- No SQL Server change. Provide development team with these code patterns:\\n\\n-- C# / .NET: using + explicit rollback in catch\\n-- using (SqlConnection conn = new SqlConnection(cs))\\n-- using (SqlTransaction txn = conn.BeginTransaction())\\n-- {\\n--   try {\\n--     conn.Open();\\n--     // ... execute SQL ...\\n--     txn.Commit();\\n--   } catch (Exception ex) {\\n--     txn.Rollback();  // explicit rollback\\n--     throw;           // re-throw to caller\\n--   }\\n-- }  // conn.Dispose() called here — returns to pool\\n\\n-- Java: try-with-resources for connection + explicit rollback\\n-- try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection()) {\\n--   conn.setAutoCommit(false);\\n--   try {\\n--     // ... execute SQL ...\\n--     conn.commit();\\n--   } catch (SQLException e) {\\n--     conn.rollback();  // explicit rollback on exception\\n--     throw e;\\n--   }\\n-- }  // conn.close() called automatically by try-with-resources\\n\\n-- Python: SQLAlchemy session/transaction context manager\\n-- with Session(engine) as session:\\n--   with session.begin():  # auto-commit or rollback on exit\\n--     session.execute(stmt)\\n-- # Both session and transaction are cleaned up even on exception\\n\\n-- Search for unguarded transaction patterns:\\n-- grep -rn \\"BeginTransaction\\" --include='*.cs' . | grep -v 'using\\\\|try'\\n-- grep -rn \\"setAutoCommit(false)\\" --include='*.java' . | grep -B5 -A10 .  # check for rollback in catch", "parameters": {}, "description": "Instructs developers to audit all transaction-using code paths to ensure that every exception path calls rollback() before the connection goes out of scope, and that the connection is closed in a finally block or equivalent. Provides concrete patterns for common frameworks."}	null	low	f	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.444602
2698	sqlserver	configure	Fix identified root cause: auth, network, or pool validation settings	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"testOnBorrow": false, "connectionTestQuery": null, "tcp_keepalive_time_sec": 60}, "description": "Choose the appropriate fix based on Step 1 findings:\\n\\n(A) Login failure / auth: Reset service account password, fix SPN registration (setspn -A MSSQLSvc/host:port domain\\\\account), or switch to SQL Auth for the application pool login.\\n\\n(B) Network instability / dropped idle connections: Configure TCP keepalive on the OS (net.ipv4.tcp_keepalive_time=60 on Linux, or equivalent registry key on Windows). On the SQL Server side, set the 'remote login timeout' and verify firewall idle connection timeout settings exceed pool connection lifetime.\\n\\n(C) Unnecessary test-on-borrow: Disable connection validation in the pool config (e.g., HikariCP: connectionTestQuery=null, testOnBorrow=false) and rely instead on SQL Server's built-in connection reset (sp_reset_connection) which is always safe."}	{"description": "Revert pool configuration changes or SPN changes if connection failures increase after the change."}	medium	t	t	01:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.457064
2722	sqlserver	instruct	Migrate non-production databases to a dedicated non-production SQL Server instance	{"sql": "-- Generate a list of non-production databases to detach or backup/restore:\\nSELECT name, database_id, state_desc\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE name LIKE '%test%' OR name LIKE '%dev%'\\n  OR name LIKE '%staging%' OR name LIKE '%qa%'\\n  OR name LIKE '%sandbox%' OR name LIKE '%uat%'\\n  AND database_id > 4;  -- Exclude system databases\\n\\n-- For each identified database, on the target non-production instance:\\n-- 1. BACKUP DATABASE [dbname] TO DISK = 'path\\\\dbname.bak';\\n-- 2. Restore on non-production server.\\n-- 3. Update all application connection strings to point to non-production server.\\n-- 4. Once applications are re-pointed and verified, DROP DATABASE [dbname] on production.", "parameters": {}, "description": "Move non-production databases off the production server. Use backup/restore rather than detach/attach to maintain production availability during migration. Update application configuration and test connectivity before dropping from production."}	{"sql": "-- If migration fails, restore non-production databases from backup on original server.\\n-- Revert application connection strings to production server.", "description": "Restore from backup on the original server and revert connection string changes if non-production migration causes application issues."}	medium	t	t	08:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.457077
3005	sqlserver	execute	Distribute database files across multiple controllers using filegroups	{"sql": "-- After adding a second controller and presenting new LUN:\\n\\n-- Create a new filegroup for data files on the new controller\\nALTER DATABASE [<database_name>]\\n  ADD FILEGROUP [FG_CONTROLLER2];\\n\\n-- Add data files on the new controller\\nALTER DATABASE [<database_name>]\\n  ADD FILE (\\n    NAME = N'<db>_data2',\\n    FILENAME = N'<new_controller_drive>:\\\\SQLData\\\\<db>_data2.ndf',\\n    SIZE = 10240 MB,\\n    MAXSIZE = UNLIMITED,\\n    FILEGROWTH = 1024 MB\\n  ) TO FILEGROUP [FG_CONTROLLER2];\\n\\n-- Move the most I/O-intensive table to the new filegroup:\\n-- (Rebuild moves table to new filegroup)\\nCREATE CLUSTERED INDEX [CIX_<table>_id] ON [dbo].[<table>]([id])\\nWITH (DROP_EXISTING = ON, ONLINE = ON)\\nON [FG_CONTROLLER2];\\n\\n-- Verify file distribution\\nSELECT f.name, f.physical_name, fg.name AS filegroup_name\\nFROM sys.database_files f\\nJOIN sys.filegroups fg ON f.data_space_id = fg.data_space_id;", "parameters": {}, "description": "Creates a second filegroup on the new controller's LUN and redistributes the most I/O-heavy tables by rebuilding their clustered indexes onto the new filegroup, splitting I/O load across controllers."}	{"sql": "-- Move table back to original filegroup:\\nCREATE CLUSTERED INDEX [CIX_<table>_id] ON [dbo].[<table>]([id])\\nWITH (DROP_EXISTING = ON, ONLINE = ON)\\nON [PRIMARY];\\n\\n-- Remove the filegroup and file if needed:\\nALTER DATABASE [<database_name>] REMOVE FILE [<db>_data2];\\nALTER DATABASE [<database_name>] REMOVE FILEGROUP [FG_CONTROLLER2];", "description": "Reverts table to original filegroup if the new controller causes issues."}	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.497902
3027	sqlserver	execute	Enable lock pages in memory and verify read-ahead is not suppressed by LPIM absence	{"sql": "-- Check if Lock Pages in Memory is enabled\\nSELECT sql_memory_model_desc\\nFROM sys.dm_os_sys_info;\\n-- 'LOCK_PAGES' = enabled; 'CONVENTIONAL' = not enabled\\n\\n-- Check for memory pressure signals that suppress read-ahead\\nSELECT\\n  cntr_value AS lazy_writes_per_sec\\nFROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\nWHERE object_name LIKE '%Buffer Manager%'\\n  AND counter_name = 'Lazy writes/sec';\\n-- Lazy writes > 20/sec = memory pressure suppressing read-ahead effectiveness", "parameters": {"lpim_gpo_path": "Local Security Policy > Local Policies > User Rights Assignment > Lock pages in memory", "service_account": "Add the SQL Server service account (e.g., NT SERVICE\\\\MSSQLSERVER)"}, "description": "When SQL Server buffer pool memory is being paged out by Windows (sql_memory_model_desc = CONVENTIONAL), read-ahead pages are evicted before they can be consumed. Enable Lock Pages in Memory (LPIM) via Local Security Policy > User Rights Assignment > Lock pages in memory for the SQL Server service account. This prevents buffer pool paging. Requires SQL Server service restart to activate."}	{"description": "Remove the SQL Server service account from the 'Lock pages in memory' Local Security Policy right. Restart SQL Server service. Note: LPIM prevents Windows from reclaiming SQL buffer pool memory, which could stress the OS if SQL max server memory is misconfigured."}	low	t	t	00:20:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.500844
3059	sqlserver	verify	Measure peak log usage and current autogrowth configuration	{"sql": "-- Current log file size and growth configuration\\nSELECT d.name,\\n  mf.logical_name,\\n  mf.physical_name,\\n  mf.size * 8 / 1024 AS current_size_mb,\\n  CASE mf.is_percent_growth\\n    WHEN 1 THEN CAST(mf.growth AS VARCHAR) + '% (PERCENT - DANGEROUS)'\\n    ELSE CAST(mf.growth * 8 / 1024 AS VARCHAR) + ' MB (fixed)'\\n  END AS autogrowth_setting,\\n  mf.max_size,\\n  mf.is_percent_growth\\nFROM sys.databases d\\nJOIN sys.master_files mf ON d.database_id = mf.database_id\\nWHERE mf.type = 1 AND d.database_id > 4\\nORDER BY mf.size ASC;\\n-- Current log space usage\\nSELECT d.name,\\n  ls.total_log_size_in_bytes / 1048576 AS total_log_mb,\\n  ls.used_log_space_in_bytes / 1048576 AS used_log_mb,\\n  ls.used_log_space_in_percent\\nFROM sys.dm_db_log_space_usage ls\\nJOIN sys.databases d ON ls.database_id = d.database_id\\nWHERE d.database_id > 4\\nORDER BY ls.used_log_space_in_percent DESC;\\n-- Check SQL Server error log for autogrowth events (requires xp_cmdshell or SSMS)\\n-- Alternatively, query the default trace:\\nSELECT TE.name AS EventName, T.StartTime, T.DatabaseName,\\n  T.Filename, T.IntegerData * 8 / 1024 AS GrowthMB, T.Duration / 1000 AS DurationMs\\nFROM sys.fn_trace_gettable(CONVERT(NVARCHAR(260),\\n  (SELECT REVERSE(SUBSTRING(REVERSE(path), CHARINDEX('\\\\', REVERSE(path)), 260))\\n   + 'log.trc'\\n   FROM sys.traces WHERE is_default = 1)), DEFAULT) T\\nJOIN sys.trace_events TE ON T.EventClass = TE.trace_event_id\\nWHERE TE.name = 'Log File Auto Grow'\\nORDER BY T.StartTime DESC;", "parameters": {}, "description": "Determine the current log file size, growth settings, peak usage percentage, and historical autogrowth frequency from the default trace. This data drives the pre-sizing target calculation."}	null	low	f	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.505689
5624	postgresql	verify	Verify backup can be decrypted with correct key	{"sql": "SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_database_size(current_database())) AS db_size;", "parameters": [], "description": "Provides database size context for backup verification"}	\N	low	f	t	seconds	2026-02-25 08:59:43.77256
3061	sqlserver	verify	Confirm no autogrowth events occur during next peak workload window	{"sql": "-- Re-check log space usage after pre-sizing\\nSELECT d.name,\\n  ls.total_log_size_in_bytes / 1048576 AS total_log_mb,\\n  ls.used_log_space_in_bytes / 1048576 AS used_log_mb,\\n  ls.used_log_space_in_percent\\nFROM sys.dm_db_log_space_usage ls\\nJOIN sys.databases d ON ls.database_id = d.database_id\\nWHERE d.database_id > 4\\nORDER BY ls.used_log_space_in_percent DESC;\\n-- Verify no new autogrowth events in default trace since the change\\nSELECT TE.name, T.StartTime, T.DatabaseName,\\n  T.IntegerData * 8 / 1024 AS GrowthMB, T.Duration / 1000 AS DurationMs\\nFROM sys.fn_trace_gettable(CONVERT(NVARCHAR(260),\\n  (SELECT REVERSE(SUBSTRING(REVERSE(path), CHARINDEX('\\\\', REVERSE(path)), 260))\\n   + 'log.trc'\\n   FROM sys.traces WHERE is_default = 1)), DEFAULT) T\\nJOIN sys.trace_events TE ON T.EventClass = TE.trace_event_id\\nWHERE TE.name = 'Log File Auto Grow'\\n  AND T.StartTime > DATEADD(HOUR, -24, GETDATE())\\nORDER BY T.StartTime DESC;", "parameters": {}, "description": "After the next peak workload cycle, verify that log used percentage has not approached 100% and that no new autogrowth events have been recorded in the default trace."}	null	low	f	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.505691
3063	sqlserver	execute	Apply the appropriate resolution for the detected log_reuse_wait_desc	{"sql": "-- ===== CASE 1: LOG_BACKUP =====\\n-- Take a transaction log backup immediately:\\nBACKUP LOG [<DatabaseName>]\\n  TO DISK = N'<BackupPath>\\\\<DatabaseName>_log_emergency_' +\\n    REPLACE(CONVERT(VARCHAR, GETDATE(), 120), ':', '') + '.bak'\\n  WITH COMPRESSION, STATS = 10;\\n\\n-- ===== CASE 2: ACTIVE_TRANSACTION =====\\n-- Identify the blocking long-running transaction:\\nSELECT t.transaction_id, t.transaction_begin_time,\\n  DATEDIFF(MINUTE, t.transaction_begin_time, GETDATE()) AS open_minutes,\\n  dt.database_transaction_log_bytes_used / 1048576 AS log_mb,\\n  s.session_id, s.login_name, s.host_name, s.program_name,\\n  r.command, r.status, r.blocking_session_id\\nFROM sys.dm_tran_active_transactions t\\nJOIN sys.dm_tran_database_transactions dt ON t.transaction_id = dt.transaction_id\\nJOIN sys.dm_tran_session_transactions st ON t.transaction_id = st.transaction_id\\nJOIN sys.dm_exec_sessions s ON st.session_id = s.session_id\\nLEFT JOIN sys.dm_exec_requests r ON s.session_id = r.session_id\\nWHERE s.is_user_process = 1\\nORDER BY t.transaction_begin_time ASC;\\n-- Kill the session after confirming it is safe to roll back:\\n-- KILL <session_id>;  -- Uncomment only after DBA review\\n\\n-- ===== CASE 3: REPLICATION =====\\n-- Check distribution queue depth:\\nSELECT * FROM distribution.dbo.MSdistribution_status;\\n-- If Distribution Agent is stopped, restart it via SQL Server Agent or:\\n-- EXEC sp_startrootcauseation_snapshot @rootcauseation = '<rootcauseationName>';\\n-- If replication is no longer in use, mark database as non-replicated:\\n-- EXEC sp_removedbreplication '<DatabaseName>';\\n\\n-- ===== CASE 4: AVAILABILITY_REPLICA =====\\n-- Check AG secondary redo lag:\\nSELECT ar.replica_server_name, drs.synchronization_state_desc,\\n  drs.log_send_queue_size / 1024 AS send_queue_kb,\\n  drs.redo_queue_size / 1024 AS redo_queue_kb,\\n  drs.redo_rate / 1024 AS redo_rate_kbps\\nFROM sys.availability_replicas ar\\nJOIN sys.dm_hadr_database_replica_states drs ON ar.replica_id = drs.replica_id\\nWHERE drs.is_primary_replica = 0;\\n-- If secondary is severely lagged, consider temporarily switching to ASYNCHRONOUS_COMMIT:\\n-- ALTER AVAILABILITY GROUP [<AGName>]\\n--   MODIFY REPLICA ON '<SecondaryServerName>'\\n--   WITH (AVAILABILITY_MODE = ASYNCHRONOUS_COMMIT);\\n-- Revert after secondary catches up.", "parameters": {"BackupPath": "Path to backup destination with sufficient free space", "DatabaseName": "Name of the affected database"}, "description": "Apply the appropriate fix for the detected truncation blocker. LOG_BACKUP: immediate emergency log backup. ACTIVE_TRANSACTION: identify and kill or await completion of the oldest open transaction. REPLICATION: restart Distribution Agent or remove stale replication. AVAILABILITY_REPLICA: address secondary redo lag or temporarily relax commit mode."}	{"sql": "-- For AVAILABILITY_REPLICA availability mode change, revert back to synchronous:\\n-- ALTER AVAILABILITY GROUP [<AGName>]\\n--   MODIFY REPLICA ON '<SecondaryServerName>'\\n--   WITH (AVAILABILITY_MODE = SYNCHRONOUS_COMMIT);", "description": "Revert any temporary availability mode changes after secondary redo lag has been resolved."}	medium	t	t	01:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.505693
3168	sqlserver	execute	Add tempdb data files to match CPU count (up to 8)	{"sql": "-- Run this block to add files; adjust @target_files as needed (min(8, logical_cpu_count))\\n-- Replace <tempdb_path> with the actual path used by existing tempdb files\\nDECLARE @current_files INT = (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sys.master_files WHERE database_id = 2 AND type = 0);\\nDECLARE @target_files INT = (SELECT MIN(v) FROM (VALUES (8), ((SELECT cpu_count FROM sys.dm_os_sys_info))) AS t(v));\\nDECLARE @file_size_mb INT = (SELECT TOP 1 size * 8 / 1024 FROM sys.master_files WHERE database_id = 2 AND type = 0 ORDER BY file_id);\\nDECLARE @data_path NVARCHAR(512) = (SELECT LEFT(physical_name, LEN(physical_name) - CHARINDEX('\\\\', REVERSE(physical_name))) FROM sys.master_files WHERE database_id = 2 AND type = 0 AND file_id = 1);\\nDECLARE @i INT = @current_files + 1;\\nDECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(MAX);\\nWHILE @i <= @target_files\\nBEGIN\\n  SET @sql = 'ALTER DATABASE tempdb ADD FILE (NAME = N''tempdev' + CAST(@i AS NVARCHAR) + ''', '\\n    + 'FILENAME = N''' + @data_path + '\\\\tempdev' + CAST(@i AS NVARCHAR) + '.ndf'', '\\n    + 'SIZE = ' + CAST(@file_size_mb AS NVARCHAR) + 'MB, '\\n    + 'FILEGROWTH = 64MB);';\\n  EXEC sp_executesql @sql;\\n  SET @i = @i + 1;\\nEND;\\nSELECT file_id, physical_name, size * 8 / 1024 AS size_mb FROM sys.master_files WHERE database_id = 2 AND type = 0 ORDER BY file_id;", "parameters": {"autogrowth": "64MB fixed", "initial_size": "Match existing tempdb primary file size", "target_files": "min(8, logical_cpu_count)"}, "description": "Adds tempdb data files so the total count equals min(8, logical CPU count). All files are sized to match the existing primary tempdb file to enable equal round-robin allocation. Paths are inferred from the existing tempdb file location."}	{"sql": "-- Remove added files if rollback is needed (replace N with added file numbers)\\n-- ALTER DATABASE tempdb REMOVE FILE tempdev2;\\n-- ALTER DATABASE tempdb REMOVE FILE tempdev3;\\n-- ... repeat for each added file\\nSELECT file_id, name, physical_name FROM sys.master_files WHERE database_id = 2 AND type = 0 ORDER BY file_id;", "description": "Remove each added tempdb file. Note: tempdb files cannot be removed while data is present; a SQL Server restart may be required."}	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.519918
31699	mariadb	configure	mysql_configure_gr_ssl	{"sql": "SET GLOBAL group_replication_ssl_mode = 'REQUIRED'; SET GLOBAL group_replication_recovery_ssl_ca = '/path/to/ca.pem'; SET GLOBAL group_replication_recovery_ssl_cert = '/path/to/server-cert.pem'; SET GLOBAL group_replication_recovery_ssl_key = '/path/to/server-key.pem';", "parameters": [], "description": "Configure consistent SSL certificates for Group Replication on this node"}	\N	medium	t	t	seconds	2026-05-11 22:17:39.229791
