4397	sqlserver	execute	Enable login auditing and create dormancy detection report	{"sql": "-- Step 1: Create server audit for login tracking\\nIF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.server_audits WHERE name = 'DormancyTrackingAudit')\\nBEGIN\\n  CREATE SERVER AUDIT [DormancyTrackingAudit] TO FILE (\\n    FILEPATH = 'C:\\\\SQLAudit\\\\', MAXSIZE = 500 MB, MAX_ROLLOVER_FILES = 30\\n  ) WITH (ON_FAILURE = CONTINUE);\\nEND\\nALTER SERVER AUDIT [DormancyTrackingAudit] WITH (STATE = ON);\\n\\nIF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.server_audit_specifications WHERE name = 'DormancyTrackingSpec')\\nBEGIN\\n  CREATE SERVER AUDIT SPECIFICATION [DormancyTrackingSpec]\\n    FOR SERVER AUDIT [DormancyTrackingAudit]\\n    ADD (SUCCESSFUL_LOGIN_GROUP),\\n    ADD (FAILED_LOGIN_GROUP),\\n    ADD (LOGIN_CHANGE_PASSWORD_GROUP)\\n    WITH (STATE = ON);\\nEND\\n\\n-- Step 2: Generate dormancy report\\nSELECT sp.name, sp.type_desc, sp.create_date, sp.modify_date,\\n  sp.is_disabled,\\n  (SELECT MAX(login_time) FROM sys.dm_exec_sessions WHERE login_name = sp.name) AS last_session,\\n  DATEDIFF(DAY, sp.modify_date, GETDATE()) AS days_since_modified,\\n  CASE WHEN sp.type = 'S'\\n    THEN CAST(LOGINPROPERTY(sp.name, 'PasswordLastSetTime') AS datetime)\\n    ELSE NULL END AS password_last_set\\nFROM sys.server_principals sp\\nWHERE sp.type IN ('S', 'U', 'G')\\n  AND sp.is_disabled = 0\\n  AND sp.name NOT LIKE '##%'\\n  AND sp.name NOT LIKE 'NT %'\\n  AND sp.name <> 'sa'\\n  AND (SELECT MAX(login_time) FROM sys.dm_exec_sessions WHERE login_name = sp.name) IS NULL\\nORDER BY sp.modify_date ASC;", "parameters": ["audit_file_path"], "description": "Creates login tracking audit and generates a dormancy report showing all enabled accounts with no current sessions."}	null	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.735894
4459	sqlserver	instruct	Migrate applications to Windows Authentication and secure remaining SQL logins	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "For each application using SQL Authentication: (1) Create a Windows AD service account and assign appropriate SPNs. (2) Create a Windows login: CREATE LOGIN [DOMAIN\\\\ServiceAccount] FROM WINDOWS. (3) Grant equivalent permissions to the Windows login. (4) Update application connection strings to use Integrated Security=True. (5) Test connectivity. (6) Disable the old SQL login: ALTER LOGIN [old_sql_login] DISABLE. For SQL logins that cannot migrate: enforce strong passwords with CHECK_POLICY = ON, CHECK_EXPIRATION = ON."}	null	medium	t	t	08:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.738007
4413	sqlserver	instruct	Migrate SQL logins to Windows Auth and configure AD-based lifecycle	{"sql": null, "parameters": ["domain_name"], "description": "Implement AD integration for account lifecycle: (1) For each user with a SQL Auth login who also has an AD account: CREATE LOGIN [DOMAIN\\\\username] FROM WINDOWS; then map their database users to the new Windows login and disable the SQL login. (2) For Windows logins already in use, verify they resolve against AD: use xp_logininfo 'DOMAIN\\\\username' to check validity. Any login returning an error may belong to a deleted AD account. (3) Configure a SQL Agent job that runs weekly: query sys.server_principals for Windows logins (type IN ('U','G')), call xp_logininfo for each, and flag/disable any that fail (indicating deleted AD accounts). (4) For service accounts, use Group Managed Service Accounts (gMSA) where possible: CREATE LOGIN [DOMAIN\\\\gMSA_account$] FROM WINDOWS; gMSA passwords are managed by AD automatically. (5) Integrate with HR provisioning: configure the Identity Management system to trigger SQL Server account creation/removal via a PowerShell webhook or SCIM adapter."}	null	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.742015
4415	sqlserver	verify	Verify orphaned Windows logins have been disabled	{"sql": "SELECT sp.name, sp.type_desc, sp.is_disabled,\\n  sp.create_date, sp.modify_date,\\n  DATEDIFF(DAY, sp.modify_date, GETDATE()) AS days_since_modified\\nFROM sys.server_principals sp\\nWHERE sp.type IN ('U', 'G')\\n  AND sp.name NOT LIKE 'NT %'\\n  AND sp.name NOT LIKE 'BUILTIN\\\\%'\\nORDER BY sp.is_disabled, sp.modify_date DESC;", "parameters": [], "description": "Lists all Windows logins showing which are enabled and disabled. All orphaned AD accounts should now be disabled."}	null	low	f	t	00:01:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.742487
4425	sqlserver	execute	Create login audit and dormancy detection job	{"sql": "-- Step 1: Create server audit for comprehensive login tracking\\nIF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.server_audits WHERE name = 'DormancyDetectionAudit')\\nBEGIN\\n  CREATE SERVER AUDIT [DormancyDetectionAudit] TO FILE (\\n    FILEPATH = 'C:\\\\SQLAudit\\\\', MAXSIZE = 500 MB, MAX_ROLLOVER_FILES = 30\\n  ) WITH (ON_FAILURE = CONTINUE);\\nEND\\nALTER SERVER AUDIT [DormancyDetectionAudit] WITH (STATE = ON);\\n\\nIF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.server_audit_specifications WHERE name = 'DormancyDetectionSpec')\\nBEGIN\\n  CREATE SERVER AUDIT SPECIFICATION [DormancyDetectionSpec]\\n    FOR SERVER AUDIT [DormancyDetectionAudit]\\n    ADD (SUCCESSFUL_LOGIN_GROUP),\\n    ADD (FAILED_LOGIN_GROUP),\\n    ADD (LOGOUT_GROUP)\\n    WITH (STATE = ON);\\nEND\\n\\n-- Step 2: Create Extended Events session for real-time login tracking\\nIF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.server_event_sessions WHERE name = 'LoginTracking')\\nBEGIN\\n  CREATE EVENT SESSION [LoginTracking] ON SERVER\\n    ADD EVENT sqlserver.login (ACTION (sqlserver.client_hostname, sqlserver.username)),\\n    ADD EVENT sqlserver.logout (ACTION (sqlserver.client_hostname, sqlserver.username))\\n    ADD TARGET package0.event_file (SET filename = N'C:\\\\SQLAudit\\\\LoginTracking.xel', max_file_size = 100)\\n    WITH (STARTUP_STATE = ON, MAX_DISPATCH_LATENCY = 30 SECONDS);\\n  ALTER EVENT SESSION [LoginTracking] ON SERVER STATE = START;\\nEND\\n\\n-- Step 3: Generate dormancy report\\nSELECT sp.name, sp.type_desc, sp.create_date,\\n  DATEDIFF(DAY, sp.modify_date, GETDATE()) AS days_since_modified,\\n  (SELECT MAX(login_time) FROM sys.dm_exec_sessions WHERE login_name = sp.name) AS last_session,\\n  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sys.server_role_members srm WHERE srm.member_principal_id = sp.principal_id) AS server_roles,\\n  CASE WHEN DATEDIFF(DAY, sp.modify_date, GETDATE()) > 90 THEN 'DISABLE'\\n       WHEN DATEDIFF(DAY, sp.modify_date, GETDATE()) > 30 THEN 'INVESTIGATE'\\n       ELSE 'OK' END AS recommended_action\\nFROM sys.server_principals sp\\nWHERE sp.type IN ('S', 'U', 'G')\\n  AND sp.is_disabled = 0\\n  AND sp.name NOT LIKE '##%' AND sp.name NOT LIKE 'NT %' AND sp.name <> 'sa'\\n  AND (SELECT MAX(login_time) FROM sys.dm_exec_sessions WHERE login_name = sp.name) IS NULL\\nORDER BY DATEDIFF(DAY, sp.modify_date, GETDATE()) DESC;", "parameters": ["audit_file_path"], "description": "Creates SQL Server Audit, Extended Events session for login tracking, and generates a dormancy report with recommended actions."}	null	low	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.745591
4429	sqlserver	execute	Create comprehensive login audit with historical retention	{"sql": "-- Step 1: Create server audit with extended retention\\nIF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.server_audits WHERE name = 'ComprehensiveLoginAudit')\\nBEGIN\\n  CREATE SERVER AUDIT [ComprehensiveLoginAudit] TO FILE (\\n    FILEPATH = 'C:\\\\SQLAudit\\\\LoginHistory\\\\',\\n    MAXSIZE = 1 GB,\\n    MAX_ROLLOVER_FILES = 365  -- 1 year of audit data\\n  ) WITH (ON_FAILURE = CONTINUE, QUEUE_DELAY = 1000);\\nEND\\nALTER SERVER AUDIT [ComprehensiveLoginAudit] WITH (STATE = ON);\\n\\nIF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.server_audit_specifications WHERE name = 'ComprehensiveLoginSpec')\\nBEGIN\\n  CREATE SERVER AUDIT SPECIFICATION [ComprehensiveLoginSpec]\\n    FOR SERVER AUDIT [ComprehensiveLoginAudit]\\n    ADD (SUCCESSFUL_LOGIN_GROUP),\\n    ADD (FAILED_LOGIN_GROUP),\\n    ADD (LOGIN_CHANGE_PASSWORD_GROUP),\\n    ADD (SERVER_PRINCIPAL_CHANGE_GROUP)\\n    WITH (STATE = ON);\\nEND\\n\\n-- Step 2: Create a historical tracking table for long-term dormancy analysis\\nIF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM sys.tables WHERE name = 'LoginActivityHistory' AND SCHEMA_NAME(schema_id) = 'dbo')\\nBEGIN\\n  CREATE TABLE dbo.LoginActivityHistory (\\n    login_name NVARCHAR(256),\\n    event_time DATETIME,\\n    action_id VARCHAR(10),\\n    succeeded BIT,\\n    client_ip NVARCHAR(128),\\n    application_name NVARCHAR(256),\\n    INDEX IX_LoginHistory_Name_Time (login_name, event_time DESC)\\n  );\\nEND\\n\\n-- Step 3: Populate from existing audit files if available\\n-- INSERT INTO dbo.LoginActivityHistory\\n-- SELECT server_principal_name, event_time, action_id, succeeded, server_instance_name, application_name\\n-- FROM sys.fn_get_audit_file('C:\\\\SQLAudit\\\\LoginHistory\\\\*', DEFAULT, DEFAULT)\\n-- WHERE action_id IN ('LGIS', 'LGIF');", "parameters": ["audit_file_path"], "description": "Creates a server audit with 1-year retention for login events and a tracking table for historical dormancy analysis."}	null	low	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.747421
9631	postgresql	verify	Verify improved parallel execution with custom plans	{"sql": "EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, VERBOSE) :query;", "parameters": ["query"], "description": "Confirms the custom plan accounts for the actual data distribution and distributes work evenly across parallel workers."}	\N	low	f	t	seconds	2026-02-25 08:59:43.77256
4495	sqlserver	execute	Create complete audit infrastructure with server and login specifications	{"sql": "-- Set login auditing to both successes and failures\\nEXEC xp_instance_regwrite N'HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE',\\n  N'Software\\\\Microsoft\\\\MSSQLServer\\\\MSSQLServer',\\n  N'AuditLevel', REG_DWORD, 3;\\n\\n-- Create file-based server audit\\nCREATE SERVER AUDIT [ComplianceAudit]\\nTO FILE (FILEPATH = @audit_file_path, MAXSIZE = 100 MB, MAX_ROLLOVER_FILES = 50, RESERVE_DISK_SPACE = OFF)\\nWITH (QUEUE_DELAY = 1000, ON_FAILURE = CONTINUE);\\n\\nALTER SERVER AUDIT [ComplianceAudit] WITH (STATE = ON);\\n\\n-- Create server-level specification\\nCREATE SERVER AUDIT SPECIFICATION [ServerAuditSpec]\\nFOR SERVER AUDIT [ComplianceAudit]\\nADD (FAILED_LOGIN_GROUP),\\nADD (SUCCESSFUL_LOGIN_GROUP),\\nADD (SERVER_ROLE_MEMBER_CHANGE_GROUP),\\nADD (SERVER_PERMISSION_CHANGE_GROUP),\\nADD (SERVER_PRINCIPAL_CHANGE_GROUP),\\nADD (SERVER_OBJECT_CHANGE_GROUP),\\nADD (DATABASE_CHANGE_GROUP),\\nADD (AUDIT_CHANGE_GROUP)\\nWITH (STATE = ON);", "parameters": ["audit_file_path"], "description": "Deploys complete audit infrastructure: registry-level login auditing plus a file-based SQL Server Audit with comprehensive server-level specifications."}	{"sql": "ALTER SERVER AUDIT SPECIFICATION [ServerAuditSpec] WITH (STATE = OFF);\\nDROP SERVER AUDIT SPECIFICATION [ServerAuditSpec];\\nALTER SERVER AUDIT [ComplianceAudit] WITH (STATE = OFF);\\nDROP SERVER AUDIT [ComplianceAudit];", "description": "Removes all audit infrastructure."}	low	t	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.751017
4514	sqlserver	instruct	Expand disk space or move audit to a larger volume	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "Choose one approach: (A) Expand current volume: Use storage management tools to expand the disk/LUN. (B) Move audit to a larger volume: ALTER SERVER AUDIT [AuditName] WITH (STATE = OFF); ALTER SERVER AUDIT [AuditName] TO FILE (FILEPATH = 'D:\\\\NewAuditPath\\\\'); ALTER SERVER AUDIT [AuditName] WITH (STATE = ON). (C) Implement automated archival: Create a SQL Agent job that copies audit files to long-term storage and deletes local files older than 90 days using: EXEC master.dbo.xp_delete_file 0, N'D:\\\\AuditPath\\\\', N'sqlaudit', @cutoff_date."}	null	medium	t	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.751029
4517	sqlserver	instruct	Create a SQL Agent job to archive audit files daily	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "Create a SQL Agent job with the following steps: (1) Copy audit files to the archive destination: xp_cmdshell 'robocopy \\"D:\\\\AuditFiles\\" \\"\\\\\\\\archive\\\\sqlaudit\\\\servername\\" *.sqlaudit /MOV /MINAGE:1' (copies files older than 1 day). Or for Azure: use AzCopy or BCP to upload to blob storage. (2) Verify the copy succeeded by checking the exit code. (3) Set unlimited rollover on the audit (MAX_ROLLOVER_FILES = 0) since archival handles retention. (4) Schedule the job to run daily during off-peak hours. (5) Configure job failure alerts via Database Mail."}	null	low	t	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.751032
4542	sqlserver	instruct	Lock down audit directory permissions on the file system	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "On the server OS: (1) Remove inheritance from the audit directory. (2) Remove all permissions except: SYSTEM (Full Control), SQL Server service account (Modify), and a dedicated security audit group (Read only). Using PowerShell: $acl = Get-Acl 'D:\\\\AuditFiles'; $acl.SetAccessRuleProtection($true, $false); $acl.SetAccessRule((New-Object System.Security.AccessControl.FileSystemAccessRule('NT SERVICE\\\\MSSQLSERVER','Modify','ContainerInherit,ObjectInherit','None','Allow'))); $acl.SetAccessRule((New-Object System.Security.AccessControl.FileSystemAccessRule('SYSTEM','FullControl','ContainerInherit,ObjectInherit','None','Allow'))); Set-Acl 'D:\\\\AuditFiles' $acl. (3) Remove Administrators group if possible (audit files should be accessed through SQL Server, not directly)."}	null	low	t	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.751047
4557	sqlserver	instruct	Implement audit file shipping to remote storage	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "Choose an approach: (A) SQL Agent job with robocopy to UNC share: Create a job that runs hourly: xp_cmdshell 'robocopy \\"D:\\\\AuditFiles\\" \\"\\\\\\\\siem-share\\\\sqlaudit\\\\servername\\" *.sqlaudit /MOV /MINAGE:1'. The share should be writable only by the SQL Agent account and readable only by the security team. (B) SIEM integration: Install a Splunk Universal Forwarder, Elastic Filebeat, or similar agent that monitors the audit directory and ships files in real-time. (C) Azure/AWS: Use AzCopy or AWS CLI via Agent job to upload to blob storage with immutable storage policies. (D) Windows Event Forwarding: Add a Security Log audit (see RC02 resolution) and configure WEF to ship events to a central collector."}	null	low	t	t	04:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.751055
