5353	sqlserver	instruct	Enable Force Encryption and enforce password policies	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "1) Install a valid TLS certificate on the SQL Server host (not self-signed for production). 2) In SQL Server Configuration Manager > SQL Server Network Configuration > Protocols: Set 'Force Encryption' to Yes and configure the certificate. 3) Restart SQL Server (required for encryption change). 4) Enforce password policy on all SQL logins: ALTER LOGIN [login_name] WITH CHECK_POLICY = ON, CHECK_EXPIRATION = ON. 5) Consider switching to Windows Authentication only if possible: EXEC xp_instance_regwrite N'HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE', N'Software\\\\Microsoft\\\\MSSQLServer\\\\MSSQLServer', N'LoginMode', REG_DWORD, 1. 6) Change the port to a non-default value (see port change procedures). 7) Enable login failure auditing."}	{"sql": null, "description": "Set Force Encryption to No in Configuration Manager and restart if applications cannot support TLS."}	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.906395
5358	sqlserver	execute	Enable password policy and lockout on all SQL logins	{"sql": "-- Enable password policy on all active SQL logins that lack it\\nDECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(MAX) = '';\\nSELECT @sql = @sql + 'ALTER LOGIN ' + QUOTENAME(name) + ' WITH CHECK_POLICY = ON, CHECK_EXPIRATION = ON; '\\nFROM sys.sql_logins\\nWHERE is_disabled = 0\\n  AND (is_policy_checked = 0 OR is_expiration_checked = 0)\\n  AND name NOT LIKE '##%'\\n  AND name <> 'sa';\\nEXEC sp_executesql @sql;\\n\\n-- Enable failed login auditing\\nEXEC xp_instance_regwrite N'HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE',\\n  N'Software\\\\Microsoft\\\\MSSQLServer\\\\MSSQLServer',\\n  N'AuditLevel', REG_DWORD, 2;", "parameters": [], "description": "Enforces password policy on all SQL logins and enables login failure auditing."}	{"sql": null, "description": "Disable password policy on specific logins if applications fail: ALTER LOGIN [name] WITH CHECK_POLICY = OFF."}	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.908183
5438	sqlserver	instruct	Set up centralized key management and deploy Always Encrypted keys	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "Option A - Azure Key Vault (recommended for cloud/hybrid): 1) Create an Azure Key Vault instance with soft-delete and purge protection enabled. 2) Create a key in the vault for Always Encrypted CMK. 3) Grant the application service principal GET, UNWRAP KEY, WRAP KEY, SIGN, VERIFY permissions. 4) Grant DBA accounts KEY MANAGEMENT permissions for rotation. 5) In SQL Server: CREATE COLUMN MASTER KEY [CMK_AKV] WITH (KEY_STORE_PROVIDER_NAME = 'AZURE_KEY_VAULT', KEY_PATH = 'https://vault-name.vault.azure.net/keys/key-name/version'). Option B - Windows Certificate Store (on-premises): 1) Generate a certificate using the Local Machine store: New-SelfSignedCertificate -Subject 'CN=AlwaysEncryptedCMK' -CertStoreLocation Cert:\\\\CurrentUser\\\\My -KeyExportPolicy Exportable -Type DocumentEncryptionCert. 2) Export the certificate with private key and store securely. 3) Install the certificate on ALL application servers that need to decrypt data. 4) CREATE COLUMN MASTER KEY [CMK_CERT] WITH (KEY_STORE_PROVIDER_NAME = 'MSSQL_CERTIFICATE_STORE', KEY_PATH = 'CurrentUser/My/thumbprint'). For both options: 5) Create the Column Encryption Key: CREATE COLUMN ENCRYPTION KEY [CEK_PII] WITH VALUES (COLUMN_MASTER_KEY = [CMK_name], ALGORITHM = 'RSA_OAEP', ENCRYPTED_VALUE = 0x...). 6) Document the key hierarchy and backup procedures. 7) Set up key rotation schedule (annually for CMK, as needed for CEK). 8) Backup the CMK securely (Azure Key Vault has built-in backup; for cert store, export with password)."}	null	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.92943
5444	sqlserver	instruct	Enable backup encryption for all databases with PII	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "1) Create a backup encryption certificate if one does not exist: USE master; CREATE MASTER KEY ENCRYPTION BY PASSWORD = 'StrongPassword!'; CREATE CERTIFICATE BackupEncryptCert WITH SUBJECT = 'Backup Encryption Certificate', EXPIRY_DATE = '20290101'. 2) CRITICAL: Backup the certificate immediately: BACKUP CERTIFICATE BackupEncryptCert TO FILE = 'path\\\\BackupEncryptCert.cer' WITH PRIVATE KEY (FILE = 'path\\\\BackupEncryptCert.pvk', ENCRYPTION BY PASSWORD = 'CertBackupPassword!'). Store securely in a separate location from backups. 3) Update all backup scripts to include encryption: BACKUP DATABASE [db_name] TO DISK = 'path' WITH ENCRYPTION (ALGORITHM = AES_256, SERVER CERTIFICATE = BackupEncryptCert), COMPRESSION. 4) Update SQL Agent backup jobs to include the ENCRYPTION clause. 5) For TDE-enabled databases: TDE automatically encrypts backups, but explicit backup encryption adds an additional layer. 6) Restrict access to backup file locations: a) Remove Everyone/Users group from backup directories. b) Grant access only to the SQL Server service account and backup administrators. 7) For dev/test restores: Create a separate certificate and anonymize PII after restore."}	{"sql": null, "description": "Remove the ENCRYPTION clause from backup scripts to revert to unencrypted backups."}	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.931686
5463	sqlserver	instruct	Secure dump files and restrict dump generation	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "1) Restrict dump file access: a) Locate the SQL Server LOG directory (typically C:\\\\Program Files\\\\Microsoft SQL Server\\\\MSSQL16.MSSQLSERVER\\\\MSSQL\\\\LOG). b) Remove all non-administrator access from the LOG directory. c) Grant access only to the SQL Server service account and DBA team. 2) Clean up existing dump files: a) Review files in sys.dm_server_memory_dumps. b) Delete dump files older than 30 days. c) If dumps contain PII, securely delete (use cipher /w: or sdelete). 3) Configure dump behavior: a) Use DBCC TRACEON(2551) to generate filtered mini dumps instead of full dumps (reduces PII exposure). b) Note: Some Microsoft support scenarios require full dumps. Document when full dumps are needed. 4) Automated cleanup: a) Create a SQL Agent job that deletes dump files older than 30 days. b) Monitor dump generation frequency (frequent dumps indicate stability issues). 5) Encrypt the dump directory: a) Enable EFS or BitLocker on the volume containing LOG directory. b) This protects dump files at rest."}	null	low	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.936091
876	sqlserver	execute	Purge old job history, backup history, and maintenance plan logs	{"sql": "USE msdb;\\n-- Remove job history older than 30 days\\nEXEC dbo.sp_purge_jobhistory @oldest_date = DATEADD(day, -30, GETDATE());\\n\\n-- Remove backup/restore history older than 30 days\\nEXEC dbo.sp_delete_backuphistory @oldest_date = DATEADD(day, -30, GETDATE());\\n\\n-- Remove maintenance plan history older than 30 days\\nDELETE FROM msdb.dbo.sysmaintplan_log\\nWHERE start_time < DATEADD(day, -30, GETDATE());\\n\\nDELETE FROM msdb.dbo.sysmaintplan_logdetail\\nWHERE start_time < DATEADD(day, -30, GETDATE());\\n\\n-- Check resulting sizes\\nSELECT 'sysjobhistory' AS tbl, COUNT(*) AS rows FROM msdb.dbo.sysjobhistory\\nUNION ALL\\nSELECT 'backupset', COUNT(*) FROM msdb.dbo.backupset\\nUNION ALL\\nSELECT 'sysmaintplan_log', COUNT(*) FROM msdb.dbo.sysmaintplan_log;", "parameters": ["retention_days: number of days of history to keep (typically 14-90)"], "description": "Uses Microsoft-supported procedures to purge old history. Adjust the retention period (-30 days) to match your retention policy."}	{"sql": "-- Purged history cannot be restored from MSDB; restore from backup taken in step 1 if needed\\nRESTORE DATABASE [msdb] FROM DISK = N'<BackupPath>\\\\msdb_pre_cleanup.bak' WITH REPLACE, RECOVERY;"}	medium	t	f	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.192388
31715	mariadb	verify	mysql_verify_access_restricted	{"sql": "SELECT @@bind_address AS bind_addr; SELECT User, Host FROM mysql.user WHERE Host = '%' OR Host = '0.0.0.0';", "parameters": [], "description": "Verify no users have unrestricted network access"}	\N	low	f	t	seconds	2026-05-11 22:17:39.229791
106	sqlserver	execute	Run a test backup to validate permissions	{"sql": "BACKUP DATABASE [$(database_name)]\\nTO DISK = N'$(backup_path)\\\\$(database_name)_permission_test.bak'\\nWITH NOFORMAT, NOINIT, NAME = N'Permission Test Backup',\\n     COMPRESSION, STATS = 10;", "parameters": ["database_name", "backup_path"], "description": "Executes a test backup to the destination path to confirm the permission fix worked end-to-end."}	{"sql": "-- Delete test backup file: DEL $(backup_path)\\\\$(database_name)_permission_test.bak", "description": "Remove the test backup file after verification."}	low	t	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.102991
110	sqlserver	configure	Configure backup to local staging with async transfer to network destination	{"sql": "-- Step 1: Backup to local staging path (fast, no network dependency):\\nBACKUP DATABASE [$(database_name)]\\nTO DISK = N'$(local_staging_path)\\\\$(database_name)_$(date_stamp).bak'\\nWITH COMPRESSION, STATS = 10;\\n\\n-- Step 2: Copy to network destination (separate job step, can retry independently):\\n-- Use ROBOCOPY or PowerShell Copy-Item in a subsequent job step:\\n-- EXEC xp_cmdshell 'robocopy $(local_staging_path) \\\\\\\\backupserver\\\\share *.bak /R:3 /W:60';", "parameters": ["database_name", "local_staging_path", "date_stamp"], "description": "Separates the SQL Server backup operation (local) from the network transfer, so network failures do not cause backup failures."}	{"sql": "-- Revert to direct-to-network backup:\\n-- BACKUP DATABASE [$(database_name)] TO DISK = N'\\\\\\\\backupserver\\\\share\\\\$(database_name).bak';", "description": "Revert to direct network backup if local staging space is not available."}	medium	t	t	01:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.102994
123	sqlserver	execute	Run a test backup to confirm file locking is resolved	{"sql": "BACKUP DATABASE [$(database_name)]\\nTO DISK = N'$(backup_path)\\\\$(database_name)_locktest.bak'\\nWITH COMPRESSION, NOINIT, STATS = 10;", "parameters": ["database_name", "backup_path"], "description": "Executes a test backup immediately after AV exclusion and agent reconfiguration to confirm the file locking issue is resolved."}	{"sql": "-- Remove test file: EXEC xp_cmdshell 'DEL $(backup_path)\\\\$(database_name)_locktest.bak';", "description": "Clean up the test backup file after verification."}	low	t	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.103004
824	sqlserver	execute	Change job owner to SA or sysadmin login, or grant db_owner per database	{"sql": "-- Option A (recommended): Change job owner to 'sa' or a dedicated sysadmin service account\\nEXEC msdb.dbo.sp_update_job\\n  @job_name = '<integrity_job_name>',\\n  @owner_login_name = 'sa';  -- or a dedicated service account that is sysadmin\\n\\n-- Option B: Grant sysadmin to the existing job owner (use only if policy permits)\\n-- ALTER SERVER ROLE sysadmin ADD MEMBER [<domain\\\\service_account>];\\n\\n-- Option C: Grant db_owner on each database (least privilege, but must repeat per DB)\\n-- USE [<database_name>];\\n-- ALTER ROLE db_owner ADD MEMBER [<job_owner_login>];", "parameters": ["integrity_job_name: name of the SQL Agent integrity check job", "job_owner_login: current job owner login for Option B/C"], "description": "Change the job owner to a login with sysadmin rights, or grant minimum required permissions. Option A is safest for automation: use a dedicated sysadmin service account rather than sa in production."}	{"sql": "-- Revert job owner to original login:\\nEXEC msdb.dbo.sp_update_job\\n  @job_name = '<integrity_job_name>',\\n  @owner_login_name = '<original_owner>';\\n\\n-- Revoke sysadmin if Option B was used:\\n-- ALTER SERVER ROLE sysadmin DROP MEMBER [<domain\\\\service_account>];"}	medium	t	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.192352
875	sqlserver	execute	Backup MSDB before any cleanup	{"sql": "BACKUP DATABASE [msdb]\\n  TO DISK = N'<BackupPath>\\\\msdb_pre_cleanup.bak'\\n  WITH FORMAT, COMPRESSION, STATS = 10, CHECKSUM;", "parameters": ["BackupPath: accessible backup directory path"], "description": "Create a verified MSDB backup before any cleanup operations so it can be restored if corruption or data loss is discovered"}	{"sql": "RESTORE DATABASE [msdb]\\n  FROM DISK = N'<BackupPath>\\\\msdb_pre_cleanup.bak'\\n  WITH REPLACE, RECOVERY;"}	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.192387
1100	sqlserver	instruct	Restore or reconfigure the cluster witness	{"sql": null, "parameters": ["witness_server: Hostname of the file share witness server", "share_name: Name of the file share used as witness", "storageaccount: Azure Storage Account name for cloud witness", "key: Azure Storage Account access key"], "description": "Choose the appropriate remediation based on witness type and failure:\\n\\nOption A — Restore File Share Witness (fix the file server):\\n1. Verify the file share server is online: ping <witness_server>\\n2. Confirm the share exists and cluster computer accounts have Read/Write access:\\n   net use \\\\\\\\<witness_server>\\\\<share_name>\\n3. If share is inaccessible, repair on the file server or recreate the share.\\n4. Cluster will automatically re-detect the witness within the heartbeat interval.\\n\\nOption B — Replace with Cloud Witness (Azure Blob Storage — recommended for modern deployments):\\n1. Create an Azure Storage Account (LRS is sufficient).\\n2. In Failover Cluster Manager: Cluster > More Actions > Configure Cluster Quorum Settings\\n   > Select Quorum Witness > Use a cloud witness\\n   > Enter Storage Account name, primary access key, and endpoint (optional).\\n3. PowerShell alternative:\\n   Set-ClusterQuorum -CloudWitness -AccountName <storageaccount> -AccessKey <key>\\n\\nOption C — Replace with a new File Share Witness:\\n   Set-ClusterQuorum -FileShareWitness \\\\\\\\<new_server>\\\\<new_share>\\n\\nOption D — Remove witness and switch to Node Majority (only for odd-node clusters):\\n   Set-ClusterQuorum -NodeMajority"}	{"sql": null, "description": "To revert to the previous witness configuration:\\n  Set-ClusterQuorum -FileShareWitness \\\\\\\\<original_server>\\\\<original_share>\\nOr for disk witness:\\n  Set-ClusterQuorum -DiskWitness <DiskResourceName>"}	medium	t	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.224735
1106	sqlserver	instruct	Apply correct quorum configuration using Failover Cluster Manager or PowerShell	{"sql": null, "parameters": ["storageaccount: Azure Storage Account name for cloud witness", "key: Azure Storage Account access key", "fileserver: File share witness server name", "share: File share name", "DRNodeName: DR site cluster node name(s) to adjust weight"], "description": "Based on the audit findings, apply the appropriate quorum configuration fix:\\n\\nScenario A — Even-node cluster with no witness (most common misconfiguration):\\n  Add a Cloud Witness (recommended):\\n    Set-ClusterQuorum -CloudWitness -AccountName <storageaccount> -AccessKey <key>\\n  Or File Share Witness:\\n    Set-ClusterQuorum -FileShareWitness \\\\\\\\<fileserver>\\\\<share>\\n\\nScenario B — Witness votes assigned to wrong datacenter in 4-node 2-site cluster:\\n  The witness should be in a THIRD independent location (not Site A or Site B).\\n  Reconfigure witness to Azure cloud storage or a management network server outside both sites.\\n  Set-ClusterQuorum -CloudWitness -AccountName <storageaccount> -AccessKey <key>\\n\\nScenario C — Node votes assigned incorrectly (e.g., DR site nodes should not hold votes in active-passive config):\\n  Adjust node vote weights:\\n    (Get-ClusterNode '<DRNodeName>').NodeWeight = 0\\n  Set DR site nodes to 0 weight — they participate but don't count toward quorum when DR site is isolated.\\n\\nScenario D — Odd-node cluster with unnecessary witness adding confusion:\\n  Remove witness and use pure NODE_MAJORITY:\\n    Set-ClusterQuorum -NodeMajority\\n\\nVerify total remaining votes still allow majority under any single failure scenario before applying."}	{"sql": null, "description": "To revert quorum changes:\\n  # Record current quorum type before making changes\\n  Get-ClusterQuorum\\n  # To restore previous witness:\\n  Set-ClusterQuorum -FileShareWitness \\\\\\\\<original_server>\\\\<original_share>\\n  # To restore node weight:\\n  (Get-ClusterNode '<NodeName>').NodeWeight = 1"}	medium	t	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.226571
