2328	sqlserver	instruct	Execute rolling patch using the cluster-aware sequence: secondaries first, then primary	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"failover_sql": "ALTER AVAILABILITY GROUP [<AG_name>] FAILOVER;", "installer_flags": "/quiet /action=patch /IAcceptSQLServerLicenseTerms", "health_check_interval_minutes": 5}, "description": "Use the following cluster-aware rolling patch sequence:\\n\\nFOR EACH secondary replica (one at a time):\\n  1. Verify this secondary is SYNCHRONIZED and CONNECTED:\\n     SELECT synchronization_state_desc FROM sys.dm_hadr_database_replica_states WHERE is_local=1\\n  2. Apply the CU on the secondary node:\\n     SQLServer<version>-KB<number>-x64.exe /quiet /action=patch /instancename=MSSQLSERVER /IAcceptSQLServerLicenseTerms\\n  3. After service restarts, wait for CONNECTED + SYNCHRONIZED before moving to the next node.\\n  4. If the secondary does not reconnect within 10 minutes, investigate before continuing.\\n\\nFOR THE PRIMARY (last):\\n  5. Perform a controlled failover to a patched secondary:\\n     ALTER AVAILABILITY GROUP [<AG_name>] FAILOVER;\\n  6. Verify the new primary is serving connections.\\n  7. Apply the CU to the former primary (now secondary).\\n  8. Verify SYNCHRONIZED.\\n  9. Optional: fail back to original primary.\\n\\nFor SQL Server Agent coordination, create a job that:\\n  - Checks AG health (step 1 SQL above) as a pre-flight\\n  - Launches the installer via xp_cmdshell or a PowerShell step\\n  - Checks AG health as a post-flight\\n  - Sends an alert if health degrades"}	{"description": "If a replica fails to reconnect after patching, do not proceed to the next replica. Investigate and resolve the disconnected replica first. If the primary node fails to patch, restore it from the pre-patch backup and rejoin as a secondary."}	medium	t	t	04:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389684
2352	sqlserver	instruct	Stop SQL Server and all dependent services in correct order	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"service_stop_order": ["SQLSERVERAGENT", "ReportServer", "MsDtsServer160", "MSSQLSERVER", "SQLBrowser"]}, "description": "Stop all SQL Server services using PowerShell (run as Administrator) in this exact order to avoid dependency errors: 1. Stop-Service SQLSERVERAGENT -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue 2. Stop-Service ReportServer -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue (SSRS) 3. Stop-Service MsDtsServer160 -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue (SSIS, adjust version number) 4. Stop-Service MSSQLSERVER -Force (or Stop-Service 'MSSQL$INSTANCENAME' for named instances) 5. Stop-Service SQLBrowser -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue 6. Verify all are stopped: Get-Service SQL* | Where-Object {$_.Status -ne 'Stopped'} | Format-Table Name, Status. For Failover Cluster Instances: Use Cluster Manager to take the SQL Server resource group OFFLINE rather than stopping the service directly. This ensures cluster health is maintained. For AlwaysOn AG: Consider failing over to a secondary before patching the primary."}	{"description": "-- Restart services in reverse order if patch fails:\\n-- Start-Service MSSQLSERVER\\n-- Start-Service SQLSERVERAGENT\\n-- Start-Service ReportServer"}	medium	t	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389699
2371	sqlserver	verify	Run CU/SP installer and verify successful completion	{"sql": "-- After CU/SP installation completes, verify new patch level\\nSELECT\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ServerName') AS server_name,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductVersion') AS product_version,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductLevel') AS patch_level,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('Edition') AS edition;\\n\\n-- Check error log for any file-locking or access-denied errors during install\\nCREATE TABLE #errlog (LogDate DATETIME, ProcessInfo VARCHAR(64), Text NVARCHAR(MAX));\\nINSERT INTO #errlog EXEC xp_readerrorlog 0, 1, N'access denied';\\nINSERT INTO #errlog EXEC xp_readerrorlog 0, 1, N'locked';\\nINSERT INTO #errlog EXEC xp_readerrorlog 0, 1, N'sharing violation';\\nSELECT * FROM #errlog WHERE LogDate > DATEADD(hour, -2, GETDATE());\\nDROP TABLE #errlog;\\n\\n-- Verify all databases are online\\nSELECT name, state_desc FROM sys.databases WHERE state_desc != 'ONLINE'", "parameters": {"expected_result": "New ProductLevel matches target CU/SP; no access-denied errors in log; all databases ONLINE", "post_patch_action": "Re-enable antivirus real-time scanning and resume backup agent schedules"}, "description": "With AV exclusions in place and backup agents paused, run the CU/SP installer. After completion, verify the new patch level is reflected in SERVERPROPERTY and confirm no access-denied or file-locking errors appear in the SQL Server error log. Then re-enable antivirus and resume backup agent schedules."}	{"description": "If installation fails again, engage AV vendor to identify which process is holding file locks using Process Monitor (Sysinternals); escalate to vendor support if locks persist after exclusions are applied"}	low	f	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.396703
2373	sqlserver	configure	Increase session timeout and switch secondaries to asynchronous mode before patching	{"sql": "-- Step 1: Increase session_timeout on all replicas to reduce false-positive timeouts\\n-- Run on PRIMARY replica\\nALTER AVAILABILITY GROUP [<ag_name>]\\nMODIFY REPLICA ON '<secondary_server_name>'\\nWITH (SESSION_TIMEOUT = 60);  -- Increase from default 10s to 60s\\n\\n-- Step 2: Switch synchronous secondaries to ASYNCHRONOUS_COMMIT during upgrade\\n-- to prevent the primary from being blocked if secondary goes offline for patching\\n-- Run on PRIMARY replica\\nALTER AVAILABILITY GROUP [<ag_name>]\\nMODIFY REPLICA ON '<secondary_being_upgraded>'\\nWITH (AVAILABILITY_MODE = ASYNCHRONOUS_COMMIT);\\n\\n-- Verify the mode change\\nSELECT ar.replica_server_name, ar.availability_mode_desc, ar.session_timeout\\nFROM sys.availability_replicas ar\\nJOIN sys.availability_groups ag ON ar.group_id = ag.group_id\\nWHERE ag.name = '<ag_name>'", "parameters": {"scope": "Apply per-replica as each secondary is patched; revert before starting next replica", "availability_mode_change": "SYNCHRONOUS_COMMIT -> ASYNCHRONOUS_COMMIT (temporary; revert after each node patches)", "session_timeout_recommended": "60 seconds (increase from default 10s for upgrade window)"}, "description": "For rolling upgrades, temporarily convert synchronous replicas to asynchronous mode before patching each secondary. This prevents the primary from stalling if the secondary goes offline longer than expected during CU installation. Also increase the session_timeout from the default 10 seconds to 60 seconds to reduce spurious failovers triggered by the brief unavailability during service restart. Revert these settings to their original values after each node completes patching."}	{"description": "Revert availability mode back to SYNCHRONOUS_COMMIT and session_timeout to original value (typically 10): ALTER AVAILABILITY GROUP [<ag_name>] MODIFY REPLICA ON '<node>' WITH (AVAILABILITY_MODE = SYNCHRONOUS_COMMIT, SESSION_TIMEOUT = 10)"}	medium	t	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.396704
2374	sqlserver	verify	Verify all replicas healthy after patching and restore original AG configuration	{"sql": "-- Verify all replicas reconnected and synchronized after upgrade\\nSELECT\\n  ag.name AS ag_name,\\n  ar.replica_server_name,\\n  ars.role_desc,\\n  ars.connected_state_desc,\\n  ars.synchronization_health_desc,\\n  ar.availability_mode_desc,\\n  ar.session_timeout,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('ProductVersion') AS sql_version\\nFROM sys.dm_hadr_availability_replica_states ars\\nJOIN sys.availability_replicas ar ON ars.replica_id = ar.replica_id\\nJOIN sys.availability_groups ag ON ar.group_id = ag.group_id\\nORDER BY ag.name, ars.role_desc;\\n\\n-- Revert asynchronous replicas back to synchronous mode (run on primary)\\n-- ALTER AVAILABILITY GROUP [<ag_name>]\\n-- MODIFY REPLICA ON '<upgraded_node>' WITH (\\n--   AVAILABILITY_MODE = SYNCHRONOUS_COMMIT,\\n--   SESSION_TIMEOUT = 10\\n-- );\\n\\n-- Verify no cluster errors in the period\\nCREATE TABLE #errlog (LogDate DATETIME, ProcessInfo VARCHAR(64), Text NVARCHAR(MAX));\\nINSERT INTO #errlog EXEC xp_readerrorlog 0, 1, N'timeout';\\nINSERT INTO #errlog EXEC xp_readerrorlog 0, 1, N'lease';\\nINSERT INTO #errlog EXEC xp_readerrorlog 0, 1, N'failover';\\nSELECT * FROM #errlog WHERE LogDate > DATEADD(hour, -4, GETDATE())\\n  AND (Text LIKE '%unexpected%' OR Text LIKE '%error%' OR Text LIKE '%failed%');\\nDROP TABLE #errlog", "parameters": {"success_criteria": "All replicas: connected_state_desc=CONNECTED, synchronization_health_desc=HEALTHY, availability_mode_desc reverted to SYNCHRONOUS_COMMIT"}, "description": "After each node completes patching, verify it reconnects to the AG and reaches a SYNCHRONIZED state before patching the next node. Once all nodes are patched, revert session_timeout and availability mode to their original synchronous configuration. Confirm the AG health dashboard shows all replicas CONNECTED and SYNCHRONIZED."}	{"description": "If a replica fails to reconnect after patching, check WSFC cluster logs and SQL Server error log. Consider performing a manual failover to a healthy node and re-patching the failed node"}	low	f	t	00:20:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.396705
2379	sqlserver	execute	Incrementally raise compatibility level one major version at a time	{"sql": "-- Step-by-step compatibility level upgrade\\n-- Map: SQL 2008=100, 2012=110, 2014=120, 2016=130, 2017=140, 2019=150, 2022=160\\n-- Always increment by one level at a time; test between each step\\n\\n-- Example: Raising from 100 -> 130 in steps for a database named 'MyDB'\\n\\n-- Step 1: Raise to 110 (SQL Server 2012 compat)\\nALTER DATABASE [<database_name>] SET COMPATIBILITY_LEVEL = 110;\\n-- Run workload validation, check Query Store for regressions\\nSELECT TOP 10\\n  qsq.query_id, qsq.query_hash,\\n  qsqt.query_sql_text,\\n  qsp.plan_id,\\n  qsrs.avg_duration / 1000.0 AS avg_duration_ms,\\n  qsrs.avg_logical_io_reads\\nFROM sys.query_store_query qsq\\nJOIN sys.query_store_query_text qsqt ON qsq.query_text_id = qsqt.query_text_id\\nJOIN sys.query_store_plan qsp ON qsq.query_id = qsp.query_id\\nJOIN sys.query_store_runtime_stats qsrs ON qsp.plan_id = qsrs.plan_id\\nJOIN sys.query_store_runtime_stats_interval qsrsi ON qsrs.runtime_stats_interval_id = qsrsi.runtime_stats_interval_id\\nWHERE qsrsi.start_time > DATEADD(hour, -2, GETDATE())\\nORDER BY qsrs.avg_duration DESC;\\n\\n-- If no regressions, raise to next level:\\n-- ALTER DATABASE [<database_name>] SET COMPATIBILITY_LEVEL = 120;\\n-- ALTER DATABASE [<database_name>] SET COMPATIBILITY_LEVEL = 130;\\n-- etc.\\n\\n-- Confirm final level\\nSELECT name, compatibility_level FROM sys.databases\\nWHERE name = '<database_name>'", "parameters": {"increment_size": "One major version at a time (10 units per step)", "regression_fix": "sys.sp_query_store_force_plan to force old stable plan until query is tuned", "compatibility_map": "100=SQL2008, 110=SQL2012, 120=SQL2014, 130=SQL2016, 140=SQL2017, 150=SQL2019, 160=SQL2022", "regression_detection": "Query Store: compare avg_duration before/after each compat change"}, "description": "Raise the compatibility level one major version at a time (e.g., 100 -> 110 -> 120 -> 130), testing the application workload at each level before advancing. This incremental approach allows identification of the specific compatibility level that introduces query plan regressions or behavioral changes. Use Query Store to detect regressions after each change. If regressions are detected at a given level, use Query Store plan forcing to stabilize affected queries before advancing to the next level."}	{"description": "Revert to previous compatibility level: ALTER DATABASE [<database_name>] SET COMPATIBILITY_LEVEL = <previous_level>. Use Query Store plan forcing to stabilize any regressed queries before attempting the level change again"}	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.398178
2384	sqlserver	execute	Notify applications and set databases to restricted access mode	{"sql": "-- Step 1: Check current active sessions and open transactions\\nSELECT\\n  s.session_id, s.login_name, s.host_name,\\n  s.program_name, s.status,\\n  s.last_request_start_time,\\n  t.transaction_begin_time,\\n  DATEDIFF(minute, t.transaction_begin_time, GETDATE()) AS tran_age_minutes\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions s\\nLEFT JOIN sys.dm_tran_session_transactions st ON s.session_id = st.session_id\\nLEFT JOIN sys.dm_tran_active_transactions t ON st.transaction_id = t.transaction_id\\nWHERE s.is_user_process = 1\\n  AND s.session_id != @@SPID\\nORDER BY tran_age_minutes DESC;\\n\\n-- Step 2: Set each user database to RESTRICTED_USER mode\\n-- This prevents new connections while allowing existing sessions to continue and disconnect naturally\\n-- Repeat for each database:\\nUSE master;\\n-- This will wait for existing connections to finish and roll them back if they don't:\\nALTER DATABASE [<database_name>]\\nSET RESTRICTED_USER WITH ROLLBACK AFTER 300;  -- Wait 300 seconds, then rollback\\n\\n-- Confirm restricted access is set\\nSELECT name, user_access_desc\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE database_id > 4", "parameters": {"scope": "Apply to all user databases, then master/msdb if needed by installer", "restricted_user_effect": "Only members of db_owner, dbcreator, and sysadmin can connect; new app connections rejected", "rollback_after_seconds": "300 (5 minutes grace period; reduce to 60 during off-hours)"}, "description": "Before setting databases to restricted access, ensure applications have been notified and connection pools are being drained. Use ALTER DATABASE SET RESTRICTED_USER WITH ROLLBACK AFTER N to provide a grace period for active transactions to complete, after which SQL Server will roll them back and disconnect the sessions. Use 300 seconds (5 minutes) as the grace period for most workloads; use a shorter period during off-hours maintenance."}	{"description": "To immediately restore database access: ALTER DATABASE [<database_name>] SET MULTI_USER"}	medium	t	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.398885
3262	sqlserver	execute	Drop nonclustered indexes from confirmed empty development tables	{"sql": "-- Drop each confirmed unused index on an empty or near-empty table.\\nDROP INDEX [<index_name>] ON [<schema>].[<table_name>];\\n\\n-- If the table itself is a development artifact with no production use,\\n-- consider dropping the table entirely after confirming with the team:\\n-- DROP TABLE [<schema>].[<table_name>];", "parameters": {}, "description": "Removes nonclustered indexes from tables confirmed to be empty development artifacts. For tables that will eventually hold production data, leaving only the primary key / clustered index is appropriate until the production access patterns are known."}	{"description": "-- Recreate the index if needed when production data arrives.\\nCREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX [<index_name>]\\n    ON [<schema>].[<table_name>] (<key_columns>)\\n    INCLUDE (<included_columns>)\\n    WITH (ONLINE = ON);"}	low	t	f	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.47946
2385	sqlserver	execute	Kill remaining blocking sessions and verify no open transactions	{"sql": "-- Identify any remaining sessions after grace period\\nSELECT\\n  s.session_id, s.login_name, s.host_name,\\n  s.program_name, s.status,\\n  t.transaction_begin_time,\\n  DATEDIFF(minute, t.transaction_begin_time, GETDATE()) AS tran_age_min\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_sessions s\\nLEFT JOIN sys.dm_tran_session_transactions st ON s.session_id = st.session_id\\nLEFT JOIN sys.dm_tran_active_transactions t ON st.transaction_id = t.transaction_id\\nWHERE s.is_user_process = 1\\n  AND s.session_id != @@SPID;\\n\\n-- Kill any remaining sessions (run individually after confirming session purpose):\\n-- KILL <session_id>;\\n\\n-- After killing sessions, verify no open transactions remain:\\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS remaining_open_transactions\\nFROM sys.dm_tran_active_transactions\\nWHERE transaction_type != 2  -- Exclude system transactions\\n  AND dtc_state != 1;  -- Exclude active distributed transactions\\n\\n-- Verify no blocking\\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS blocking_chains\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_requests\\nWHERE blocking_session_id > 0", "parameters": {"do_not_kill": "system sessions (session_id < 50), current session (@@SPID), distributed transactions in prepared state", "expected_result": "remaining_open_transactions = 0; blocking_chains = 0"}, "description": "After the grace period, if any sessions remain connected, identify them and issue KILL commands for each. Prioritize killing long-running transactions first. Verify that the count of open user transactions reaches zero before proceeding. Do not KILL system spids (session_id <= 50) or the current connection."}	{"description": "If any critical application sessions cannot be terminated safely, defer the maintenance window and restore MULTI_USER access: ALTER DATABASE [<database_name>] SET MULTI_USER"}	medium	t	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.398886
2391	sqlserver	configure	Apply new max server memory setting using sp_configure	{"sql": "-- Apply the calculated max server memory value\\n-- Replace <new_value_mb> with the calculated value from Step 1\\n\\nEXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1;\\nRECONFIGURE;\\n\\nEXEC sp_configure 'max server memory (MB)', <new_value_mb>;\\nRECONFIGURE WITH OVERRIDE;\\n\\n-- Verify the new setting is applied (takes effect immediately, no restart required)\\nSELECT\\n  name,\\n  value AS configured_value_mb,\\n  value_in_use AS active_value_mb,\\n  description\\nFROM sys.configurations\\nWHERE name IN ('max server memory (MB)', 'min server memory (MB)');\\n\\n-- Also set min server memory to prevent SQL Server from releasing memory to OS\\n-- under transient load (optional but recommended for dedicated SQL Server instances)\\n-- EXEC sp_configure 'min server memory (MB)', <min_value_mb>;  -- typically 25% of max\\n-- RECONFIGURE WITH OVERRIDE;", "parameters": {"supported_method": "sp_configure only (not registry)", "change_takes_effect": "Immediately — no restart required", "min_server_memory_recommendation": "Set to 25-50% of max server memory on dedicated SQL Server instances to prevent excessive memory release under low load"}, "description": "Apply the new max server memory value using sp_configure. This change takes effect immediately without requiring a SQL Server service restart — SQL Server will begin expanding the buffer pool up to the new limit as pages are loaded. Setting max server memory via sp_configure is the only supported method; do not modify registry directly."}	{"description": "Revert to previous value: EXEC sp_configure 'max server memory (MB)', <previous_value>; RECONFIGURE WITH OVERRIDE"}	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.401507
2394	sqlserver	configure	Increase max server memory to give SQL Server more buffer pool headroom	{"sql": "-- Check current setting first\\nSELECT name, value_in_use, description\\nFROM sys.configurations\\nWHERE name IN ('max server memory (MB)', 'min server memory (MB)');\\n\\n-- Calculate safe max: leave at least 10% or 4 GB for OS (whichever is larger)\\n-- Replace @new_max_mb with your calculated value before running\\nDECLARE @total_ram_mb INT;\\nSELECT @total_ram_mb = physical_memory_kb / 1024 FROM sys.dm_os_sys_info;\\n\\nSELECT\\n  @total_ram_mb AS total_ram_mb,\\n  CASE\\n    WHEN @total_ram_mb <= 8192  THEN @total_ram_mb - 2048  -- leave 2 GB\\n    WHEN @total_ram_mb <= 16384 THEN @total_ram_mb - 3072  -- leave 3 GB\\n    WHEN @total_ram_mb <= 32768 THEN @total_ram_mb - 4096  -- leave 4 GB\\n    ELSE @total_ram_mb - CAST(@total_ram_mb * 0.10 AS INT) -- leave 10%\\n  END AS recommended_max_server_memory_mb;\\n\\n-- After confirming the recommended value, execute:\\n-- EXEC sp_configure 'max server memory (MB)', <recommended_value>;\\n-- RECONFIGURE WITH OVERRIDE;", "parameters": {"change_method": "sp_configure + RECONFIGURE WITH OVERRIDE", "target_setting": "max server memory (MB)", "restart_required": false}, "description": "Calculate the safe maximum server memory value leaving sufficient headroom for the OS and other processes, then apply the new setting. This is a dynamic change that does not require a SQL Server restart."}	{"description": "-- Revert to previous max server memory value captured in baseline\\nEXEC sp_configure 'max server memory (MB)', <previous_value_from_baseline>;\\nRECONFIGURE WITH OVERRIDE;"}	medium	t	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.401512
2396	sqlserver	instruct	Apply Resource Governor to cap buffer pool consumption by runaway workloads	{"sql": "-- If a specific application or workload is consuming disproportionate buffer,\\n-- use Resource Governor to cap its memory grant percentage.\\n-- This prevents one workload from evicting pages needed by another.\\n\\n-- Example: cap a reporting workload to 25% of max memory grants\\nCREATE RESOURCE POOL ReportingPool\\n  WITH (MAX_MEMORY_GRANT_PERCENT = 25);\\n\\nCREATE WORKLOAD GROUP ReportingGroup\\n  USING ReportingPool;\\n\\n-- Create a classifier function to route connections to ReportingGroup\\n-- (replace 'ReportingLogin' with the actual application login)\\nCREATE FUNCTION dbo.RG_Classifier()\\nRETURNS SYSNAME WITH SCHEMABINDING\\nAS\\nBEGIN\\n  IF SUSER_NAME() = 'ReportingLogin'\\n    RETURN 'ReportingGroup';\\n  RETURN 'default';\\nEND;\\n\\nALTER RESOURCE GOVERNOR WITH (CLASSIFIER_FUNCTION = dbo.RG_Classifier);\\nALTER RESOURCE GOVERNOR RECONFIGURE;", "parameters": {"requires_enterprise_or_developer_edition": true, "max_memory_grant_percent_for_heavy_workload": 25}, "description": "When a single workload (e.g., batch reporting, ETL) is responsible for most of the buffer pressure, Resource Governor memory grant caps prevent it from crowding out OLTP queries. This is a complementary measure to increasing max server memory."}	{"description": "-- Remove Resource Governor configuration\\nALTER RESOURCE GOVERNOR WITH (CLASSIFIER_FUNCTION = NULL);\\nALTER RESOURCE GOVERNOR RECONFIGURE;\\nDROP WORKLOAD GROUP ReportingGroup;\\nDROP RESOURCE POOL ReportingPool;\\nDROP FUNCTION dbo.RG_Classifier;"}	medium	t	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.401515
3241	sqlserver	execute	Drop the overly restrictive filtered index and optionally replace with a broader index	{"sql": "-- Option A: Drop the filtered index entirely if no valid use case exists.\\nDROP INDEX [<index_name>] ON [<schema>].[<table_name>];\\n\\n-- Option B: Replace with a broader filtered index matching actual query predicates.\\n-- Script the new definition based on plan cache analysis from step 2.\\nCREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX [<new_index_name>]\\n    ON [<schema>].[<table_name>] (<key_columns>)\\n    INCLUDE (<included_columns>)\\n    WHERE <corrected_filter_predicate>\\n    WITH (ONLINE = ON);", "parameters": {}, "description": "Either drops the unusable filtered index or recreates it with a corrected WHERE clause that matches the actual query predicate patterns identified in the plan cache analysis."}	{"description": "-- Recreate original filtered index if regression observed.\\nCREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX [<index_name>]\\n    ON [<schema>].[<table_name>] (<key_columns>)\\n    WHERE <original_filter_definition>\\n    WITH (ONLINE = ON);"}	medium	t	f	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.525884
2401	sqlserver	execute	Create the highest-impact indexes using ONLINE=ON to avoid blocking	{"sql": "-- Execute the CREATE INDEX statements generated in step 1, one at a time.\\n-- Always use ONLINE=ON for SQL Server Enterprise Edition to allow concurrent DML.\\n-- Example (replace with actual columns from step 1 output):\\n--\\n-- CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX IX_Orders_CustomerID_OrderDate\\n-- ON dbo.Orders (CustomerID, OrderDate)\\n-- INCLUDE (OrderStatus, TotalAmount, ShipDate)\\n-- WITH (\\n--   ONLINE = ON,          -- allow DML during build (Enterprise only)\\n--   FILLFACTOR = 85,      -- leave 15% free space to reduce future fragmentation\\n--   SORT_IN_TEMPDB = ON,  -- build sort in tempdb to reduce log contention\\n--   DATA_COMPRESSION = PAGE  -- optional: compress to reduce buffer pool footprint\\n-- );\\n\\n-- Monitor progress during index build:\\nSELECT\\n  r.command,\\n  r.percent_complete,\\n  r.estimated_completion_time / 1000 AS est_completion_sec,\\n  SUBSTRING(st.text, (r.statement_start_offset/2)+1,\\n    ((CASE r.statement_end_offset WHEN -1 THEN DATALENGTH(st.text)\\n      ELSE r.statement_end_offset END - r.statement_start_offset)/2)+1) AS sql_text\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_requests r\\nCROSS APPLY sys.dm_exec_sql_text(r.sql_handle) st\\nWHERE r.command LIKE '%INDEX%';", "parameters": {"fillfactor": 85, "online_build": true, "sort_in_tempdb": true, "data_compression": "PAGE (optional)"}, "description": "Create the prioritized indexes using ONLINE=ON on Enterprise Edition. On Standard Edition, schedule index creation during a maintenance window. Use DATA_COMPRESSION=PAGE to further reduce buffer pool footprint."}	{"description": "-- If a new index causes unexpected plan regressions, drop it:\\n-- DROP INDEX IX_<index_name> ON dbo.<table_name>;\\n-- Or disable it first for immediate relief without losing the index definition:\\n-- ALTER INDEX IX_<index_name> ON dbo.<table_name> DISABLE;"}	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.405962
2404	sqlserver	instruct	Apply targeted plan fixes: statistics update, hints, or Query Store plan forcing	{"sql": "-- Approach A: Update statistics to give optimizer accurate cardinality data\\n-- (addresses most spill/grant issues caused by stale statistics)\\nUPDATE STATISTICS dbo.<table_name> WITH FULLSCAN;\\n-- Or for all tables in a database:\\nEXEC sp_updatestats;  -- updates only modified tables\\n\\n-- Approach B: Disable adaptive memory grant feedback if it is over-reacting\\n-- (SQL 2019+ — per-query scoped hint)\\n-- SELECT ... OPTION (USE HINT ('DISABLE_BATCH_MODE_MEMORY_GRANT_FEEDBACK'));\\n\\n-- Approach C: Use OPTION (MAXDOP 1) to prevent parallelism-induced over-grants\\n-- on queries that run frequently and don't benefit from parallelism:\\n-- SELECT ... OPTION (MAXDOP 1);\\n\\n-- Approach D: Force a known-good plan via Query Store\\n-- 1. Find the good plan_id from Query Store (lower avg_logical_io_reads)\\nSELECT qsp.plan_id, qsrs.avg_logical_io_reads, qsrs.avg_rowcount\\nFROM sys.query_store_plan qsp\\nJOIN sys.query_store_runtime_stats qsrs ON qsp.plan_id = qsrs.plan_id\\nJOIN sys.query_store_query qsq ON qsp.query_id = qsq.query_id\\nWHERE qsq.query_id = <query_id_from_step1>\\nORDER BY qsrs.avg_logical_io_reads ASC;\\n\\n-- 2. Force the good plan:\\nEXEC sys.sp_query_store_force_plan @query_id = <query_id>, @plan_id = <good_plan_id>;\\n\\n-- Approach E: Add a min/max memory grant hint to prevent over-allocation\\n-- SELECT ... OPTION (MIN_GRANT_PERCENT = 5, MAX_GRANT_PERCENT = 25);", "parameters": {}, "description": "Apply the appropriate fix based on root cause of the bad plan: stale stats → update statistics; over-granting → max grant hint or disable adaptive MGF; plan regression → Query Store force plan; parallelism overhead → MAXDOP 1 hint."}	{"description": "-- Unforce Query Store plan:\\nEXEC sys.sp_query_store_unforce_plan @query_id = <query_id>, @plan_id = <plan_id>;\\n-- Remove query hints by reverting stored procedure to previous version.\\n-- Revert statistics by running UPDATE STATISTICS WITH ROWCOUNT = <previous_value> if needed."}	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.406859
2407	sqlserver	configure	Add tempdb data files to reduce PFS/GAM/SGAM allocation page contention	{"sql": "-- Check current tempdb file count\\nSELECT name, physical_name, size * 8 / 1024 AS size_mb, growth, is_percent_growth\\nFROM sys.master_files\\nWHERE database_id = 2\\nORDER BY file_id;\\n\\n-- SQL Server best practice: 1 tempdb data file per logical CPU, up to 8 files\\n-- Check logical CPU count:\\nSELECT cpu_count AS logical_cpus FROM sys.dm_os_sys_info;\\n\\n-- Add tempdb files if count < min(logical_cpus, 8)\\n-- All files should be the same size for equal round-robin allocation\\n-- Replace paths and sizes with your tempdb location and current file size:\\n/*\\nALTER DATABASE tempdb ADD FILE (\\n  NAME = tempdev2,\\n  FILENAME = 'D:\\tempdb\\tempdev2.ndf',\\n  SIZE = <same_as_existing_file_mb>MB,\\n  MAXSIZE = UNLIMITED,\\n  FILEGROWTH = 512MB\\n);\\n-- Repeat for additional files as needed\\n*/\\n\\n-- Verify after adding files:\\nSELECT name, physical_name, size * 8 / 1024 AS size_mb\\nFROM sys.master_files\\nWHERE database_id = 2;", "parameters": {"requires_restart": false, "all_files_same_size": true, "recommended_file_count": "min(logical_cpu_count, 8)"}, "description": "Increase tempdb data file count to match the number of logical CPUs (up to 8) so that allocation page contention (PFS, GAM, SGAM) is distributed across multiple files using SQL Server's proportional fill algorithm."}	{"description": "-- Files cannot be removed from tempdb without restarting SQL Server.\\n-- Reduce file count at next restart by removing the extra files:\\n-- ALTER DATABASE tempdb REMOVE FILE tempdev2;  -- repeat for each added file\\n-- Changes take effect at next SQL Server service restart."}	low	t	f	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.407821
2416	sqlserver	configure	Enable AUTO_UPDATE_STATISTICS and AUTO_UPDATE_STATISTICS_ASYNC to prevent recurrence	{"sql": "-- Check current auto-update settings\\nSELECT name,\\n  is_auto_update_stats_on,\\n  is_auto_update_stats_async_on,\\n  is_auto_create_stats_on\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE name = DB_NAME();\\n\\n-- Enable auto-update statistics (synchronous trigger on stale threshold)\\nALTER DATABASE [<your_database_name>]\\nSET AUTO_UPDATE_STATISTICS ON;\\n\\n-- Enable async updates to prevent query blocking during stat update\\n-- (query uses old stats immediately, update happens in background)\\nALTER DATABASE [<your_database_name>]\\nSET AUTO_UPDATE_STATISTICS_ASYNC ON;\\n\\n-- Enable auto-create statistics for columns missing stats objects\\nALTER DATABASE [<your_database_name>]\\nSET AUTO_CREATE_STATISTICS ON;\\n\\n-- Verify settings applied\\nSELECT name,\\n  is_auto_update_stats_on,\\n  is_auto_update_stats_async_on,\\n  is_auto_create_stats_on\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE name = '<your_database_name>';", "parameters": {"AUTO_CREATE_STATISTICS": "ON", "AUTO_UPDATE_STATISTICS": "ON", "AUTO_UPDATE_STATISTICS_ASYNC": "ON"}, "description": "Enable the three auto-statistics database settings so SQL Server automatically creates and updates statistics as data changes, preventing future stale-statistics plan regressions."}	{"description": "ALTER DATABASE [<your_database_name>] SET AUTO_UPDATE_STATISTICS OFF;\\nALTER DATABASE [<your_database_name>] SET AUTO_UPDATE_STATISTICS_ASYNC OFF;"}	low	f	t	00:02:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.410097
2413	sqlserver	execute	Create the top-priority missing indexes one at a time with progress monitoring	{"sql": "-- Execute the highest improvement_measure index DDL from step 1.\\n-- Template (replace with actual DDL from step 1 output):\\n/*\\nCREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX [IX_dbo_TableName_Col1_Col2]\\nON dbo.TableName (EqualityCol1, EqualityCol2, InequalityCol3)\\nINCLUDE (IncludeCol1, IncludeCol2)\\nWITH (\\n  ONLINE = ON,           -- Enterprise: allow concurrent DML during build\\n  FILLFACTOR = 85,       -- 15% free space per page\\n  SORT_IN_TEMPDB = ON,   -- keep log contention off primary data files\\n  DATA_COMPRESSION = PAGE,  -- reduce buffer pool footprint of new index\\n  MAXDOP = 4             -- limit CPU consumption during build\\n);\\n*/\\n\\n-- Monitor build progress:\\nSELECT\\n  r.command,\\n  r.percent_complete,\\n  r.estimated_completion_time / 1000 / 60 AS est_completion_min,\\n  r.wait_type,\\n  r.wait_time / 1000 AS wait_sec\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_requests r\\nWHERE r.command LIKE '%CREATE%INDEX%';\\n\\n-- After build completes, verify the index exists:\\nSELECT i.name, i.type_desc, i.is_disabled, i.fill_factor\\nFROM sys.indexes i\\nWHERE i.object_id = OBJECT_ID('dbo.<table_name>')\\nORDER BY i.index_id;", "parameters": {"maxdop": 4, "online": true, "fillfactor": 85, "data_compression": "PAGE"}, "description": "Execute the prioritized CREATE INDEX statement for the highest-impact missing index. Use ONLINE=ON on Enterprise Edition to avoid blocking concurrent DML. Monitor build progress and verify the index is created and enabled."}	{"description": "-- If the index causes unexpected plan regressions or write performance degradation:\\nDROP INDEX [IX_dbo_TableName_Col1_Col2] ON dbo.TableName;\\n-- Or disable immediately without losing the definition:\\nALTER INDEX [IX_dbo_TableName_Col1_Col2] ON dbo.TableName DISABLE;"}	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.409197
2415	sqlserver	execute	Update statistics on all tables with high modification ratios	{"sql": "-- Identify tables with stale statistics (>= 20% modification counter)\\nSELECT\\n  OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME(sp.object_id) + '.' + OBJECT_NAME(sp.object_id) AS table_name,\\n  s.name AS stat_name,\\n  sp.last_updated,\\n  sp.rows AS stat_rows,\\n  sp.modification_counter,\\n  CAST(sp.modification_counter AS FLOAT) / NULLIF(sp.rows, 0) * 100 AS pct_modified,\\n  DATEDIFF(day, sp.last_updated, GETDATE()) AS days_stale,\\n  'UPDATE STATISTICS ' +\\n    OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME(sp.object_id) + '.' + OBJECT_NAME(sp.object_id) +\\n    ' ' + QUOTENAME(s.name) + ' WITH FULLSCAN;' AS update_cmd\\nFROM sys.stats s\\nCROSS APPLY sys.dm_db_stats_properties(s.object_id, s.stats_id) sp\\nWHERE OBJECTPROPERTY(s.object_id, 'IsUserTable') = 1\\n  AND sp.rows > 1000\\n  AND (\\n    CAST(sp.modification_counter AS FLOAT) / NULLIF(sp.rows, 0) > 0.20  -- > 20% changed\\n    OR sp.last_updated < DATEADD(day, -7, GETDATE())                    -- older than 7 days\\n  )\\nORDER BY pct_modified DESC;\\n\\n-- Execute updates for the top offenders (run update_cmd column output manually\\n-- or use sp_updatestats for all tables in the database at once):\\nEXEC sp_updatestats;  -- updates only tables with modifications since last update\\n\\n-- For critical tables with known bad plans, use FULLSCAN for precise histograms:\\n-- UPDATE STATISTICS dbo.<CriticalTable> WITH FULLSCAN;", "parameters": {"update_method": "sp_updatestats for all + FULLSCAN for critical tables", "age_threshold_days": 7, "modification_threshold_pct": 20}, "description": "Identify all statistics objects with modification ratios above 20% or older than 7 days, then update them. Use FULLSCAN for the most critical tables to produce accurate histograms rather than relying on sampling."}	{"description": "-- Statistics updates cannot be directly rolled back.\\n-- If an update causes a plan regression, force the previous good plan via Query Store:\\n-- EXEC sys.sp_query_store_force_plan @query_id = <id>, @plan_id = <good_plan_id>;\\n-- Or use a plan guide with USE PLAN hint to lock in the good plan."}	low	f	f	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.409758
3212	sqlserver	execute	Create filtered index and drop the original full index	{"sql": "-- Step 1: Create the filtered replacement index\\nCREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX [IX_<table>_<column>_NotNull]\\n  ON [<schema>].[<table>] ([<column>])\\n  INCLUDE ([<included_col1>], [<included_col2>])  -- Add included cols from original index\\n  WHERE [<column>] IS NOT NULL\\n  WITH (\\n    ONLINE = ON,\\n    SORT_IN_TEMPDB = ON,\\n    FILLFACTOR = 90\\n  );\\n\\n-- Step 2: Verify the new index is being picked up by the optimizer\\n-- Run a representative query and check SHOWPLAN_XML confirms seek on new index\\n\\n-- Step 3: Drop the original full index (after confirming new index works)\\nDROP INDEX [<original_index_name>] ON [<schema>].[<table>];", "parameters": {"table": "Target table", "column": "The nullable column", "schema": "Target schema", "original_index_name": "Name of the existing full index to replace"}, "description": "Creates the WHERE IS NOT NULL filtered index with ONLINE=ON to avoid blocking, then drops the original full index after verification"}	{"description": "CREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX [<original_index_name>] ON [<schema>].[<table>] ([<column>]) INCLUDE ([<included_cols>]) WITH (ONLINE = ON);"}	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.525865
2419	sqlserver	instruct	Apply targeted CE fixes: resolve type mismatches, update stats, or change CE model version	{"sql": "-- Fix A: Eliminate implicit type conversions that cause CE to ignore statistics\\n-- Example: nvarchar column filtered with varchar parameter\\n--   Bad:  WHERE CustomerCode = @VarcharParam  (column is nvarchar, param is varchar)\\n--   Fix:  WHERE CustomerCode = CAST(@VarcharParam AS NVARCHAR(50))\\n--   Or:   Change parameter type in application / stored procedure to match column\\n\\n-- Fix B: Add statistics on columns referenced in JOINs and WHERE clauses\\n--        that lack auto-created stats\\nCREATE STATISTICS ST_TableName_ColumnName\\nON dbo.TableName (ColumnName)\\nWITH FULLSCAN;\\n\\n-- Fix C: Update statistics on key tables with FULLSCAN for maximum accuracy\\nUPDATE STATISTICS dbo.TableName WITH FULLSCAN;\\n\\n-- Fix D: Switch problematic query to use legacy CE (CE70 for SQL 2014+)\\n--        when the new CE model produces worse estimates for a specific query\\n-- Per-query hint (non-invasive):\\n-- SELECT ... OPTION (USE HINT ('FORCE_LEGACY_CARDINALITY_ESTIMATION'));\\n-- Database-level (impacts all queries — use with caution):\\n-- ALTER DATABASE SCOPED CONFIGURATION SET LEGACY_CARDINALITY_ESTIMATION = ON;\\n\\n-- Fix E: Use OPTION (OPTIMIZE FOR) for parameter-sensitive queries\\n--        where optimizer uses a non-representative parameter value at compile time\\n-- SELECT ... WHERE OrderDate > @date OPTION (OPTIMIZE FOR (@date = '2024-01-01'));\\n-- Or for unknown value:\\n-- SELECT ... WHERE OrderDate > @date OPTION (OPTIMIZE FOR (@date UNKNOWN));\\n\\n-- Fix F: Force a specific good plan via Query Store for persistent CE regressions\\nEXEC sys.sp_query_store_force_plan @query_id = <query_id>, @plan_id = <good_plan_id>;", "parameters": {}, "description": "Based on the CE warning type identified in step 1, apply the appropriate fix: eliminate type conversions, create missing statistics, update stale statistics with FULLSCAN, or force a good plan via Query Store when the CE model consistently fails."}	{"description": "-- Unforce Query Store plan:\\nEXEC sys.sp_query_store_unforce_plan @query_id = <query_id>, @plan_id = <plan_id>;\\n-- Remove LEGACY_CARDINALITY_ESTIMATION:\\nALTER DATABASE SCOPED CONFIGURATION SET LEGACY_CARDINALITY_ESTIMATION = OFF;\\n-- Drop manual statistics:\\nDROP STATISTICS dbo.TableName.ST_TableName_ColumnName;"}	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.410708
2422	sqlserver	execute	Rebuild or reorganize the highest-impact fragmented indexes	{"sql": "-- Option A: REBUILD (removes fragmentation, reclaims ghost records, updates statistics)\\n-- Enterprise Edition with ONLINE=ON (allows concurrent DML):\\nALTER INDEX [IX_TargetIndex] ON dbo.TargetTable\\nREBUILD WITH (\\n  ONLINE = ON,\\n  FILLFACTOR = 85,\\n  SORT_IN_TEMPDB = ON,\\n  DATA_COMPRESSION = PAGE,\\n  MAXDOP = 4\\n);\\n\\n-- Standard Edition (offline, blocks concurrent DML — use maintenance window):\\n-- ALTER INDEX [IX_TargetIndex] ON dbo.TargetTable\\n-- REBUILD WITH (FILLFACTOR = 85, SORT_IN_TEMPDB = ON, MAXDOP = 2);\\n\\n-- Option B: REORGANIZE (online, incremental, lower resource usage, does not update stats)\\n-- Use for 10–30% fragmentation or when REBUILD is too resource-intensive:\\nALTER INDEX [IX_TargetIndex] ON dbo.TargetTable\\nREORGANIZE WITH (LOB_COMPACTION = ON);\\n\\n-- To rebuild ALL indexes on a table at once (Enterprise ONLINE):\\n-- ALTER INDEX ALL ON dbo.TargetTable\\n-- REBUILD WITH (ONLINE=ON, FILLFACTOR=85, SORT_IN_TEMPDB=ON);\\n\\n-- Monitor rebuild progress:\\nSELECT\\n  r.command,\\n  r.percent_complete,\\n  r.estimated_completion_time / 1000 / 60 AS est_minutes_remaining,\\n  r.wait_type\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_requests r\\nWHERE r.command LIKE 'ALTER INDEX%';", "parameters": {"maxdop": 4, "fillfactor": 85, "online_rebuild": "Enterprise Edition only", "sort_in_tempdb": true, "data_compression": "PAGE"}, "description": "Execute the rebuild (fragmentation > 30% or ghost count > 5000) or reorganize (fragmentation 10–30%) operations on the prioritized indexes. Use ONLINE=ON on Enterprise Edition to avoid blocking DML during the operation."}	{"description": "-- Index rebuilds cannot be rolled back once complete.\\n-- If the new index causes unexpected behavior, disable and rebuild with previous FILLFACTOR:\\n-- ALTER INDEX [IX_TargetIndex] ON dbo.TargetTable REBUILD WITH (FILLFACTOR=100);\\n-- This effectively reverts any page density change."}	medium	t	f	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.411454
2438	sqlserver	configure	Normalize user default schemas to dbo where safe	{"sql": "-- Change user default schema to dbo for users where schema routing is not intentional\\n-- Replace 'UserName' with each affected user identified in step 1\\nALTER USER [UserName] WITH DEFAULT_SCHEMA = dbo;\\n\\n-- Verify change\\nSELECT name, default_schema_name\\nFROM sys.database_principals\\nWHERE type IN ('S', 'U', 'G')\\n  AND name NOT IN ('guest', 'INFORMATION_SCHEMA', 'sys')\\nORDER BY name;", "parameters": {"prerequisite": "All application object references must already be schema-qualified before this step", "target_default_schema": "dbo"}, "description": "After schema-qualifying all object references, normalize remaining user default schemas to dbo. This ensures all users resolve unqualified references identically, preventing future plan proliferation even if new unqualified references are inadvertently introduced."}	{"description": "ALTER USER [UserName] WITH DEFAULT_SCHEMA = [original_schema_name];"}	medium	t	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.415117
2430	sqlserver	configure	Enable optimize for ad hoc workloads to stub single-use plans	{"sql": "-- Enable optimize for ad hoc workloads (online, no restart required)\\n-- Instead of caching the full plan on first execution, stores a lightweight stub\\n-- Full plan only cached on second execution (genuine reuse)\\nEXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1;\\nRECONFIGURE;\\nEXEC sp_configure 'optimize for ad hoc workloads', 1;\\nRECONFIGURE;\\n\\n-- Optionally flush the existing bloated cache after enabling (use during low-traffic window)\\n-- DBCC FREEPROCCACHE;  -- Uncomment only if plan cache is critically bloated\\n\\n-- Verify setting\\nSELECT name, value_in_use\\nFROM sys.configurations\\nWHERE name = 'optimize for ad hoc workloads';", "parameters": {"value": 1, "restart_required": false, "cache_flush_required": false}, "description": "Enabling this server-level option causes SQL Server to store only a plan stub on the first execution of any ad-hoc query. The full plan is only cached if the query executes a second time, preventing single-use plans from consuming buffer pool memory."}	{"description": "EXEC sp_configure 'optimize for ad hoc workloads', 0;\\nRECONFIGURE;"}	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.411752
2444	sqlserver	configure	Enable optimize for ad hoc workloads as immediate interim mitigation	{"sql": "-- Immediate mitigation: prevents single-use full plans from consuming cache\\nEXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1;\\nRECONFIGURE;\\nEXEC sp_configure 'optimize for ad hoc workloads', 1;\\nRECONFIGURE;\\n\\n-- Verify\\nSELECT name, value_in_use\\nFROM sys.configurations\\nWHERE name = 'optimize for ad hoc workloads';\\n\\n-- Monitor plan cache improvement after 1 hour\\nSELECT\\n  COUNT(*) AS total_plans,\\n  SUM(CASE WHEN usecounts = 1 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS single_use_plans,\\n  SUM(size_in_bytes) / 1048576 AS total_mb\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_cached_plans\\nWHERE objtype = 'Adhoc';", "parameters": {"value": 1, "restart_required": false}, "description": "While application consolidation work is in progress (step 2), enable 'optimize for ad hoc workloads' to immediately reduce plan cache memory pressure from single-use plans generated by multiple applications."}	{"description": "EXEC sp_configure 'optimize for ad hoc workloads', 0;\\nRECONFIGURE;"}	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.416648
3231	sqlserver	execute	Drop confirmed unused defensive indexes	{"sql": "-- After monitoring period, permanently drop each confirmed candidate.\\n-- Replace <schema>, <table>, and <index_name> with actual values.\\nDROP INDEX [<index_name>] ON [<schema>].[<table_name>];", "parameters": {}, "description": "Permanently removes the index, recovering storage and eliminating ongoing write overhead for future DML operations."}	{"description": "-- Recreate the index if query regression is observed.\\n-- Retrieve original CREATE INDEX script from sys.indexes / sys.index_columns\\n-- or from a pre-change database snapshot."}	medium	t	f	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.525878
2446	sqlserver	configure	Enable forced parameterization on the database	{"sql": "-- Enable forced parameterization for the target database\\n-- All queries will be parameterized regardless of complexity\\n-- (except those excluded by plan guides)\\nALTER DATABASE [YourDatabaseName] SET PARAMETERIZATION FORCED;\\n\\n-- Verify the change\\nSELECT name, is_parameterization_forced\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE name = 'YourDatabaseName';\\n\\n-- Monitor for 30 minutes: check forced parameterization counters\\nSELECT\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE counter_name = 'Forced Parameterizations/sec'\\n     AND instance_name = 'YourDatabaseName') AS forced_param_sec,\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE counter_name = 'Failed Forced Parameterizations/sec'\\n     AND instance_name = 'YourDatabaseName') AS failed_forced_param_sec;", "parameters": {"scope": "Database-level setting — does not affect other databases on the instance", "database_name": "Replace [YourDatabaseName] with the target database"}, "description": "Enable forced parameterization at the database level. SQL Server will parameterize all eligible queries automatically, reusing plans across literal value variations. Monitor for failed forced parameterizations (stored procedures, views, and queries with TOP, TABLESAMPLE are excluded from forced parameterization)."}	{"description": "ALTER DATABASE [YourDatabaseName] SET PARAMETERIZATION SIMPLE;"}	medium	t	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.416651
2449	sqlserver	configure	Revert database to simple parameterization mode	{"sql": "-- Revert forced parameterization to simple (default) mode\\nALTER DATABASE [YourDatabaseName] SET PARAMETERIZATION SIMPLE;\\n\\n-- Optionally flush the plan cache for this database to clear sniffed plans\\nDECLARE @dbid INT = DB_ID('YourDatabaseName');\\nDBCC FLUSHPROCINDB(@dbid);\\n\\n-- Verify the change\\nSELECT name, is_parameterization_forced\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE name = 'YourDatabaseName';\\n\\n-- Enable optimize for ad hoc workloads as a lighter-touch mitigation\\nEXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1; RECONFIGURE;\\nEXEC sp_configure 'optimize for ad hoc workloads', 1;\\nRECONFIGURE;", "parameters": {"cache_flush": "FLUSHPROCINDB clears sniffed plans allowing fresh compilation with better-representative parameter values", "database_name": "Replace [YourDatabaseName] with the target database"}, "description": "Revert the database to simple parameterization and flush sniffed plans, then enable 'optimize for ad hoc workloads' to reduce plan cache bloat from legitimate single-use queries without forcing parameterization."}	{"description": "ALTER DATABASE [YourDatabaseName] SET PARAMETERIZATION FORCED;"}	medium	t	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.416654
2450	sqlserver	execute	Apply OPTION hints or plan guides to high-variance queries	{"sql": "-- Option A: Add OPTION (OPTIMIZE FOR UNKNOWN) to queries with parameter sniffing\\n-- This instructs the optimizer to use average statistics rather than sniffed values\\n-- Apply this in stored procedures or application queries for affected query_hash groups\\nSELECT o.order_id, o.total_amount\\nFROM dbo.Orders o\\nWHERE o.customer_id = @CustomerId\\nOPTION (OPTIMIZE FOR UNKNOWN);\\n\\n-- Option B: Use OPTION (RECOMPILE) for queries where per-execution optimal plan is needed\\n-- More expensive (compiles every execution) but eliminates sniffing entirely\\nSELECT o.order_id, o.total_amount\\nFROM dbo.Orders o\\nWHERE o.customer_id = @CustomerId\\nOPTION (RECOMPILE);\\n\\n-- Option C: Create a plan guide to apply hints without modifying application code\\nEXEC sp_create_plan_guide\\n  @name = N'PG_GetOrdersByCustomer',\\n  @stmt = N'SELECT o.order_id, o.total_amount FROM dbo.Orders o WHERE o.customer_id = @CustomerId',\\n  @type = N'SQL',\\n  @module_or_batch = NULL,\\n  @params = N'@CustomerId int',\\n  @hints = N'OPTION (OPTIMIZE FOR UNKNOWN)';", "parameters": {"preferred_option": "OPTIMIZE FOR UNKNOWN (stable average-statistics plan, no per-execution recompile)", "recompile_threshold": "Use OPTION (RECOMPILE) only if the query runs less than 100 times/min — overhead is acceptable"}, "description": "For the specific high-variance queries identified in step 1, apply OPTION (OPTIMIZE FOR UNKNOWN) via stored procedure modification or plan guide to produce stable plans independent of parameter sniffing, without requiring full forced parameterization at the database level."}	{"description": "EXEC sp_drop_plan_guide N'PG_GetOrdersByCustomer';"}	low	f	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.416654
2451	sqlserver	configure	Enable optimize for ad hoc workloads instance-wide	{"sql": "-- Enable optimize for ad hoc workloads\\n-- Stores a lightweight plan stub on first execution; full plan only cached on second execution\\nEXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1;\\nRECONFIGURE;\\nEXEC sp_configure 'optimize for ad hoc workloads', 1;\\nRECONFIGURE;\\n\\n-- Verify\\nSELECT name, value_in_use\\nFROM sys.configurations\\nWHERE name = 'optimize for ad hoc workloads';\\n\\n-- Baseline current plan cache before flushing\\nSELECT\\n  COUNT(*) AS total_plans,\\n  SUM(CASE WHEN usecounts = 1 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS single_use_plans,\\n  SUM(CASE WHEN usecounts = 1 THEN CAST(size_in_bytes AS BIGINT) ELSE 0 END) / 1048576 AS single_use_mb,\\n  SUM(CAST(size_in_bytes AS BIGINT)) / 1048576 AS total_cache_mb\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_cached_plans\\nWHERE objtype = 'Adhoc';\\n\\n-- Optional: flush existing bloated cache during low-traffic window\\n-- DBCC FREEPROCCACHE;", "parameters": {"value": 1, "effect": "New single-use plans cached as stubs (~300 bytes each instead of full plan); full plan cached only on second execution", "restart_required": false}, "description": "Enable the optimize for ad hoc workloads server configuration to prevent future single-use plans from being stored as full cached plans. Existing single-use plans will naturally evict under memory pressure."}	{"description": "EXEC sp_configure 'optimize for ad hoc workloads', 0;\\nRECONFIGURE;"}	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.417618
3209	sqlserver	execute	Apply chosen strategy and drop original index	{"sql": "-- Example: Create filtered replacement index\\nCREATE INDEX [IX_table_col_filtered_v2]\\n  ON [<schema>].[<table>] ([<low_card_col>])\\n  INCLUDE ([<col1>], [<col2>])\\n  WHERE [<low_card_col>] = '<selective_value>'\\n  WITH (ONLINE = ON, SORT_IN_TEMPDB = ON);\\n\\n-- After verifying new index is used, drop the original\\nDROP INDEX [<original_index_name>] ON [<schema>].[<table>];", "parameters": {"table": "Target table", "schema": "Target schema", "low_card_col": "The low-cardinality column", "selective_value": "The specific value to filter on (Strategy B)", "original_index_name": "Name of the index being replaced"}, "description": "Creates the replacement index (if Strategy B/C/D chosen) with ONLINE=ON to avoid blocking, then drops the original low-cardinality index"}	{"description": "CREATE INDEX [<original_index_name>] ON [<schema>].[<table>] ([<low_card_col>]) WITH (ONLINE = ON);"}	medium	t	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.525863
3238	sqlserver	execute	Drop index if optimizer still avoids it after rebuild	{"sql": "-- If the index remains unused after the monitoring period, drop it.\\nDROP INDEX [<index_name>] ON [<schema>].[<table_name>];", "parameters": {}, "description": "Permanently removes an index that provides no read benefit even after fragmentation was addressed. This eliminates ongoing write maintenance cost and page split pressure."}	{"description": "-- Recreate from scripted definition if query regression is observed.\\nCREATE NONCLUSTERED INDEX [<index_name>]\\n    ON [<schema>].[<table_name>] (<key_columns>)\\n    INCLUDE (<included_columns>)\\n    WITH (FILLFACTOR = 80);"}	medium	t	f	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.525882
2927	sqlserver	configure	Adjust MAXDOP and Cost Threshold for Parallelism	{"sql": "-- Option A: Server-level MAXDOP (affects all databases)\\n-- Calculate recommended value: physical_cores_per_numa_node, capped at 8\\nEXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1;\\nRECONFIGURE;\\n\\n-- Set MAXDOP (replace 4 with physical_cores / numa_node_count, max 8)\\nEXEC sp_configure 'max degree of parallelism', 4;\\nRECONFIGURE;\\n\\n-- Raise Cost Threshold for Parallelism to prevent trivial queries going parallel\\nEXEC sp_configure 'cost threshold for parallelism', 50;\\nRECONFIGURE;\\n\\n-- Option B: Database-scoped (SQL Server 2016+, preferred for targeted tuning)\\n-- ALTER DATABASE SCOPED CONFIGURATION SET MAXDOP = 4;\\n\\n-- Verify new settings\\nSELECT name, value_in_use\\nFROM sys.configurations\\nWHERE name IN ('max degree of parallelism', 'cost threshold for parallelism');", "parameters": {"ctp_value": "50 (SQL Server default is 5, which is too low for most OLTP)", "maxdop_value": "physical cores per NUMA node, max 8 (commonly 4)"}, "description": "Reduce MAXDOP to limit parallel threads per query, minimizing cross-core cache line contention. Raise CTP to prevent low-cost queries from going parallel. Use database-scoped configuration for non-disruptive targeted changes."}	{"description": "EXEC sp_configure 'max degree of parallelism', <previous_value>;\\nRECONFIGURE;\\nEXEC sp_configure 'cost threshold for parallelism', <previous_value>;\\nRECONFIGURE;"}	medium	t	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.484011
2930	sqlserver	execute	Enable AUTO_UPDATE_STATISTICS and ASYNC on affected databases	{"sql": "-- Run for each affected database identified in step 1\\n-- Replace 'YourDatabase' with each database name\\n\\nALTER DATABASE [YourDatabase] SET AUTO_UPDATE_STATISTICS ON;\\nALTER DATABASE [YourDatabase] SET AUTO_UPDATE_STATISTICS_ASYNC ON;\\nALTER DATABASE [YourDatabase] SET AUTO_CREATE_STATISTICS ON;\\n\\n-- Verify settings applied\\nSELECT\\n  name,\\n  is_auto_update_stats_on,\\n  is_auto_update_stats_async_on,\\n  is_auto_create_stats_on\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE name = 'YourDatabase';", "parameters": {"database_name": "Each affected user database identified in step 1"}, "description": "Enable automatic statistics updates in asynchronous mode. With ASYNC ON, a query that triggers a statistics update proceeds immediately with stale statistics; a background thread performs the update for subsequent executions."}	{"description": "ALTER DATABASE [YourDatabase] SET AUTO_UPDATE_STATISTICS_ASYNC OFF;\\n-- Note: do not disable AUTO_UPDATE_STATISTICS unless it was OFF before this change"}	low	f	t	00:02:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.484013
2933	sqlserver	configure	Raise database compatibility level to 130 or higher	{"sql": "-- Raise to SQL Server 2016 behavior (dynamic stats threshold)\\n-- Test in non-production first; validate query plans for regressions\\nALTER DATABASE [YourDatabase] SET COMPATIBILITY_LEVEL = 130;\\n\\n-- Alternatively, use Query Store to shield specific queries from plan regressions\\nALTER DATABASE [YourDatabase] SET QUERY_STORE = ON;\\nALTER DATABASE [YourDatabase] SET QUERY_STORE (OPERATION_MODE = READ_WRITE,\\n  QUERY_CAPTURE_MODE = AUTO, MAX_STORAGE_SIZE_MB = 1024);\\n\\n-- Verify\\nSELECT name, compatibility_level FROM sys.databases WHERE database_id = DB_ID();", "parameters": {"database_name": "Target database", "target_compatibility_level": "130 (SQL Server 2016) or 140/150/160 matching instance version"}, "description": "Upgrade compatibility level to 130+ to activate the dynamic statistics update threshold. Enable Query Store first to capture and force good plans if any regressions occur post-upgrade."}	{"description": "ALTER DATABASE [YourDatabase] SET COMPATIBILITY_LEVEL = <previous_level>;\\n-- Use Query Store forced plans to revert specific regressed queries:\\n-- EXEC sp_query_store_force_plan @query_id = <id>, @plan_id = <old_plan_id>;"}	medium	t	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.484014
2939	sqlserver	configure	Schedule frequent filtered statistics maintenance job	{"sql": "-- Create a SQL Agent job to refresh filtered statistics every 4 hours\\n-- (Adjust schedule based on data churn rate)\\nUSE msdb;\\nGO\\n\\nEXEC sp_add_job @job_name = N'Refresh Filtered Statistics - [YourDatabase]';\\n\\nEXEC sp_add_jobstep\\n  @job_name = N'Refresh Filtered Statistics - [YourDatabase]',\\n  @step_name = N'Update filtered stats with FULLSCAN',\\n  @command = N'\\nUSE [YourDatabase];\\nDECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(MAX) = N'''';\\nSELECT @sql += N''UPDATE STATISTICS '' + QUOTENAME(OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME(s.object_id)) +\\n  N''.'' + QUOTENAME(OBJECT_NAME(s.object_id)) +\\n  N'' '' + QUOTENAME(s.name) + N'' WITH FULLSCAN;'' + CHAR(10)\\nFROM sys.stats s\\nCROSS APPLY sys.dm_db_stats_properties(s.object_id, s.stats_id) sp\\nWHERE s.has_filter = 1 AND s.object_id > 100 AND sp.modification_counter > 0;\\nEXEC sp_executesql @sql;\\n',\\n  @database_name = N'YourDatabase';\\n\\nEXEC sp_add_schedule\\n  @schedule_name = N'Every 4 hours',\\n  @freq_type = 4,\\n  @freq_interval = 1,\\n  @freq_subday_type = 8,\\n  @freq_subday_interval = 4;\\n\\nEXEC sp_attach_schedule\\n  @job_name = N'Refresh Filtered Statistics - [YourDatabase]',\\n  @schedule_name = N'Every 4 hours';\\n\\nEXEC sp_add_jobserver @job_name = N'Refresh Filtered Statistics - [YourDatabase]';", "parameters": {"frequency": "Every 4 hours (adjust to 1-2 hours for high-churn filtered columns)", "database_name": "Target database containing the filtered statistics"}, "description": "Create a recurring SQL Agent job to update filtered statistics every 4 hours. Filtered statistics are not refreshed by sp_updatestats when only the filtered partition changes, so explicit scheduling is required."}	{"description": "USE msdb;\\nEXEC sp_delete_job @job_name = N'Refresh Filtered Statistics - [YourDatabase]';"}	low	t	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.484018
2941	sqlserver	execute	Enable AUTO_CREATE_STATISTICS on databases where disabled	{"sql": "-- Enable auto-create on all user databases where currently OFF\\nSELECT\\n  'ALTER DATABASE [' + name + '] SET AUTO_CREATE_STATISTICS ON;' AS enable_cmd\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE database_id > 4\\n  AND state_desc = 'ONLINE'\\n  AND is_auto_create_stats_on = 0;\\n\\n-- Execute for each database found above\\n-- Example: ALTER DATABASE [YourDatabase] SET AUTO_CREATE_STATISTICS ON;\\n\\n-- Verify\\nSELECT name, is_auto_create_stats_on\\nFROM sys.databases\\nWHERE database_id > 4 AND state_desc = 'ONLINE';", "parameters": {}, "description": "Enable automatic statistics creation so the optimizer can generate single-column statistics on-demand for any column used in a WHERE clause predicate that lacks statistics."}	{"description": "ALTER DATABASE [YourDatabase] SET AUTO_CREATE_STATISTICS OFF;"}	low	f	t	00:02:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.484019
3018	sqlserver	execute	Reduce SQL Server I/O pressure with max async I/O configuration check	{"sql": "-- Check current advanced options; max async IO is determined by affinity mask and number of schedulers\\n-- Verify SQL Server I/O completion ports and worker thread configuration\\nSELECT\\n  name,\\n  value_in_use,\\n  description\\nFROM sys.configurations\\nWHERE name IN (\\n  'max worker threads',\\n  'lightweight pooling',\\n  'affinity I/O mask'\\n);\\n\\n-- Check for resource governor I/O configuration\\nSELECT pool_id, name, min_iops_per_volume, max_iops_per_volume\\nFROM sys.resource_governor_resource_pools\\nWHERE pool_id > 2;", "parameters": {"resource_governor": "Configure max_iops_per_volume if workload isolation is needed", "lightweight_pooling": "0 (must be OFF)"}, "description": "Verify SQL Server worker thread and I/O configuration. If Resource Governor is available (Enterprise/Developer), configure max_iops_per_volume on non-critical workload pools to prevent single workloads from saturating the queue. Ensure 'lightweight pooling' is OFF (fiber mode degrades I/O)."}	{"description": "If Resource Governor pools were modified: ALTER RESOURCE POOL with original max_iops_per_volume values, then ALTER RESOURCE GOVERNOR RECONFIGURE."}	low	f	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.500836
2942	sqlserver	execute	Manually create statistics on columns flagged in missing statistics warnings	{"sql": "-- Create statistics on specific columns identified from plan XML MissingStatistics warnings\\n-- Replace table/column names with those found during detection\\nCREATE STATISTICS [stat_YourTable_YourColumn]\\n  ON [dbo].[YourTable] ([YourColumn])\\n  WITH FULLSCAN;\\n\\n-- For compound columns (multi-column predicates):\\n-- CREATE STATISTICS [stat_YourTable_Col1_Col2]\\n--   ON [dbo].[YourTable] ([Col1], [Col2])\\n--   WITH FULLSCAN;\\n\\n-- Verify statistics created\\nSELECT\\n  OBJECT_NAME(s.object_id) AS table_name,\\n  s.name AS stats_name,\\n  sp.rows,\\n  sp.rows_sampled,\\n  CAST(sp.rows_sampled * 100.0 / NULLIF(sp.rows, 0) AS decimal(5,2)) AS sample_pct,\\n  sp.last_updated\\nFROM sys.stats s\\nCROSS APPLY sys.dm_db_stats_properties(s.object_id, s.stats_id) sp\\nWHERE OBJECT_NAME(s.object_id) = 'YourTable'\\nORDER BY sp.last_updated DESC;", "parameters": {"scan_mode": "FULLSCAN for tables under 10M rows; SAMPLE 50 PERCENT for larger tables", "column_targets": "Columns identified from MissingStatistics warnings in detection step 3"}, "description": "Create statistics explicitly on columns that have missing statistics warnings in query plans. Use FULLSCAN for maximum accuracy on newly-added or low-density columns."}	{"description": "DROP STATISTICS [dbo].[YourTable].[stat_YourTable_YourColumn];"}	low	f	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.48402
2945	sqlserver	configure	Create maintenance plan to preserve high-sample statistics	{"sql": "-- Create a SQL Agent job that runs FULLSCAN on skew-sensitive tables weekly\\n-- (Adjust frequency based on data churn rate)\\nUSE msdb;\\n\\nEXEC sp_add_job @job_name = N'High-Sample Statistics Rebuild - [YourDatabase]';\\n\\nEXEC sp_add_jobstep\\n  @job_name = N'High-Sample Statistics Rebuild - [YourDatabase]',\\n  @step_name = N'Rebuild stats with FULLSCAN on skew-sensitive tables',\\n  @command = N'\\nUSE [YourDatabase];\\n-- Update specific high-priority statistics with FULLSCAN\\nUPDATE STATISTICS [dbo].[YourTable1] WITH FULLSCAN;\\nUPDATE STATISTICS [dbo].[YourTable2] WITH SAMPLE 50 PERCENT;\\n-- Add more tables as needed\\n',\\n  @database_name = N'YourDatabase';\\n\\nEXEC sp_add_schedule\\n  @schedule_name = N'Weekly Sunday 2AM',\\n  @freq_type = 8,\\n  @freq_interval = 1,\\n  @active_start_time = 20000;\\n\\nEXEC sp_attach_schedule\\n  @job_name = N'High-Sample Statistics Rebuild - [YourDatabase]',\\n  @schedule_name = N'Weekly Sunday 2AM';\\n\\nEXEC sp_add_jobserver\\n  @job_name = N'High-Sample Statistics Rebuild - [YourDatabase]';", "parameters": {"schedule": "Weekly during off-peak maintenance window", "table_priority": "Tables with skewed distributions on frequently filtered columns"}, "description": "Schedule weekly FULLSCAN statistics rebuilds on skew-sensitive tables to prevent automatic updates from reverting to low sample rates. This preserves the high-accuracy histograms created in step 1."}	{"description": "USE msdb;\\nEXEC sp_delete_job @job_name = N'High-Sample Statistics Rebuild - [YourDatabase]';"}	low	t	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.488339
2948	sqlserver	execute	Create new multi-column statistics for correlated join columns lacking coverage	{"sql": "-- Create multi-column statistics for column pairs used together in JOIN or WHERE\\n-- Identify join patterns from high-CPU-variance queries found in detection\\n-- Example: table with (CustomerID, OrderDate) used together frequently\\nCREATE STATISTICS [stat_YourTable_Col1_Col2]\\n  ON [dbo].[YourTable] ([Col1], [Col2])\\n  WITH FULLSCAN;\\n\\n-- Example: (StatusCode, RegionCode) used in compound WHERE clauses\\nCREATE STATISTICS [stat_YourTable_StatusCode_RegionCode]\\n  ON [dbo].[YourTable] ([StatusCode], [RegionCode])\\n  WITH FULLSCAN;\\n\\n-- Verify all multi-column stats on target tables\\nSELECT\\n  OBJECT_NAME(s.object_id) AS table_name,\\n  s.name AS stats_name,\\n  (\\n    SELECT STRING_AGG(COL_NAME(sc.object_id, sc.column_id), ', ')\\n    WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY sc.stats_column_id)\\n    FROM sys.stats_columns sc\\n    WHERE sc.object_id = s.object_id AND sc.stats_id = s.stats_id\\n  ) AS column_list,\\n  sp.last_updated,\\n  sp.rows,\\n  sp.rows_sampled\\nFROM sys.stats s\\nCROSS APPLY sys.dm_db_stats_properties(s.object_id, s.stats_id) sp\\nWHERE s.object_id > 100\\n  AND (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sys.stats_columns sc WHERE sc.object_id = s.object_id AND sc.stats_id = s.stats_id) > 1\\nORDER BY OBJECT_NAME(s.object_id), s.name;", "parameters": {"column_pairs": "Identify from high-variance query plans showing estimated rows far from actual rows on join operations"}, "description": "Create multi-column statistics for correlated column pairs that are used together in predicates but lack dedicated multi-column stats. Focus on columns that appear together in JOIN conditions and compound WHERE clauses."}	{"description": "DROP STATISTICS [dbo].[YourTable].[stat_YourTable_Col1_Col2];"}	low	f	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.488341
2953	sqlserver	execute	Create statistics on non-leading index key columns lacking coverage	{"sql": "-- Generate CREATE STATISTICS statements for non-leading index key columns without stats\\nDECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(MAX) = N'';\\n\\nSELECT DISTINCT @sql +=\\n  N'CREATE STATISTICS [stat_' +\\n  REPLACE(OBJECT_NAME(i.object_id), ' ', '_') + N'_' +\\n  COL_NAME(ic.object_id, ic.column_id) +\\n  N'] ON ' +\\n  QUOTENAME(OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME(i.object_id)) + N'.' +\\n  QUOTENAME(OBJECT_NAME(i.object_id)) +\\n  N' (' + QUOTENAME(COL_NAME(ic.object_id, ic.column_id)) +\\n  N') WITH FULLSCAN;' + CHAR(10)\\nFROM sys.indexes i\\nJOIN sys.index_columns ic ON i.object_id = ic.object_id AND i.index_id = ic.index_id\\nJOIN sys.partitions p ON i.object_id = p.object_id AND p.index_id IN (0, 1)\\nWHERE i.object_id > 100\\n  AND ic.key_ordinal > 1\\n  AND p.rows > 10000\\n  AND NOT EXISTS (\\n    SELECT 1 FROM sys.stats_columns sc\\n    JOIN sys.stats s ON sc.object_id = s.object_id AND sc.stats_id = s.stats_id\\n    WHERE sc.object_id = ic.object_id\\n      AND sc.column_id = ic.column_id\\n      AND sc.stats_column_id = 1\\n  );\\n\\nPRINT @sql;\\n-- EXEC sp_executesql @sql; -- Uncomment after review\\n\\n-- Verify statistics created\\nSELECT\\n  OBJECT_NAME(s.object_id) AS table_name,\\n  s.name AS stats_name,\\n  sp.last_updated,\\n  sp.rows,\\n  sp.rows_sampled,\\n  CAST(sp.rows_sampled * 100.0 / NULLIF(sp.rows, 0) AS decimal(5,2)) AS sample_pct\\nFROM sys.stats s\\nCROSS APPLY sys.dm_db_stats_properties(s.object_id, s.stats_id) sp\\nWHERE s.name LIKE 'stat_%'\\nORDER BY sp.last_updated DESC;", "parameters": {}, "description": "Generate and execute CREATE STATISTICS WITH FULLSCAN statements for all non-leading index columns lacking dedicated stats. Print the SQL first for review before executing. FULLSCAN ensures accurate histograms, especially for selective or skewed columns."}	{"description": "-- Drop auto-created statistics if they cause issues\\n-- DROP STATISTICS [dbo].[YourTable].[stat_YourTable_YourColumn];"}	low	f	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.488346
3017	sqlserver	configure	Increase HBA queue depth and verify disk scheduler settings	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"windows_disk_policy": "None for NVMe; WriteCacheEnabled for SAS/SATA data volumes; WriteCacheDisabled for log volumes without BBU", "recommended_queue_depth": "32-64 per HBA path for SAN/iSCSI"}, "description": "Work with the storage/OS team to increase the HBA (Host Bus Adapter) queue depth for the LUNs hosting SQL Server files. On Windows Server, the recommended queue depth for SAN-attached LUNs is 32-64 per path. Verify via: Device Manager > Disk Drives > Properties > Advanced. For iSCSI, adjust MaxRequestHoldTime and LinkDownTime in iSCSI initiator properties. Confirm Windows disk scheduler is set to 'None' (NVMe) or appropriate policy (not 'Read Cache' for log volumes). Run: Get-PhysicalDisk | Select FriendlyName, OperationalStatus, BusType | Format-Table in PowerShell to enumerate storage paths."}	{"description": "Revert HBA queue depth to previous value via Device Manager or storage management console. Document original value before changing."}	medium	t	t	01:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.500835
3024	sqlserver	execute	Enable delayed durability for high-commit-rate databases during rebuild window	{"sql": "-- CAUTION: Delayed durability risks losing up to last ~60 seconds of committed transactions on crash\\n-- Only enable for databases where this risk is acceptable (OLAP, reporting, dev/test)\\n-- First check which databases have high write stalls\\nSELECT d.name,\\n  vfs.io_stall_write_ms / NULLIF(vfs.num_of_writes, 0) AS avg_write_ms,\\n  d.delayed_durability_desc\\nFROM sys.databases d\\nJOIN sys.dm_io_virtual_file_stats(NULL, NULL) vfs ON d.database_id = vfs.database_id\\nJOIN sys.master_files mf ON vfs.database_id = mf.database_id AND vfs.file_id = mf.file_id\\nWHERE mf.type = 1 AND d.database_id > 4\\nORDER BY avg_write_ms DESC;\\n\\n-- Enable for approved databases only:\\n-- ALTER DATABASE [YourDatabase] SET DELAYED_DURABILITY = FORCED;\\n-- To revert after rebuild completes:\\n-- ALTER DATABASE [YourDatabase] SET DELAYED_DURABILITY = DISABLED;", "parameters": {"revert_after": "DISABLED once rebuild confirmed complete by storage team", "delayed_durability_mode": "FORCED (for acceptable-risk databases only)"}, "description": "For databases where the brief data loss risk is acceptable (OLAP, reporting, analytics), enable FORCED delayed durability to batch log flushes and reduce write pressure on the array during the rebuild window. This is a temporary mitigation — must be reverted after rebuild completes."}	{"description": "ALTER DATABASE [YourDatabase] SET DELAYED_DURABILITY = DISABLED; -- Run for each database that was modified. Execute immediately if rebuild completes or if any data integrity concern arises."}	medium	t	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.500841
3025	sqlserver	execute	Defer maintenance jobs and reduce non-critical I/O during rebuild	{"sql": "-- Identify and disable non-critical SQL Agent jobs during rebuild window\\nSELECT j.job_id, j.name, j.enabled, j.description\\nFROM msdb.dbo.sysjobs j\\nJOIN msdb.dbo.sysjobschedules js ON j.job_id = js.job_id\\nWHERE j.enabled = 1\\n  AND j.name LIKE '%Index%' OR j.name LIKE '%Rebuild%'\\n  OR j.name LIKE '%Statistics%' OR j.name LIKE '%Backup%'\\nORDER BY j.name;\\n\\n-- Disable specific maintenance jobs during rebuild window:\\n-- EXEC msdb.dbo.sp_update_job @job_name = 'IndexMaintenanceJob', @enabled = 0;\\n\\n-- Check for running index rebuild or reorg operations\\nSELECT session_id, command, percent_complete, estimated_completion_time, wait_type\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_requests\\nWHERE command IN ('ALTER INDEX', 'DBCC DBREINDEX', 'DBCC INDEXDEFRAG');", "parameters": {}, "description": "Disable or postpone scheduled index maintenance, statistics updates, and database backups for the duration of the RAID rebuild. These operations generate significant sequential I/O that compounds rebuild contention. Re-enable all jobs immediately after rebuild completion."}	{"description": "Re-enable all disabled SQL Agent jobs: EXEC msdb.dbo.sp_update_job @job_name = 'JobName', @enabled = 1; -- Run for each disabled job. Also run delayed durability revert if enabled in step 2."}	low	f	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.500842
3032	sqlserver	execute	Increase max server memory to reduce buffer pool eviction	{"sql": "-- Assess current memory state\\nSELECT\\n  (SELECT value_in_use FROM sys.configurations WHERE name = 'max server memory (MB)') AS current_max_memory_mb,\\n  (SELECT physical_memory_kb FROM sys.dm_os_sys_info) / 1024 AS total_physical_mb,\\n  (SELECT available_physical_memory_kb FROM sys.dm_os_sys_info) / 1024 AS available_physical_mb,\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE object_name LIKE '%Buffer Manager%' AND counter_name = 'Page life expectancy') AS current_ple,\\n  (SELECT cntr_value FROM sys.dm_os_performance_counters\\n   WHERE object_name LIKE '%Buffer Manager%' AND counter_name = 'Lazy writes/sec') AS lazy_writes_sec;\\n\\n-- Calculate recommended max server memory\\n-- Formula: total_physical_MB - MAX(4096, total_physical_MB * 0.10)\\n-- Apply new value:\\n-- EXEC sp_configure 'show advanced options', 1; RECONFIGURE;\\n-- EXEC sp_configure 'max server memory (MB)', <calculated_value>; RECONFIGURE;", "parameters": {"formula": "max_server_memory = total_physical_MB - MAX(4096, total_physical_MB * 0.10)", "apply_command": "EXEC sp_configure 'max server memory (MB)', <value>; RECONFIGURE;"}, "description": "Calculate the optimal max server memory value and apply it. Leave at least 4GB or 10% of physical RAM (whichever is larger) for the OS and other processes. This is the primary fix for memory pressure — increasing the buffer pool reduces physical I/O by keeping more pages in cache."}	{"description": "EXEC sp_configure 'max server memory (MB)', <original_value>; RECONFIGURE; -- Revert if OS memory pressure or system instability is observed."}	low	f	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.500847
5627	postgresql	instruct	Rebuild backup catalog or take new backup with metadata	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "Restore metadata: (1) For pgBackRest: try pgbackrest --stanza=main stanza-upgrade to rebuild catalog. (2) For Barman: barman rebuild-xlogdb to rebuild WAL catalog. (3) If backup_label is missing from a physical backup, it cannot be restored — take a new backup. (4) For pg_dump backups: metadata is embedded in the dump — use pg_restore --list to verify contents. (5) Take a new full backup with complete metadata as the safest option."}	\N	medium	t	f	30-120 minutes	2026-02-25 08:59:43.77256
3033	sqlserver	execute	Identify and resolve top memory grant consumers causing RESOURCE_SEMAPHORE waits	{"sql": "-- Find queries consuming largest memory grants\\nSELECT TOP 10\\n  qs.total_grant_kb / qs.execution_count AS avg_grant_kb,\\n  qs.total_grant_kb,\\n  qs.execution_count,\\n  qs.total_elapsed_time / qs.execution_count AS avg_elapsed_us,\\n  DB_NAME(qp.dbid) AS database_name,\\n  SUBSTRING(qt.text, (qs.statement_start_offset/2)+1,\\n    ((CASE qs.statement_end_offset WHEN -1 THEN DATALENGTH(qt.text)\\n      ELSE qs.statement_end_offset END - qs.statement_start_offset)/2)+1) AS query_text\\nFROM sys.dm_exec_query_stats qs\\nCROSS APPLY sys.dm_exec_sql_text(qs.sql_handle) qt\\nCROSS APPLY sys.dm_exec_query_plan(qs.plan_handle) qp\\nWHERE qs.total_grant_kb > 102400 -- > 100 MB grants\\nORDER BY avg_grant_kb DESC;\\n\\n-- Check for stale statistics causing inflated memory grants\\nSELECT OBJECT_SCHEMA_NAME(s.object_id) + '.' + OBJECT_NAME(s.object_id) AS table_name,\\n  s.name AS stat_name,\\n  sp.last_updated,\\n  sp.rows,\\n  sp.rows_sampled,\\n  sp.modification_counter\\nFROM sys.stats s\\nCROSS APPLY sys.dm_db_stats_properties(s.object_id, s.stats_id) sp\\nWHERE sp.modification_counter > sp.rows * 0.2\\n  AND sp.rows > 10000\\nORDER BY sp.modification_counter DESC;", "parameters": {}, "description": "Identify queries with excessively large memory grants (typically caused by stale statistics leading to cardinality estimation errors). Update statistics on tables with high modification_counter. For queries with legitimate large grants, review execution plans for missing indexes that could eliminate sort/hash operations."}	{"description": "Statistics updates are not reversible but are safe — they improve estimation accuracy. If query plan changes after statistics update cause regression, use OPTION(USE HINT('FORCE_LEGACY_CARDINALITY_ESTIMATION')) as a temporary workaround."}	low	f	f	01:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.500848
31725	mariadb	verify	mysql_verify_grant_restricted	{"sql": "SELECT User, Host, Grant_priv, Create_user_priv FROM mysql.user WHERE (Grant_priv = 'Y' OR Create_user_priv = 'Y') ORDER BY User;", "parameters": [], "description": "Verify only authorized administrators retain GRANT and CREATE USER privileges"}	\N	low	f	t	seconds	2026-05-11 22:17:39.229791
3042	sqlserver	execute	Pre-allocate log files to eliminate autogrowth events and set instant file initialization context	{"sql": "-- Check if Instant File Initialization is enabled (helps with data files only, not log)\\n-- Log files always zero-initialized; pre-sizing avoids mid-workload zero-fill latency\\nSELECT\\n  d.name,\\n  mf.physical_name,\\n  mf.size * 8 / 1024 AS current_size_mb,\\n  mf.growth AS autogrowth_setting,\\n  mf.is_percent_growth\\nFROM sys.master_files mf\\nJOIN sys.databases d ON mf.database_id = d.database_id\\nWHERE mf.type = 1 AND d.database_id > 4;\\n\\n-- Pre-size log file to avoid autogrowth (use based on typical daily log consumption)\\n-- Recommended: set to 120-150% of peak daily log generation\\n-- ALTER DATABASE [DatabaseName]\\n--   MODIFY FILE (NAME = [LogicalLogName], SIZE = <target_size_mb>MB, GROWTH = <increment_mb>MB);\\n\\n-- Set autogrowth in MB (not %) to predictable fixed increments:\\n-- ALTER DATABASE [DatabaseName]\\n--   MODIFY FILE (NAME = [LogicalLogName], GROWTH = 1024MB);", "parameters": {"recommended_log_size": "120-150% of peak daily log generation volume", "recommended_autogrowth": "Fixed MB increments (e.g., 512MB or 1024MB); never use % for log files"}, "description": "Pre-allocate log files to their expected working size to prevent autogrowth events during peak workload. Log file zero-initialization cannot be skipped (unlike data files), so autogrowth during peak hours causes write latency spikes. Also convert autogrowth from percentage to fixed MB increments for predictable behavior."}	{"description": "Log file size increases cannot be reversed directly (SHRINKFILE can reduce if VLFs allow, but is generally not recommended). This change is low-risk as it only pre-allocates space."}	low	f	f	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.500854
3046	sqlserver	execute	Enable delayed durability to reduce log buffer flush frequency	{"sql": "-- Enable delayed durability to allow log buffer to accumulate before flushing\\n-- FORCED: all transactions use delayed durability (max ~60s data loss risk on crash)\\n-- ALLOWED: per-transaction opt-in with COMMIT WITH (DELAYED_DURABILITY = ON)\\n\\n-- Check current setting\\nSELECT name, delayed_durability_desc FROM sys.databases WHERE database_id > 4;\\n\\n-- Apply for acceptable-risk databases:\\n-- ALTER DATABASE [DatabaseName] SET DELAYED_DURABILITY = FORCED;\\n\\n-- Verify LOGBUFFER wait reduction (compare after workload runs for 30+ minutes):\\nSELECT wait_type,\\n  waiting_tasks_count,\\n  wait_time_ms / NULLIF(waiting_tasks_count, 0) AS avg_wait_ms\\nFROM sys.dm_os_wait_stats\\nWHERE wait_type IN ('LOGBUFFER', 'WRITELOG')\\nORDER BY wait_time_ms DESC;", "parameters": {"max_data_loss": "~60 seconds of committed transactions on unexpected server crash", "delayed_durability_mode": "FORCED for acceptable-risk databases; ALLOWED for mixed workloads"}, "description": "Delayed durability causes SQL Server to return COMMIT to the application before flushing the log buffer to disk. The in-memory log buffer is flushed asynchronously when full or on a timer. This directly reduces LOGBUFFER wait frequency since the buffer is less contended — flushes happen on a schedule rather than per-commit. Most effective when combined with application batching from step 2."}	{"description": "ALTER DATABASE [DatabaseName] SET DELAYED_DURABILITY = DISABLED; -- Immediately reverts to synchronous commits for all new transactions."}	medium	t	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.500858
3081	sqlserver	configure	Set server-level recovery interval to 1 minute as a safety floor	{"sql": "-- Set recovery interval to 1 minute (used for databases without indirect checkpoint)\\nEXEC sp_configure 'recovery interval (min)', 1;\\nRECONFIGURE;\\n-- Verify\\nSELECT name, value_in_use FROM sys.configurations\\nWHERE name = 'recovery interval (min)';", "parameters": {"recovery_interval": "1 (minute)"}, "description": "The server-level recovery interval caps how long automatic checkpoint waits. Setting it to 1 minute ensures databases not yet converted to indirect checkpoint will checkpoint at most every minute rather than 5-60 minutes. This is a minimal safety setting; per-database indirect checkpoint (next step) is the primary fix."}	{"description": "EXEC sp_configure 'recovery interval (min)', 0;\\nRECONFIGURE;"}	low	f	t	00:01:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.505705
3082	sqlserver	configure	Enable indirect checkpoint on all affected user databases	{"sql": "-- Apply indirect checkpoint to all user databases with 0 or very high target_recovery_time\\nDECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(500);\\nDECLARE db_cur CURSOR FOR\\n  SELECT name FROM sys.databases\\n  WHERE database_id > 4\\n    AND state_desc = 'ONLINE'\\n    AND (target_recovery_time_in_seconds = 0\\n      OR target_recovery_time_in_seconds > 120);\\nOPEN db_cur;\\nFETCH NEXT FROM db_cur INTO @sql;\\nWHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0\\nBEGIN\\n  EXEC('ALTER DATABASE [' + @sql + '] SET TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME = 60 SECONDS;');\\n  PRINT 'Set indirect checkpoint on: ' + @sql;\\n  FETCH NEXT FROM db_cur INTO @sql;\\nEND;\\nCLOSE db_cur;\\nDEALLOCATE db_cur;\\n-- Verify results\\nSELECT name, target_recovery_time_in_seconds\\nFROM sys.databases WHERE database_id > 4 ORDER BY name;", "parameters": {"TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME": "60 seconds — adjust per database based on acceptable recovery time vs. I/O budget"}, "description": "Indirect checkpoint with a 60-second target causes SQL Server to continuously flush dirty pages in small increments rather than accumulating them until a large automatic checkpoint fires. This is the primary fix for infrequent checkpoint accumulation."}	{"description": "-- Revert all databases to automatic checkpoint\\nDECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(500);\\nDECLARE db_cur CURSOR FOR\\n  SELECT name FROM sys.databases WHERE database_id > 4 AND state_desc = 'ONLINE';\\nOPEN db_cur; FETCH NEXT FROM db_cur INTO @sql;\\nWHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0\\nBEGIN\\n  EXEC('ALTER DATABASE [' + @sql + '] SET TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME = 0;');\\n  FETCH NEXT FROM db_cur INTO @sql;\\nEND;\\nCLOSE db_cur; DEALLOCATE db_cur;"}	low	f	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.505706
3084	sqlserver	configure	Enable indirect checkpoint to spread dirty page writes continuously	{"sql": "-- Enable indirect checkpoint on databases with high dirty page accumulation\\nDECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(500);\\nDECLARE db_cur CURSOR FOR\\n  SELECT name FROM sys.databases\\n  WHERE database_id > 4 AND state_desc = 'ONLINE'\\n    AND target_recovery_time_in_seconds = 0;\\nOPEN db_cur;\\nFETCH NEXT FROM db_cur INTO @sql;\\nWHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0\\nBEGIN\\n  EXEC('ALTER DATABASE [' + @sql + '] SET TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME = 60 SECONDS;');\\n  FETCH NEXT FROM db_cur INTO @sql;\\nEND;\\nCLOSE db_cur; DEALLOCATE db_cur;\\nSELECT name, target_recovery_time_in_seconds FROM sys.databases WHERE database_id > 4;", "parameters": {"TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME": "60 seconds"}, "description": "Indirect checkpoint forces SQL Server to continuously flush dirty pages in the background, preventing the buffer pool from accumulating large volumes of unwritten pages that trigger a massive burst write at checkpoint time."}	{"description": "-- Revert to automatic checkpoint\\nDECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(500);\\nDECLARE db_cur CURSOR FOR SELECT name FROM sys.databases WHERE database_id > 4 AND state_desc = 'ONLINE';\\nOPEN db_cur; FETCH NEXT FROM db_cur INTO @sql;\\nWHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0 BEGIN EXEC('ALTER DATABASE [' + @sql + '] SET TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME = 0;'); FETCH NEXT FROM db_cur INTO @sql; END;\\nCLOSE db_cur; DEALLOCATE db_cur;"}	low	f	t	00:02:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.508613
31726	mariadb	diagnose	mysql_check_default_accounts	{"sql": "SELECT User, Host, plugin, authentication_string = '' AS empty_password, account_locked FROM mysql.user WHERE User IN ('root','admin','') OR authentication_string = '' ORDER BY User;", "parameters": [], "description": "Identify accounts with default, empty, or no passwords"}	\N	low	f	t	seconds	2026-05-11 22:17:39.229791
3085	sqlserver	configure	Verify max server memory is not artificially constraining the buffer pool	{"sql": "-- Check current memory allocation vs. physical RAM\\nSELECT\\n  (SELECT value_in_use FROM sys.configurations WHERE name = 'max server memory (MB)') AS max_srv_mem_mb,\\n  (SELECT physical_memory_in_use_kb / 1024 FROM sys.dm_os_process_memory) AS sql_mem_in_use_mb,\\n  (SELECT available_physical_memory_kb / 1024 FROM sys.dm_os_sys_memory) AS os_free_mem_mb,\\n  (SELECT total_physical_memory_kb / 1024 FROM sys.dm_os_sys_memory) AS total_physical_mb,\\n  (SELECT COUNT(*) * 8 / 1024 FROM sys.dm_os_buffer_descriptors) AS buffer_pool_mb;\\n-- If max server memory is set too low, raise it (leave 4-10 GB for OS)\\n-- EXEC sp_configure 'max server memory (MB)', <new_value>; RECONFIGURE;", "parameters": {"recommended_max_server_memory": "total_physical_mb - 4096 (for servers < 64 GB) or total_physical_mb - 10240 (for servers >= 64 GB)"}, "description": "If max server memory is set so low that the buffer pool cannot hold the working set, dirty pages get evicted before they can be written in orderly fashion, causing a thundering herd at checkpoint time. Ensure buffer pool has room to absorb write bursts. Leave at least 4 GB for OS on servers with < 64 GB RAM."}	{"description": "-- Revert max server memory to previous value\\nEXEC sp_configure 'max server memory (MB)', <previous_value>;\\nRECONFIGURE;"}	medium	t	t	00:05:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.508616
3088	sqlserver	configure	Increase TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME to reduce background writer pressure	{"sql": "-- Increase target recovery time for databases with aggressively short values\\nSELECT name, target_recovery_time_in_seconds FROM sys.databases\\nWHERE database_id > 4 AND target_recovery_time_in_seconds BETWEEN 1 AND 29;\\n-- Apply to each affected database (adjust <db> and <new_target> as appropriate)\\nALTER DATABASE [<db>] SET TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME = 60 SECONDS;\\n-- 60 seconds is the recommended default for most OLTP workloads\\n-- For write-intensive databases under sustained pressure, 30-45 seconds may be appropriate\\n-- Verify\\nSELECT name, target_recovery_time_in_seconds FROM sys.databases WHERE database_id > 4;", "parameters": {"db": "Each affected database name", "new_target": "60 seconds (recommended default); 30-45 for write-heavy databases"}, "description": "Raising TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME from an aggressive value (e.g., 5-15 seconds) to 60 seconds allows the background writer to spread I/O over a longer window, reducing the continuous write pressure on the storage subsystem and freeing I/O bandwidth for user query reads."}	{"description": "-- Revert to previous aggressive value\\nALTER DATABASE [<db>] SET TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME = <original_value> SECONDS;"}	low	f	t	00:02:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.508618
3092	sqlserver	configure	Enable indirect checkpoint to complement write-through I/O with steady application flushes	{"sql": "-- Enable indirect checkpoint on all user databases\\nDECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(500);\\nDECLARE db_cur CURSOR FOR\\n  SELECT name FROM sys.databases\\n  WHERE database_id > 4 AND state_desc = 'ONLINE' AND target_recovery_time_in_seconds = 0;\\nOPEN db_cur;\\nFETCH NEXT FROM db_cur INTO @sql;\\nWHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0\\nBEGIN\\n  EXEC('ALTER DATABASE [' + @sql + '] SET TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME = 60 SECONDS;');\\n  PRINT 'Enabled indirect checkpoint: ' + @sql;\\n  FETCH NEXT FROM db_cur INTO @sql;\\nEND;\\nCLOSE db_cur; DEALLOCATE db_cur;\\n-- Verify\\nSELECT name, target_recovery_time_in_seconds FROM sys.databases WHERE database_id > 4;", "parameters": {}, "description": "Indirect checkpoint ensures SQL Server proactively flushes dirty pages continuously at the application layer, reducing the volume of dirty data that reaches the OS write buffer. This complements write-through I/O by smoothing the write pattern rather than submitting large batches."}	{"description": "DECLARE @sql NVARCHAR(500);\\nDECLARE db_cur CURSOR FOR SELECT name FROM sys.databases WHERE database_id > 4 AND state_desc = 'ONLINE';\\nOPEN db_cur; FETCH NEXT FROM db_cur INTO @sql;\\nWHILE @@FETCH_STATUS = 0 BEGIN EXEC('ALTER DATABASE [' + @sql + '] SET TARGET_RECOVERY_TIME = 0;'); FETCH NEXT FROM db_cur INTO @sql; END;\\nCLOSE db_cur; DEALLOCATE db_cur;"}	low	f	t	00:02:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.50862
