2319	sqlserver	configure	Disable non-standard update mechanisms and configure all nodes for the approved mechanism	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"registry_path": "HKLM:\\\\SOFTWARE\\\\Policies\\\\Microsoft\\\\Windows\\\\WindowsUpdate", "approved_mechanism": "WSUS / SCCM / Manual (select one and document)"}, "description": "For each node not using the approved mechanism:\\n\\n1. DISABLE the current non-standard mechanism:\\n   - WSUS (if switching away): Remove or update HKLM:\\\\SOFTWARE\\\\Policies\\\\Microsoft\\\\Windows\\\\WindowsUpdate\\n   - Cron/Task Scheduler: Disable-ScheduledTask -TaskName '<patch_task>'\\n   - SCCM client: Consult SCCM admin to exclude node from SQL Server Software Update Group\\n\\n2. ENABLE the approved mechanism:\\n   - For WSUS: Set WUServer registry key to point to the approved WSUS server\\n   - For SCCM: Work with SCCM admin to add node to the correct SQL Server patch collection\\n   - For manual: Document the process — installer from \\\\\\\\<fileserver>\\\\SQLPatches\\\\, applied by DBA team\\n\\n3. Apply this configuration change via Group Policy where possible to prevent recurrence."}	{"description": "Restore the previous registry settings if the approved mechanism cannot reach the node. Do not leave a node without any update mechanism — it will fall further behind."}	low	t	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389677
2322	sqlserver	instruct	Reclaim disk space on the constrained volume	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"bootstrap_log_path": "%ProgramFiles%\\\\Microsoft SQL Server\\\\<version>\\\\Setup Bootstrap\\\\Log", "minimum_free_space_gb": 8}, "description": "On the Windows host, free space using the following approaches (in order of risk, lowest first):\\n\\n1. Clear SQL Server Setup Bootstrap logs (safe):\\n   Remove-Item -Path '$env:ProgramFiles\\\\Microsoft SQL Server\\\\*\\\\Setup Bootstrap\\\\Log\\\\*' -Recurse -Force\\n   (Keep the most recent log folder for troubleshooting reference.)\\n\\n2. Clear Windows Update cache (safe):\\n   Stop-Service wuauserv\\n   Remove-Item -Path 'C:\\\\Windows\\\\SoftwareDistribution\\\\Download\\\\*' -Recurse -Force\\n   Start-Service wuauserv\\n\\n3. Run Windows Disk Cleanup (safe):\\n   cleanmgr /sagerun:1\\n\\n4. Relocate SQL Server backup files to the SD card or a network share if they are on the system drive.\\n\\n5. If the system drive is critically full, work with storage team to expand the volume via disk management or LVM.\\n\\nTarget: at least 8 GB free on the system drive before retrying the installer."}	{"description": "Disk cleanup operations are generally irreversible. Do not delete SQL Server data or log files. If in doubt, move files to a backup location rather than deleting them."}	low	f	f	00:45:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389679
2331	sqlserver	instruct	Apply the overdue CU to the missed node during the catch-up window	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"log_path": "%ProgramFiles%\\\\Microsoft SQL Server\\\\<version>\\\\Setup Bootstrap\\\\Log", "failover_sql": "ALTER AVAILABILITY GROUP [<AG_name>] FAILOVER;", "installer_flags": "/quiet /action=patch /IAcceptSQLServerLicenseTerms"}, "description": "Apply the missed CU during the approved catch-up window:\\n\\n1. If the node is the primary, first perform a controlled failover:\\n   ALTER AVAILABILITY GROUP [<AG_name>] FAILOVER;\\n   Verify the new primary is serving read-write traffic before continuing.\\n\\n2. Apply the CU on this node (now a secondary) from an elevated command prompt:\\n   SQLServer<version>-KB<number>-x64.exe /quiet /action=patch /instancename=MSSQLSERVER /IAcceptSQLServerLicenseTerms\\n\\n3. Monitor the setup log at:\\n   %ProgramFiles%\\\\Microsoft SQL Server\\\\<version>\\\\Setup Bootstrap\\\\Log\\\\<timestamp>\\\\Detail.txt\\n\\n4. After the SQL Server service restarts, allow 2-5 minutes for AG reconnection.\\n\\n5. If multiple CUs were missed, apply them in sequence — do not skip CU levels."}	{"description": "If the CU installation fails and the SQL Server service does not start, restore from the pre-maintenance backup. If the service starts but the build number has not changed, check the setup log for a silent rollback and retry."}	medium	t	t	01:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389686
2345	sqlserver	instruct	Stop all SQL Server-related Windows services before patching	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"binary_path": "C:\\\\Program Files\\\\Microsoft SQL Server\\\\", "services_to_stop": ["SQLSERVERAGENT", "MSSQLSERVER", "SQLBrowser", "ReportServer", "MsDtsServer160"]}, "description": "Stop all SQL Server-related services in the correct order to release file handles: 1. Open Services.msc or use PowerShell. 2. Stop services in this order: SQL Server Agent, SQL Server Reporting Services, SQL Server Integration Services, SQL Server Browser, SQL Server (MSSQLSERVER) or named instances. Use PowerShell: Stop-Service SQLSERVERAGENT -Force; Stop-Service MSSQLSERVER -Force; Stop-Service SQLBrowser -Force. 3. For SSRS: Stop-Service ReportServer -Force. 4. For SSIS: Stop-Service MsDtsServer160 -Force. 5. After stopping, use Sysinternals Process Explorer (procexp.exe) or Handle.exe to confirm no process holds open file handles to SQL Server binary files in C:\\\\Program Files\\\\Microsoft SQL Server\\\\. Handle64.exe /accepteula -a -p sqlservr.exe should return no results after services stop. 6. If antivirus is holding file handles, disable real-time scanning on the SQL Server binary directories during the maintenance window."}	{"description": "-- After patch completes, restart services in reverse order:\\n-- Start-Service MSSQLSERVER\\n-- Start-Service SQLSERVERAGENT\\n-- Start-Service SQLBrowser\\n-- Start-Service ReportServer"}	medium	t	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389694
2346	sqlserver	execute	Verify and correct service account local administrator membership	{"sql": "-- Query service account details from SQL Server (if instance is still running)\\nSELECT servicename, service_account, startup_type_desc, status_desc\\nFROM sys.dm_server_services;\\n\\n-- After recording the service account name, run these on the OS (not in SQL):\\n-- Check local administrator membership (run in PowerShell as Administrator):\\n-- net localgroup Administrators\\n-- If the SQL Server service account (e.g., NT SERVICE\\\\MSSQLSERVER or domain\\\\sqlsvc)\\n-- is not a local admin, the patch installer may fail to write binaries.\\n-- Add it (only for patch duration if policy prohibits permanent local admin):\\n-- net localgroup Administrators \\"DOMAIN\\\\sqlsvc\\" /add\\n--\\n-- Also verify folder permissions on the SQL Server installation directory:\\n-- icacls \\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\Microsoft SQL Server\\" /t /q | findstr \\"sqlsvc\\"\\n-- The service account should have (F) or (M) on the SQL Server directories.", "parameters": {}, "description": "The SQL Server patch installer runs under the service account context in some phases. Verify the service account is a member of the local Administrators group, or that the user running setup is a local administrator. Also confirm the service account has Full Control on the SQL Server program files directory."}	{"description": "\\"-- Remove the service account from local Administrators if it was added only for patching:\\\\n-- net localgroup Administrators \\\\\\"DOMAIN\\\\\\\\sqlsvc\\\\\\" /delete\\""}	medium	t	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389695
31751	mariadb	execute	mysql_create_standard_roles	{"sql": "CREATE ROLE IF NOT EXISTS 'app_read', 'app_write', 'app_admin'; GRANT SELECT ON *.* TO 'app_read'; GRANT INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON *.* TO 'app_write'; GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON *.* TO 'app_admin';", "parameters": [], "description": "Create standard roles for read, write, and admin access patterns"}	\N	medium	t	t	seconds	2026-05-11 22:17:39.229791
31752	mariadb	execute	mysql_assign_roles_to_users	{"sql": "GRANT 'app_read' TO '<user>'@'<host>'; SET DEFAULT ROLE 'app_read' FOR '<user>'@'<host>';", "parameters": ["user", "host"], "description": "Assign appropriate roles to user accounts and set as default"}	\N	medium	t	t	seconds	2026-05-11 22:17:39.229791
2358	sqlserver	execute	Install missing prerequisites and clear pending reboot state	{"sql": "-- Check if a reboot is pending in SQL Server's view\\nSELECT vs.volume_mount_point,\\n  vs.available_bytes / 1073741824 AS free_gb\\nFROM sys.master_files mf\\nCROSS APPLY sys.dm_os_volume_stats(mf.database_id, mf.file_id) vs\\nWHERE vs.available_bytes / 1073741824 < 10\\nORDER BY vs.available_bytes;\\n\\n-- Check OS version and CLR availability\\nSELECT host_platform, host_distribution, host_release,\\n  host_service_pack_level\\nFROM sys.dm_os_host_info;\\n\\n-- After installing prerequisites on the OS, verify .NET version in SQL:\\nSELECT\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('IsFullTextInstalled') AS fulltext_installed,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('IsIntegratedSecurityOnly') AS integrated_sec,\\n  SERVERPROPERTY('IsPolyBaseInstalled') AS polybase_installed;", "parameters": {"dotnet_48_url": "https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download/dotnet-framework/net48", "requirements_reference": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/sql-server/install/hardware-and-software-requirements-for-installing-sql-server"}, "description": "Based on the identified prerequisite failure: 1) For .NET Framework: Use Windows Update or download the required .NET installer from Microsoft (e.g., .NET 4.8: https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download/dotnet-framework/net48). Install and reboot. 2) For pending reboot: Check registry key HKLM:\\\\SYSTEM\\\\CurrentControlSet\\\\Control\\\\Session Manager\\\\PendingFileRenameOperations — if it exists and is non-empty, a reboot is required. Perform the reboot before retrying the SQL Server patch. 3) For PolyBase or Full-Text prerequisites: Run the SQL Server installer in 'Add Features' mode to install the required components first. 4) For Windows version incompatibility: Review the SQL Server hardware and software requirements page for your SQL Server version."}	{"description": "-- .NET Framework cannot be uninstalled easily; plan the prerequisite install carefully.\\n-- If a .NET update causes application issues, it can be removed via Add/Remove Programs > Installed Updates."}	medium	t	f	01:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389704
2364	sqlserver	execute	Grant SQL Server service account required filesystem and registry rights	{"sql": "-- Identify current service account\\nSELECT servicename, service_account, startup_type_desc\\nFROM sys.dm_server_services;\\n\\n-- Grant full control on SQL Server binary and data directories\\n-- Run from elevated command prompt (substitute <account> and <paths>):\\n-- icacls \\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\Microsoft SQL Server\\" /grant \\"<service_account>:(OI)(CI)F\\" /T\\n-- icacls \\"<data_directory>\\" /grant \\"<service_account>:(OI)(CI)F\\" /T\\n-- icacls \\"<log_directory>\\" /grant \\"<service_account>:(OI)(CI)F\\" /T\\n\\n-- If using SQL Server Configuration Manager, reassign to NT Service\\\\MSSQLSERVER\\n-- which is self-managed by the SQL Server installer and avoids manual permission grants\\nSELECT\\n  'USE SQL Server Configuration Manager to change service account to' AS recommendation,\\n  'NT Service\\\\MSSQLSERVER (default instance)' AS preferred_account,\\n  'NT Service\\\\MSSQL$<instance> (named instance)' AS named_instance_account", "parameters": {"tool": "SQL Server Configuration Manager (SQLServerManager*.msc)", "registry_hive": "HKLM\\\\SOFTWARE\\\\Microsoft\\\\Microsoft SQL Server", "filesystem_paths": "SQL Server installation dir, data dir, log dir, backup dir, TempDB dir", "preferred_account_type": "NT Service\\\\MSSQLSERVER or NT Service\\\\MSSQL$<instance>"}, "description": "Use SQL Server Configuration Manager (never Services.msc) to change the SQL Server service account to the recommended Virtual Service Account (NT Service\\\\MSSQLSERVER). This account is automatically granted the minimum required permissions by the SQL Server installer, including filesystem ACLs and registry keys. If a domain account is required, ensure it is a member of the local Administrators group and has Full Control on the SQL Server installation, data, log, and backup directories."}	{"description": "Revert service account to the previous account using SQL Server Configuration Manager; re-apply original ACLs if changed"}	medium	t	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.389708
2369	sqlserver	instruct	Add SQL Server directory exclusions to antivirus and pause backup agents	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"av_exclusion_paths": "Installation dir, data dir, log dir, tempdb dir, backup dir, installer .exe", "av_products_common": "Windows Defender, Symantec, CrowdStrike, SentinelOne, McAfee, Trend Micro", "backup_agents_common": "Veeam, Commvault, NetBackup, Avamar, Acronis, Azure Backup", "monitoring_agents_common": "SolarWinds DPA, Zabbix, SCOM, Datadog, SentinelOne"}, "description": "Before running the CU/SP installer, perform the following steps:\\n\\n1. ANTIVIRUS EXCLUSIONS: Add permanent exclusions (not temporary disable) in your AV console for:\\n   - SQL Server installation directory: C:\\\\Program Files\\\\Microsoft SQL Server\\\\\\n   - SQL Server data directory (all .mdf, .ldf, .ndf files)\\n   - SQL Server log directory\\n   - TempDB directory\\n   - SQL Server backup directory\\n   - The CU/SP installer executable itself\\n\\n2. PAUSE BACKUP AGENTS: In your backup software console (Veeam, Commvault, NetBackup, etc.), disable or pause SQL Server backup jobs for the duration of the maintenance window. Do not just skip the next run — ensure the agent service is paused or the SQL Server VSS writer is not being invoked.\\n\\n3. MONITORING AGENTS: Temporarily pause monitoring agents (SolarWinds, Zabbix, etc.) that may query SQL Server DMVs during installation.\\n\\n4. After adding exclusions and pausing agents, wait 2-3 minutes to confirm no third-party processes are holding locks on SQL Server files."}	{"description": "Re-enable antivirus real-time scanning and resume backup agent schedules immediately after CU/SP installation completes"}	medium	t	t	00:15:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.395564
2388	sqlserver	verify	Verify all backups are readable and restorable using RESTORE VERIFYONLY	{"sql": "-- Verify each backup file is intact and readable\\n-- Run for each backup file created in Step 1:\\n\\n-- Verify master backup\\nRESTORE VERIFYONLY\\n  FROM DISK = N'<backup_path>\\\\master_pre_patch_<timestamp>.bak'\\n  WITH CHECKSUM;\\n\\n-- Verify msdb backup\\nRESTORE VERIFYONLY\\n  FROM DISK = N'<backup_path>\\\\msdb_pre_patch_<timestamp>.bak'\\n  WITH CHECKSUM;\\n\\n-- Verify a sample of user database backups\\nRESTORE VERIFYONLY\\n  FROM DISK = N'<backup_path>\\\\<user_database>_pre_patch_<timestamp>.bak'\\n  WITH CHECKSUM;\\n\\n-- Review backup history to confirm all databases have recent entries\\nSELECT\\n  database_name,\\n  backup_start_date,\\n  backup_finish_date,\\n  DATEDIFF(minute, backup_start_date, backup_finish_date) AS duration_minutes,\\n  backup_size / 1024 / 1024 AS backup_size_mb,\\n  compressed_backup_size / 1024 / 1024 AS compressed_mb,\\n  has_backup_checksums,\\n  is_damaged\\nFROM msdb.dbo.backupset\\nWHERE backup_start_date > DATEADD(hour, -2, GETDATE())\\n  AND type = 'D'\\nORDER BY backup_start_date DESC", "parameters": {"expected_output": "The backup set on file 1 is valid (no errors)", "verify_user_dbs": "All user databases, or at minimum the largest and most critical ones", "verify_all_system_dbs": "master, msdb, model — these are critical for full instance recovery"}, "description": "After taking all backups, run RESTORE VERIFYONLY WITH CHECKSUM on each backup file to confirm it is not corrupt and can be used for recovery. A backup that cannot be verified with RESTORE VERIFYONLY is not a reliable rollback option. Confirm that is_damaged = 0 and has_backup_checksums = 1 in the backup history for all files."}	{"description": "If RESTORE VERIFYONLY reports corruption, the backup is unusable. Take a new backup immediately and verify again before proceeding with any patching activity"}	low	f	t	\N	2026-02-24 13:10:27.400857
3100	sqlserver	execute	Add secondary filegroup and data files on separate storage volumes	{"sql": "USE [master];\\n-- Add a secondary filegroup\\nALTER DATABASE [<db>] ADD FILEGROUP [FG_SECONDARY];\\n-- Add data file(s) to the new filegroup on a separate volume\\n-- Add multiple files for proportional fill across NVMe devices\\nALTER DATABASE [<db>]\\n  ADD FILE (\\n    NAME = N'<db>_data2',\\n    FILENAME = N'<new_volume>\\\\<db>_data2.ndf',\\n    SIZE = 1024 MB,\\n    MAXSIZE = UNLIMITED,\\n    FILEGROWTH = 512 MB\\n  ) TO FILEGROUP [FG_SECONDARY];\\n-- Verify\\nSELECT name, physical_name, size * 8 / 1024 AS size_mb,\\n  data_space_id\\nFROM sys.master_files\\nWHERE database_id = DB_ID('<db>');", "parameters": {"db": "Target database name", "new_volume": "Path to dedicated secondary storage volume (separate physical device from PRIMARY filegroup)"}, "description": "Creates a new filegroup on a separate physical storage volume. SQL Server's proportional fill algorithm will automatically balance writes across multiple filegroups when tables or indexes are moved to them, distributing checkpoint I/O."}	{"description": "-- Remove the new filegroup (only after moving all objects back to PRIMARY)\\nUSE [master];\\nALTER DATABASE [<db>] REMOVE FILE [<db>_data2];\\nALTER DATABASE [<db>] REMOVE FILEGROUP [FG_SECONDARY];"}	medium	t	t	00:10:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.508626
6031	postgresql	verify	Confirm replication lag is decreasing	{"sql": "SELECT client_addr, application_name, pg_size_pretty(pg_wal_lsn_diff(sent_lsn, replay_lsn)) AS replay_lag_bytes, replay_lag FROM pg_stat_replication;\\nSELECT pg_size_pretty(sum(size)) AS pg_wal_size FROM pg_ls_waldir();", "parameters": [], "description": "Verify that the replica is catching up and WAL retention on the primary is decreasing."}	\N	low	f	t	seconds	2026-02-25 08:59:43.77256
3091	sqlserver	instruct	Exclude SQL Server data and log directories from antivirus real-time scanning	{"sql": null, "parameters": {"directories_to_exclude": "SQL data directory, log directory, tempdb directory, SQL Server binaries", "file_extensions_to_exclude": ".mdf, .ndf, .ldf, .bak, .trn"}, "description": "If antivirus or endpoint protection is scanning SQL Server data files (.mdf, .ndf, .ldf) on write, it intercepts the write-through I/O path and effectively re-introduces OS-level buffering. Exclude the following from real-time scanning: (1) SQL Server data file directories (.mdf, .ndf), (2) Transaction log directories (.ldf), (3) TempDB directory, (4) SQL Server binary directory (MSSQL\\\\Binn\\\\), (5) SQL Server error log directory. Use your organization's endpoint protection management console to add these exclusions. Microsoft KB 309422 lists the full exclusion list."}	{"description": "Remove the antivirus exclusions via your endpoint protection console. Note: restoring AV scanning may reintroduce I/O latency."}	medium	t	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.505692
3078	sqlserver	instruct	Relocate transaction log file to a dedicated volume	{"sql": "-- Step 1: Set logical file path (replace <db>, <log_logical_name>, <new_path>)\\nALTER DATABASE [<db>] MODIFY FILE (\\n  NAME = N'<log_logical_name>',\\n  FILENAME = N'<new_dedicated_log_volume>\\\\<db>_log.ldf'\\n);\\n-- Step 2: Take the database offline\\nALTER DATABASE [<db>] SET OFFLINE WITH ROLLBACK IMMEDIATE;\\n-- Step 3: Physically move the .ldf file using OS tools, then bring online\\nALTER DATABASE [<db>] SET ONLINE;", "parameters": {"db": "Target database name", "log_logical_name": "Logical name of the log file (from sys.master_files type=1)", "new_dedicated_log_volume": "Path to the dedicated log volume (e.g., L:\\\\SQLLogs)"}, "description": "Move the transaction log to a volume dedicated exclusively to sequential log I/O (no data files, no OS/temp). Ideally a separate physical disk or LUN. This eliminates seek contention between checkpoint random writes to data pages and sequential log writes."}	{"description": "\\"ALTER DATABASE [<db>] MODIFY FILE (NAME = N'<log_logical_name>', FILENAME = N'<original_path>\\\\\\\\<db>_log.ldf');\\\\nALTER DATABASE [<db>] SET OFFLINE WITH ROLLBACK IMMEDIATE;\\\\n-- Move file back physically\\\\nALTER DATABASE [<db>] SET ONLINE;\\""}	medium	t	t	01:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.505703
3121	sqlserver	execute	Add data files to the PRIMARY filegroup	{"sql": "-- Add data files to increase checkpoint I/O parallelism.\\n-- Replace paths with actual storage paths; ideally spread across\\n-- different physical drives or LUN paths for maximum benefit.\\n-- Use the same initial size and growth settings as existing files.\\n\\n-- Example: add 3 files to bring total to 4 for a 10 GB database\\nUSE [YourDatabaseName];\\nGO\\n\\nALTER DATABASE [YourDatabaseName]\\n  ADD FILE (\\n    NAME = N'YourDatabaseName_data2',\\n    FILENAME = N'D:\\\\SQLData\\\\YourDatabaseName_data2.ndf',\\n    SIZE = 2048MB,\\n    MAXSIZE = UNLIMITED,\\n    FILEGROWTH = 512MB\\n  ) TO FILEGROUP [PRIMARY];\\n\\nALTER DATABASE [YourDatabaseName]\\n  ADD FILE (\\n    NAME = N'YourDatabaseName_data3',\\n    FILENAME = N'D:\\\\SQLData\\\\YourDatabaseName_data3.ndf',\\n    SIZE = 2048MB,\\n    MAXSIZE = UNLIMITED,\\n    FILEGROWTH = 512MB\\n  ) TO FILEGROUP [PRIMARY];\\n\\nALTER DATABASE [YourDatabaseName]\\n  ADD FILE (\\n    NAME = N'YourDatabaseName_data4',\\n    FILENAME = N'D:\\\\SQLData\\\\YourDatabaseName_data4.ndf',\\n    SIZE = 2048MB,\\n    MAXSIZE = UNLIMITED,\\n    FILEGROWTH = 512MB\\n  ) TO FILEGROUP [PRIMARY];\\n\\n-- Verify new files\\nSELECT name, physical_name, size * 8 / 1024 AS size_mb, growth\\nFROM sys.master_files\\nWHERE database_id = DB_ID('YourDatabaseName')\\n  AND type_desc = 'ROWS'\\nORDER BY file_id;", "parameters": {"growth_setting": "Match existing file autogrowth setting; prefer fixed MB over percentage", "file_size_guidance": "Set new files equal in size to existing file(s); this enables proportional fill to distribute pages evenly", "recommended_file_count": "min(logical_cpus/4, 8)"}, "description": "Add new data files of equal size to the PRIMARY filegroup. SQL Server uses proportional fill across all files in a filegroup, so equal file sizes ensure even data distribution and parallel checkpoint writes across all files."}	{"description": "-- Remove newly added files (only possible if empty; shrink first if needed)\\n-- DBCC SHRINKFILE (N'YourDatabaseName_data2', EMPTYFILE);\\n-- ALTER DATABASE [YourDatabaseName] REMOVE FILE [YourDatabaseName_data2];\\n-- Repeat for each added file"}	medium	t	t	01:00:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.510555
3127	sqlserver	execute	Add tempdb data files with equal size to existing files	{"sql": "-- Add tempdb files. All files MUST be the same size as the existing file.\\n-- The path should match the existing tempdb file directory.\\n-- This example assumes 1 existing file and 7 more are needed (8 total for 8+ CPUs).\\n-- Adjust file count and paths to match your environment.\\n\\nUSE [master];\\nGO\\n\\n-- Replace 8192 with the current size of tempdb_file_1 (in MB)\\n-- Replace path with actual tempdb directory\\nALTER DATABASE [tempdb]\\n  ADD FILE (NAME = N'tempdev2', FILENAME = N'C:\\\\SQLTempDB\\\\tempdev2.ndf',\\n            SIZE = 8192MB, MAXSIZE = UNLIMITED, FILEGROWTH = 512MB);\\n\\nALTER DATABASE [tempdb]\\n  ADD FILE (NAME = N'tempdev3', FILENAME = N'C:\\\\SQLTempDB\\\\tempdev3.ndf',\\n            SIZE = 8192MB, MAXSIZE = UNLIMITED, FILEGROWTH = 512MB);\\n\\nALTER DATABASE [tempdb]\\n  ADD FILE (NAME = N'tempdev4', FILENAME = N'C:\\\\SQLTempDB\\\\tempdev4.ndf',\\n            SIZE = 8192MB, MAXSIZE = UNLIMITED, FILEGROWTH = 512MB);\\n\\n-- Continue adding files until total count equals min(logical_cpus, 8).\\n-- Do NOT restart SQL Server — tempdb file changes are effective immediately.\\n\\n-- Verify\\nSELECT name, physical_name, size * 8 / 1024 AS size_mb\\nFROM sys.master_files\\nWHERE database_id = 2 AND type_desc = 'ROWS'\\nORDER BY file_id;", "parameters": {"path_guidance": "Prefer a dedicated drive for tempdb; if using same drive, performance gain comes from parallel allocation bitmaps not parallel I/O", "growth_setting": "Use fixed MB autogrowth equal to existing file's growth setting", "file_size_guidance": "Match the size of the first (existing) tempdb data file exactly"}, "description": "Add tempdb data files equal in size to the existing tempdb file. Equal file sizes are critical: SQL Server's proportional fill algorithm distributes new page allocations proportionally by size, so unequal files result in uneven distribution and persistent hotspots."}	{"description": "-- Tempdb files cannot be removed while SQL Server is running.\\n-- To remove: restart SQL Server, then at startup tempdb is re-created.\\n-- Remove extra file entries from startup parameters or use ALTER DATABASE REMOVE FILE\\n-- after shrinking to empty: DBCC SHRINKFILE (N'tempdev2', EMPTYFILE);\\n-- ALTER DATABASE [tempdb] REMOVE FILE [tempdev2];"}	low	t	t	00:30:00	2026-02-24 13:10:27.513102
5634	postgresql	instruct	Free or expand disk space on restore target	{"sql": null, "parameters": [], "description": "Ensure sufficient space: (1) Check available space: df -h /path/to/pgdata. (2) Required: at least 2x the backup size for restore + WAL replay. (3) Remove old data directories if this is a fresh restore target. (4) Expand the filesystem/volume if space is insufficient. (5) Consider using a separate tablespace on a different volume for large tables. (6) For cloud: resize the EBS/persistent disk volume."}	\N	medium	t	t	15-60 minutes	2026-02-25 08:59:43.77256
